y Dis 62 : 403–441, 2013 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 66. Meyrier A: Focal and segmental glomerulosclerosis: Multiple pathways are involved. Semin Nephrol 31: 326–332, 2011 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 67. D’Agati VD: Podocyte injury in focal segmental glomerulosclerosis: Lessons from animal models (a play in five acts). Kidney Int 73: 399–406, 2008 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 68. Chang JW, Pardo V, Sageshima J, Chen L, Tsai HL, Reiser J, Wei C, Ciancio G, Burke GW, 3rd, Fornoni A: Podocyte foot process effacement in postreperfusion allograft biopsies correlates with early recurrence of proteinuria in focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. Transplantation 93: 1238–1244, 2012 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 69. Rennke HG, Klein PS: Pathogenesis and significance of nonprimary focal and segmental glomerulosclerosis. Am J Kidney Dis 13 : 443–456, 1989 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 70. Thomas DB, Franceschini N, Hogan SL, Ten Holder S, Jennette CE, Falk RJ, Jennette JC: Clinical and pathologic characteristics of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis pathologic variants. Kidney Int 69: 920–926, 2006 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 71. Stokes MB, Valeri AM, Markowitz GS, D’Agati VD: Cellular focal segmental glomerulosclerosis: Clinical and pathologic features. Kidney Int 70: 1783–1792, 2006 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 72. Praga M, Borstein B, Andres A, Arenas J, Oliet A, Montoyo C, Ruilope LM, Rodicio JL: Nephrotic proteinuria without hypoalbuminemia: Clinical characteristics and response to angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibition. Am J Kidney Dis 17: 330–338, 1991 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 73. Praga M, Morales E, Herrero JC, Perez Campos A, Dominguez-Gil B, Alegre R, Vara J, Martinez MA: Absence of hypoalbuminemia despite massive proteinuria in focal segmental glomerulosclerosis secondary to hyperfiltration. Am J Kidney Dis 33 : 52–58, 1999 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 74. D’Agati VD, Kaskel FJ, Falk RJ: Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis. N Engl J Med 365: 2398–2411, 2011 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',136,'2025-10-15 06:03:50','2025-10-15 06:03:50');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3279,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10148329','Gaps in Nephrology Referral Care Utilization in Patients at High-Risk of Progression to Kidney Failure.','Peter S, Wang M, Chu CD, Tuot DS, Chattopadhyay A, Chen JH.','2023','slows tangri calculable calculable calculable calculable scatterplot loess scatterplot calculable calculable 60ml shc calculable outnumbered 60ml calculable fronts bikbov purcell nagler kinchen sadler bilous kisup dieberg ladhani titus cd007333 titus semnephrol tangri tangri ameta hingwala wojciechowski hiebert bhachu cockwell vallianou mitesh gkogkou geladari 1573403x14666180711124825 markanday reactants ofid ofv098 rockv hta14210 kurella hedgeman sfv103 waikar samal','Abstract Early nephrology specialty care slows progression of chronic kidney disease (CKD) to end-stage renal disease (ESRD). However, identifying which patients are expected to progress to end-stage disease has been historically challenging to predict. With a limited supply of nephrologists, optimizing nephrology referral is essential for improving patient outcomes. The Kidney Failure Risk Equation (KFRE) provides an accurate metric to identify patients who are at high risk of progression to kidney failure. In this study, we utilize the KFRE to perform a retrospective analysis in a local health network to identify rates of nephrology referral for CKD patients stratified by risk of kidney failure progression. We found a nephrology referral gap in CKD patients at higher risk of progression and an underutilization of albuminuria testing in CKD, suggesting opportunities to improve outcomes by 1) proactively targeting high-risk patients using EHR-based informatics strategies and 2) increasing albuminuria testing as a screening tool. Introduction The burden of chronic kidney disease (CKD) is substantial, with a global prevalence of 9.1% and 4.6% of global deaths being attributed to CKD in 2017. 1 , 2 CKD increases the risk of cardiovascular disease events and progression to end-stage renal disease (ESRD), ultimately leading to increased morbidity and mortality. 1 , 2 Early interventions guided by nephrology specialty care may slow the progression of disease in CKD patients, while late evaluation by a nephrologist is associated with shorter duration of survival. 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 Individuals with CKD have significant heterogeneity in the risk of progression to ESRD. With the known workforce shortage of nephrologists in the United States and worldwide, a key challenge is to optimize referrals to nephrology to assure specialty care access to those at highest risk of ESRD. 7 The Kidney Failure Risk Equation (KFRE) is a prediction model developed by Tangri et al that may enable early, appropriate patient care for patients with CKD and high risk of progression to kidney failure. 8 The KFRE uses demographic and laboratory data to accurately predict the risk of kidney failure. The risk equations have been internationally validated across diverse patient cohorts, demonstrating excellent discrimination in predicting which CKD patients progress to kidney failure. 8 , 9 When applied in a risk-based triage strategy in Canada and the United Kingdom, the KFRE model has been shown to minimize potentially unnecessary nephrology care for those at low risk of CKD progression and prevent delays in specialty care access for those at highest risk of kidney failure. 10 , 11 In the U.K., the use of KFRE to guide referral strategies to nephrology specialty care compared to previously established guidelines would have resulted in changes in nephrology referral for 14.8% of individuals with chronic kidney disease, including 8.8% of patients at high risk of ESRD who would not have been referred based on prior guidelines. 11 Given substantial differences in the way healthcare is delivered in Canada, the United Kingdom and the United States, it is not clear whether these results would apply in the United States. To date, very little has been published about the complexity of patients seen in U.S. nephrology clinics or their risk for CKD progression. 12 , 13 With this study, we aimed to (1) evaluate rates of nephrology referral in a representative regional health network across kidney failure risk levels, and (2) evaluate albuminuria ordering behavior in patients with chronic kidney disease. Results could inform future strategies to optimize the delivery of scarce nephrology care and improve clinical outcomes for patients with high risk of ESRD. Methods We conducted a retrospective cohort study that examined kidney failure risk among individuals receiving ambulatory care within one regional network in California. The STAnford Research Repository (STARR) electronic health record database includes all clinical data at Stanford Health Care, including electronic health record data from its main hospitals as well as data from various clinical ancillary systems. For our study, we limited our cohort to patients with demonstrated kidney injury that we defined as an estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) value that would classify as Stage IIIa CKD or above (eGFR<60 ml/min/1.73m 2 ) since this is the eGFR range used in the validation studies for the KFRE. 8 , 9 We identified all patient encounters between 2015 and 2020 in which an eGFR<60 ml/min/1.73m 2 and UACR were obtained, allowing for up to 90 days separation between the patient’s lab results because the labs may not have been obtained simultaneously. These were considered “calculable KFRE encounters”, which were encounters during which calculation of kidney failure risk could have been available based on recent laboratory data. Some patients had multiple calculable KFRE encounters during our time window based on our criteria. We decided to include each of these encounters in our analysis because we were interested in determining how often patients received specialty nephrology care within 1 year of a possible risk stratification timepoint, regardless of whether the time of possible risk stratification was a new diagnosis of high-risk kidney disease. This is because patients with CKD who are determined to be at high risk of progression to kidney failure can benefit from timely nephrologist care even if their kidney disease is not newly diagnosed. Additionally, we excluded patients with a history of ESRD (either receiving dialysis or having a history of kidney transplant), identified using International Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision (ICD-10) codes. Because our goal was to examine nephrology follow-up care within 1 year following potential risk stratification for patients who were not at terminal stages of disease, we also excluded patients who were not alive at least one year following their encounter date. The independent variable was 5-year risk of progression to kidney failure calculated by the 4-variable Kidney Failure Risk Equation. 8 Risk predictions were calculated for all patients using age, sex, estimated glomerular filtration rate, and urine albumin to creatinine ratio. 7 , 8 The outcome was receipt of nephrology care within 1 year following potential risk stratification. Patients were categorized as having received follow-up nephrology care in this interval if they had at least one nephrology office visit at Stanford Health Care within 1 year of their initial kidney failure risk calculation. We stratified patient encounters into 5-year kidney failure risk levels from 0% to 100% in increments of 2% and identified the proportion of calculable KFRE patient encounters that received nephrology specialty care within +/- 1 year of KFRE calculation. To evaluate albuminuria ordering behavior, we determined the total number of Stanford Health Care patients with eGFR values that would categorize them as CKD III or above (eGFR<60 ml/min/1.73m 2 ). Among these patients, we then determined how many received albuminuria testing +/-90 days from their abnormal eGFR lab result. The study was approved by the Stanford University Institutional Review Board. The analysis was conducted in R, version 4.0.1 (R Foundation). Results: Between 2015-2020, there were a total of 1,507,891 distinct patients in the hospital system. We identified 11,877 calculable ambulatory KFRE encounters representing a cohort of 6,444 patients. Mean age was 72.8 years old, and approximately 51% were male, 51% were White, 19% were Asian, 12% were Black, 8% were Hispanic/Latino, 1% were Pacific Islander, and 0.4% were Native American. 42% of patients were privately insured, while 55% were on Medicare and 3% on Medicaid ( Table 1 ). The most common encounter diagnoses for patients in our cohort were Type II diabetes, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, chronic kidney disease, and routine exam. Table 1. Patient Cohort Characteristics and Demographic Information Open in a new tab Nephrology referral rate by kidney failure risk 5-year risk of kidney failure was calculated for all patient encounters that met inclusion criteria. For each kidney failure risk level, the proportion of corresponding patient encounters that received follow-up nephrology care within 1 year was determined and graphically recorded in Figure 1 . Local estimated scatterplot smoothing (LOESS) was used for the regression line plotted within the scatterplot to allow for easy referencing of kidney failure risk level to estimated corresponding nephrology referral rate. A histogram outlining the proportion of total encounters in the cohort for each 5% kidney failure risk interval was also included in Figure 1 . Figure 1. Open in a new tab Nephrology Referral Rate for Calculable KFRE Encounters across Kidney Failure Risk % of the patient encounters in the analysis (8,223 out of 11,877) had a 5-year risk of kidney failure of less than 5%. 10.2% of these patient encounters received nephrology care within 1 year. Receipt of nephrology care increased with increasing kidney failure risk but appeared to level off above a 40% 5-year kidney failure risk level. Only 54% of patients with a calculable KFRE encounter indicating greater than a 40% risk of ESRD in 5 years received a nephrology visit within one year (216 out of 403). Evaluating Albuminuria Test Ordering Behavior Among the 859,877 total patients at Stanford Health Care between 2015-2020, 56,910 had at least one eGFR lab result that would be classified at or above the Stage III CKD category of eGFR<60mL/min/1.73m 2 . For these patients, 6,855 received an albuminuria test within 90 days of their abnormal eGFR result ( Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Flowchart Summarizing Albuminuria (uACR) Ordering Behavior between 2015-2020 at SHC Discussion A risk of 3% over 5 years for kidney failure, as determined by the KFRE, has been used in the past as a threshold to triage nephrology care. 10 In our study, while the majority of patients in our study population who receive both a urine albumin to creatinine ratio and eGFR result have a 5-year risk of progression to kidney failure of less than 3% (59% of cohort; 6,900 out of 11,689 patient encounters), a substantial fraction of the patients greater than a 3% risk of kidney failure in 5 years are not receiving timely nephrology specialty care – the patients from 64% of patient encounters at 10% kidney failure risk level (420 out of 652 patient encounters), 52% of patient encounters at 20% kidney failure risk level (110 out of 212 patient encounters), and 40% of patient encounters at 50% kidney failure risk level (20 out of 50 patient encounters) did not receive follow-up nephrology care within 1 year of a calculable KFRE encounter. Early intervention for these higher risk patients could help slow CKD progression and ultimately improve patient outcomes. Prior studies have shown that early referral to nephrology specialty care in high-risk patients is associated with better outcomes. 3 , 4 , 6 , 14 , 15 , 16 Despite the demonstrated improvement in clinical outcomes from early specialty care referral, many high-risk patients often do not receive timely nephrology specialty care. 17 , 18 In an effort to avoid late referral to specialty care services, The Kidney Disease Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) organization has outlined specific guidelines for nephrology referral in CKD patients in an effort to capture the heterogenous risk factors (ranging from eGFR cutoffs to history of recurrent nephrolithiasis) that lead to an increased likelihood of progression to kidney failure. 4 However in a study aimed at analyzing the projected impact of implementing the KDIGO referral recommendations at a well-resourced tertiary care center, the projected increase in nephrology referrals based on KDIGO guidelines far outnumbered the institution’s supply of nephrology specialty care. 19 Such a supply-demand mismatch in a well-resourced medical center suggests that strict adherence to KDIGO guidelines may not be feasible in other, lower-resourced institutions as well. 19 While recommending guidelines to standardize early specialty care referral for high risk patients is a step in the right direction, alternative strategies to prevent late referral for high risk CKD patients must be considered. 19 As shown in our study, there appears to be an underutilization of nephrology specialty care for patients at highest risk of kidney failure, likely secondary to the heterogeneity in disease burden across CKD patients which makes clinical prognostication challenging for primary care providers. The KFRE is a widely validated metric for evaluating kidney failure risk that can be utilized with an institution’s EHR data to target a nephrology referral gap in patients who are at high risk of progression to kidney failure. Informatics strategies such as developing digital tools embedded in an EHR to automate triage of patients using KFRE-based risk stratification can help streamline the referral process for these high-risk CKD patients. Once an automated risk stratification is made, population management strategies such as e-referrals and outreach programs can be deployed to proactively target the 10-15% of patients who have a 5-year risk of kidney failure greater than a certain threshold, such as 3%. Additionally, a data-driven triage system also serves to prevent unnecessary referrals for patients at very low risk of progression to kidney failure, ultimately enabling more nephrologists to be available for those high-risk patients who need timely specialty care the most. However, the limiting factor in such a population risk stratification strategy is the albuminuria testing. As shown in Figure 2 , there is a marked underutilization of albuminuria testing for patients with signs of kidney injury. In our study population, only 12% of patients with an eGFR <60ml/min/1.73m 2 received an albuminuria test result within +/-90 days of their abnormal eGFR result (6,855 out of 56,910 patients). For the 88% of patients who had a decreased eGFR with no follow-up albuminuria test, the prognostic value of KFRE cannot be appreciated and therefore these patients may be excluded from any KFRE-based risk stratification strategy. To effectively narrow the nephrology referral gap using risk prediction tools like the KFRE, the first step is to test for albuminuria more broadly in patients with demonstrated kidney disease. Doing so increases the total number of calculable KFRE predictions and enables more effective population risk stratification and better nephrology outreach based on individualized patient risk. Prevention of CKD complications requires a concerted effort on multiple fronts. Increased albuminuria testing to enable KFRE-based triage combined with additional interventions such as promoting self-efficacy of primary care providers as well as specialty e-consults for risk-based outreach may provide a means to optimize efficient use of nephrology specialty care and ultimately improve outcomes for CKD patients. Limitations: The analysis in this cohort is not within a closed system – patients could have received nephrology care outside of the Stanford health care network and therefore their follow-up nephrology care would not be captured, leading to an overestimation of the referral gap. As risk of kidney failure increases, the number of patients in a given kidney failure risk level drops significantly, with roughly 70% of the entire study population having a 5-year risk of kidney failure less than 5%. This decrease in sample size decreases the statistical power of the nephrology referral gap evaluation in higher kidney failure risk levels. The Stanford Health Care patient cohort is limited to one regional health network, therefore is not necessarily representative of referral gaps on a larger scale. However, the findings are consistent with prior studies indicating inefficient use of nephrology specialty referral. 17 , 18 The methodology used here to quantitatively evaluate the potential nephrology referral gap using EHR data can be applied broadly to target quality improvement projects and population risk stratification strategies aimed at improving nephrology care for CKD patients in other regional health networks. Conclusion In our study, when urine albumin screening was used at a local level in the United States, despite being predictive of high risk for renal failure in CKD patients using the Kidney Failure Risk Equation, there was often a missed opportunity in care delivery. We found modest nephrology referral rates in patients at higher risk of progression to kidney failure who would benefit from early nephrology specialty consultation and management, indicating an inefficient use of healthcare resources and a need for a more targeted approach to proactively provide care for higher risk patients. The KFRE provides an excellent tool to identify such patients and can possibly be utilized as an HER- embedded triage tool to optimize risk-stratification for patients with CKD and guide appropriate, efficacious specialty referral care. However, we found that urine albumin screening was markedly underutilized in patients with demonstrated kidney injury. To effectively risk-stratify patient populations based on risk of kidney failure, increased evaluation of albuminuria is needed in patients with chronic kidney disease to enable optimized nephrology specialty care and better patient outcomes for those at higher risk. Figures & Table References 1. Bikbov B, Purcell CA, Levey AS, et al. Global, regional, and national burden of chronic kidney disease, 1990-2017: a systematic analysis for the Global Burden of Disease Study 2017. Lancet. 2020. doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(20)30045-3. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 2. Webster AC, Nagler E V., Morton RL, Masson P. Chronic Kidney Disease. Lancet. 2017. doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(16)32064-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 3. Kinchen KS, Sadler J, Fink N, et al. The timing of specialist evaluation in chronic kidney disease and mortality. Ann Intern Med. 2002. doi:10.7326/0003-4819-137-6-200209170-00007. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 4. Levin A, Stevens PE, Bilous RW, et al. Kidney disease: Improving global outcomes (KDIGO) CKD work group. KDIGO 2012 clinical practice guideline for the evaluation and management of chronic kidney disease. Kidney Int Suppl. 2013. doi:10.1038/kisup.2012.73. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 5. Smart NA, Dieberg G, Ladhani M, Titus T. Early referral to specialist nephrology services for preventing the progression to end-stage kidney disease. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2014. doi:10.1002/14651858.CD007333.pub2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 6. Smart NA, Titus TT. Outcomes of early versus late nephrology referral in chronic kidney disease: A systematic review. Am J Med. 2011. doi:10.1016/j.amjmed.2011.04.026. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 7. Kohan DE, Rosenberg ME. The Chronic Kidney Disease Epidemic: A Challenge for Nephrology Training Programs. Semin Nephrol. 2009. doi:10.1016/j.semnephrol.2009.06.011. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 8. Tangri N, Stevens LA, Griffith J, et al. A predictive model for progression of chronic kidney disease to kidney failure. JAMA - J Am Med Assoc. 2011. doi:10.1001/jama.2011.451. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 9. Tangri N, Grams ME, Levey AS, et al. Multinational assessment of accuracy of equations for predicting risk of kidney failure ameta-analysis. JAMA - J Am Med Assoc. 2016. doi:10.1001/jama.2015.18202. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 10. Hingwala J, Wojciechowski P, Hiebert B, et al. Risk-based triage for nephrology referrals using the kidney failure risk equation. Can J Kidney Heal Dis. 2017. doi:10.1177/2054358117722782. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 11. Bhachu HK, Cockwell P, Subramanian A, et al. Impact of Using Risk-Based Stratification on Referral of Patients With Chronic Kidney Disease From Primary Care to Specialist Care in the United Kingdom. Kidney Int Reports. 2021. doi:10.1016/j.ekir.2021.05.031. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 12. Tsai W-C, Wu H-Y, Peng Y-S, et al. Risk Factors for Development and Progression of Chronic Kidney Disease. Medicine (Baltimore) 2016. doi:10.1097/md.0000000000003013. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 13. Vallianou NG, Mitesh S, Gkogkou A, Geladari E. Chronic Kidney Disease and Cardiovascular Disease: Is there Any Relationship? Curr Cardiol Rev. 2018. doi:10.2174/1573403x14666180711124825. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 14. Chan MR, Dall AT, Fletcher KE, Lu N, Trivedi H. Outcomes in Patients with Chronic Kidney Disease Referred Late to Nephrologists: A Meta-analysis. Am J Med. 2007. doi:10.1016/j.amjmed.2007.04.024. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 15. Markanday A. Acute Phase Reactants in Infections: Evidence-Based Review and a Guide for Clinicians. Open Forum Infect Dis. 2015. doi:10.1093/ofid/ofv098. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 16. Black C, Sharma P, Scotland G, et al. Early referral strategies for management of people with markers of renal disease: A systematic review of the evidence of clinical effectiveness, cost-effectiveness and economic analysis. Health Technol Assess (Rockv) 2010. doi:10.3310/hta14210. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 17. Fung E, Chang TI, Chertow GM, Thomas IC, Asch SM, Kurella Tamura M. Receipt of Nephrology Care and Clinical Outcomes Among Veterans With Advanced CKD. Am J Kidney Dis. 2017. doi:10.1053/j.ajkd.2017.06.025. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 18. Gillespie BW, Morgenstern H, Hedgeman E, et al. Nephrology care prior to end-stage renal disease and outcomes among new ESRD patients in the USA. Clin Kidney J. 2015. doi:10.1093/ckj/sfv103. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 19. Singh K, Waikar SS, Samal L. Evaluating the feasibility of the KDIGO CKD referral recommendations. BMC Nephrol. 2017. doi:10.1186/s12882-017-0646-y. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ]',2819,'2025-10-15 06:04:03','2025-10-15 06:04:03');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3280,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9269575','Provider Perspectives and Clinical Outcomes with Inpatient Telenephrology.','Androga LA, Zoghby Z, Ramar P, Amundson RH, d''Uscio M, Philpot LM, Thorsteinsdottir B, Kattah AG, Albright RC Jr.','2022','telenephrologists telenephrologists telenephrologists telenephrologists 2022_04_11_cjn13441021 mp3 mchs telenephrologists mchs mankato crosse mchs multistate mchs mankato mchs crosse telenephrologist telenephrologists teladoc teladoc mchs crosse mankato mchs eau mchs eau eau mchs eau mchs elixhauser overdispersion arrr mchs crosse mankato eau mankato crosse eau mankato crosse mankato crosse eau elixhauser mchs mankato crosse mchs eau elixhauser arrr arrr mankato crosse crosse mankato mankato crosse eau arrr mchs mankato crosse mchs eau elixhauser arrrs crosse mankato mankato crosse eau mchs mankato crosse mchs eau elixhauser arrr subspecialities telenephrologist telenephrologist mchs mankato crosse mchs eau noninferior telenephrologists telenephrologists telenephrologists nonphysicians sanderling strove amundson mchs mankato telenephrologists kattah thorsteinsdottir owning aurora blackberry crisps motors kyndryl hldg nokia biotechnological xpev xpeng androga philpot ramar amundson uscio zoghby kadmon 13441021supplementarydata1 teammates amundson androga kattah philpot thorsteinsdottir zoghby androga androga androga amundson androga uscio amundson uscio kattah philpot thorsteinsdottir zoghby androga androga thorsteinsdottir androga kattah philpot kattah thorsteinsdottir zoghby androga kattah philpot androga amundson androga uscio kattah philpot zoghby asnjournals dcsupplemental mchs amundson healthypeople healthypeople douthit kiv dwolatzky bullard pavkov neugarten bellin yunes abramowitz mokrzycki coco prudhvi golestaneh pelayo bhargav gul dejoy salacup albano gopalakrishnan azmaiparashvili patarroyo aponte rangaswami ramnath maggio khazeni ensell trifecta trifecta 20inpatient 20dialysis 20is 20the 20telemedicine 20trifecta 20improving 20provide 20dialysis 20patients 20who 20were 20hospitalized akbik rabinov rost schwamm mazwi neurointerv nallamothu sasson cram tannenbaum koonin farris antall zelis stevermer 1357633x20963893 13441021supplementarydata1','Visual Abstract Open in a new tab Keywords: telenephrology, telehealth, inpatient nephrology, outcomes, patient perspectives, prospective studies Abstract Background and objectives Despite the dramatic increase in the provision of virtual nephrology care, only anecdotal reports of outcomes without comparators to usual care exist in the literature. This study aimed to provide objective determination of clinical noninferiority of hybrid (telenephrology plus face-to-face) versus standard (face-to-face) inpatient nephrology care. Design, setting, participants, & measurements This retrospective study compares objective outcomes in patients who received inpatient hybrid care versus standard nephrology care at two Mayo Clinic Health System community hospitals. Outcomes were then additionally compared with those patients receiving care at another Mayo Clinic Health System site where only standard care is available. Hospitalized adults who had nephrology consults from March 1, 2020 to February 28, 2021 were considered. Regression was used to assess 30-day mortality, length of hospitalization, readmissions, odds of being prescribed dialysis, and hospital transfers. Sensitivity analysis was performed using patients who had ≥50% of their care encounters via telenephrology. Structured surveys were used to understand the perspectives of non-nephrology hospital providers and telenephrologists. Results In total, 850 patients were included. Measured outcomes that included the number of hospital transfers (odds ratio, 1.19; 95% confidence interval, 0.37 to 3.82) and 30-day readmissions (odds ratio, 0.97; 95% confidence interval, 0.84 to 1.06), among others, did not differ significantly between controls and patients in the general cohort. Telenephrologists ( n =11) preferred video consults (82%) to phone for communication. More than half (64%) of telenephrologists spent less time on telenephrology compared with standard care. Non-nephrology hospital providers ( n =21) were very satisfied (48%) and satisfied (29%) with telenephrology response time and felt telenephrology was as safe as standard care (67%), while providing them enough information to make patient care decisions (76%). Conclusions Outcomes for in-hospital nephrology consults were not significantly different comparing hybrid care versus standard care. Non-nephrology hospital providers and telenephrologists had favorable opinions of telenephrology and most perceived it is as safe and effective as standard care. Podcast This article contains a podcast at https://www.asn-online.org/media/podcast/CJASN/2022_04_11_CJN13441021.mp3 Introduction In total, 19% of the US population live in rural areas, where they face scarcity of health care facilities, provider shortages, insufficient public transport, and financial challenges, among other social determinants of health constraints ( 1 , 2 ). Among other issues, health care delivery factors have led to suboptimal outcomes among rural patients compared with some of their urban counterparts, and highlight disparities in our health care system ( 3 ). Simultaneously, the incidence of AKI among patients who are hospitalized has continued to be substantial ( 4 – 6 ), while health centers and community hospitals struggle to recruit and retain provider specialists such as nephrologists ( 7 , 8 ). Telenephrology has been proposed as a modality of health care delivery that could alleviate some health care system deficiencies by expanding access to value-based, specialized kidney care. Tele–intensive care unit (ICU) and tele-stroke implementation have shown objective positive health outcomes ( 9 , 10 ). The coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic prompted a Medicare-supported expansion of telehealth services, augmenting the already available forms of virtual care for outpatient dialysis. Currently, comparative experience determining effectiveness between traditional face-to-face and virtual/telenephrology with respect to objective clinical outcomes and provider perspectives on inpatient synchronous telenephrology care has been unexplored. The goal of the study was to determine whether outcomes of patients receiving hybrid nephrology care (telenephrology and face to face) differed when compared with those receiving standard care (face to face only), and hence whether this modality is safe and effective. Materials and Methods The study was approved by the Mayo Clinic Institutional Review Board. In this study of prospectively determined objective outcomes, we present data comparing health outcomes in patients seen via hybrid care versus standard nephrology care at two Mayo Clinic Health System (MCHS) community hospitals. We also utilized standardized structured survey questionnaires to understand the perspectives of the hospital-based non-nephrology providers and telenephrologists on this new modality of nephrology care delivery in hospitals. Participants, Clinic Model, and Setting MCHS community hospitals in Mankato, Minnesota, and La Crosse, Wisconsin, were the nephrology spoke sites, while Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota (MCR), a tertiary care center, was the hub site. MCHS is an integrated multistate, multispecialty health system sharing a common electronic health system encompassing 16 hospitals and caring for a midwest population base of >250,000 patients ( Supplemental Figure 1 ). MCHS Mankato is a 112-bed hospital while MCHS La Crosse is a 121-bed hospital, and each has 14 ICU-capable beds. The two spoke sites have five nephrologists and two advance-practice providers (APPs) combined, providing inpatient nephrology care. However, secondary to nephrologist staffing challenges, they each had just two nephrologists and a total of two APPs available during the study period. One telenephrologist and one APP located at the hub site (MCR) provided care for 7 consecutive days per month for each of the spoke sites, accepting telenephrology consults from the emergency departments, medical, surgical, and the ICU. The periods during which telenephrology was offered was determined 6 months in advance, without knowledge of which patients would require nephrology consults, and no face-to-face consults were offered during these times. The telenephrologists provided direct patient care using Teladoc Health Inc. (Harrison, NY), a virtual care platform that allowed real-time stethoscope functionality. Nurses at the hub site set up and managed the virtual platforms. Communication with non-nephrology providers before or after the telenephrology consults was either via phone or video (Teladoc). The telenephrology process is described in Figure 1 . Telenephrology consults were received via the Epic System (Verona, WI). Given the integrated system and depending on the length of stay, patients may have received both models of care at the local sites during the hospitalization encounter; in such cases, the telenephrology team and face-to-face teams transitioned care back and forth using standard sign out. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Inpatient telenephrology workflow process. EHR, electronic health record. We compared patient outcomes from strictly on-site standard nephrology care at MCHS La Crosse and Mankato to the MCR-derived hybrid care. Additionally, we compared the telenephrology care model outcomes and experiences to those of patients who received care at MCHS in Eau Claire, Wisconsin, a larger community hospital with 304 beds that provided only traditional nephrology care in a comparable community-based hospital setting. Finally, for sensitivity analyses, outcomes of patients who received >50% of their care via telenephrology were analyzed. Patients aged ≥18 years who had inpatient nephrology consults from March 1, 2020 to February 28, 2021 with primary diagnoses grouped into AKI, acid-base imbalance, chronic heart failure/cardiovascular disease, kidney failure, kidney/pancreas transplant, and fluid/electrolytes/anemia disorders, as determined by the primary billing diagnosis code ( Supplemental Table 1 ) were included in the study. MCHS Eau Claire, unlike the spoke sites, has cardiothoracic and neurosurgery hospital services. To enhance cohort comparability, patients with nephrology consultations in the setting of cardiovascular or neurologic surgery were excluded from the Eau Claire cohort. MCHS Eau Claire has four nephrologists who provide only standard nephrology consult services. Outcomes Patient Outcome Data. Outcomes of interest were the number of hospital transfers from MCHS to MCR (spoke to hub), length of hospital stay (days), readmission within 30 days of discharge, death within 30 days of the initial nephrology consult, and number of inpatient hemodialysis or continuous venovenous hemofiltration sessions. Patient demographics including age, sex, Elixhauser Comorbidity Index scores, and race were obtained from the electronic health record and summarized using descriptive statistics. Logistic regression was used to assess association of the intervention with 30-day mortality, readmissions, and hospital transfers. Firth’s method was applied to minimize bias in the case of rare events. Distribution of the length of hospital stay was assessed, and a negative binomial regression model was fit to account for overdispersion in the data. Adjusted relative rate ratios (aRRR) and associated P values were calculated. In consideration of potential site-level differences, secondary analysis comparing hybrid care to standard care (controls) at MCHS La Crosse, Mankato, and Eau Claire was conducted, limiting comparisons to the primary intervention sites. All data management and statistical analyses were performed using Statistical Analysis Software (SAS) Version 9.4 (Cary, NC). Two patients in the standard care group at the telenephrology providing sites missing date of death information were excluded from the outcome of death within 30 days. There was no loss to follow-up. Provider Satisfaction Data. Standardized structured surveys ( Supplemental Figures 2 and 3 ) were developed to assess the perspectives of telenephrology providers at the hub site and those of the providers requesting nephrology consults at the spoke sites. The telenephrology providers surveyed were physicians and APPs, whereas the inpatient non-nephrology hospital providers were physicians, APPs, and any other hospital provider who was involved in the care of the patients included in the study. Surveys were electronically deployed hospital-wide in February 2021 and providers were given 8 weeks to respond. Data were captured using the Research Electronic Data Capture system, a secure, web-based application designed to support data capture for research studies. The spoke site survey assessed hospital providers’ views on the effect of telenephrology with respect to ( 1 ) improving service efficiency of medical staff, ( 2 ) bridging gaps in care delivery, ( 3 ) changes to medical management of patients, ( 4 ) avoidance of need for urgent dialysis, ( 5 ) reducing patient morbidity, and ( 6 ) reducing transfers to intensive care units. Responses were graded on a five-point Likert scale that ranged from 1, not at all influential, to 5, very influential. Similarly, a survey was used to capture information on the hub site telenephrology providers’ views with regard to ( 1 ) safety of giving recommendations via telenephrology, ( 2 ) feeling they had sufficient information to provide a telenephrology consult, ( 3 ) ability to add value to patient care, and ( 4 ) efficiency of the telenephrology consult service. Responses were graded on a five-point Likert scale ranging from 1, never, to 5, always. Unstructured comments on the service from both hub and spoke providers were also collected. Responses for both surveys are summarized as counts and proportions. Results Participant Characteristics A total of 850 patients from Mankato, La Crosse, and Eau Claire were included in the study; 194 patients had telenephrology consults at Mankato or La Crosse (hybrid care), 235 patients received only standard nephrology consults at Mankato or La Crosse, and 421 at Eau Claire received only standard care ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Characteristics of patients who received hybrid or only standard nephrology care in the Mayo Clinic Health System Intervention Group ( n =194) Intervention Group Subset with ≥50% Telenephrology ( n =87 of 194) Controls Telenephrology Sites ( n =235) Controls Alternate Site ( n =421) Total All Sites ( n =850) Age in years, mean (SD) 68 (14) 67 (16) 69 (15) 69 (14) 69 (15) Sex, n (%) Male 115 (59) 49 (56) 134 (57) 243 (58) 492 (58) Female 79 (41) 38 (44) 101 (43) 178 (42) 358 (42) Race, n (%) Other 15 (8) 6 (7) 16 (7) 26 (6) 57 (7) White 179 (92) 81 (93) 219 (93) 395 (94) 793 (93) Elixhauser Comorbidity Index Score, mean (SD) 9.5 (3.5) 8.9 (3.5) 9.4 (3.6) 9.0 (3.5) 9.2 (3.5) Diagnosis group, n (%) AKI 116 (60) 50 (57) 132 (56) 224 (53) 472 (55) Acid-base imbalance 3 (1) 1 (1) 7 (3) 7 (2) 17 (2) Chronic heart failure/ cardiovascular disease 5 (3) 2 (2) 9 (4) 3 (0.7) 17 (2) CKD 15 (8) 6 (7) 13 (6) 39 (9) 67 (8) Kidney failure 34 (17) 16 (18) 30 (13) 78 (18) 142 (17) Transplant 4 (2) 0 (0) 1 (0.4) 17 (4) 22 (3) Fluid/electrolytes/anemia 17 (9) 12 (15) 43 (18) 53 (13) 113 (13) Open in a new tab Intervention group: received telenephrology plus face-to-face care (hybrid care). Controls: received only face-to-face care (standard care). Telenephrology sites: sites that offer hybrid care (Mayo Clinic Health System [MCHS] Mankato and La Crosse). Alternate site: offers only standard care (MCHS Eau Claire). Outcomes There were no significant differences between the hybrid and the standard care groups in the measured outcomes of number of hospital transfers, length of stay, 30-day readmission, death within 30 days of initial consult, or number of dialysis procedures. The same results held when the odds ratio was adjusted for age, sex, race, diagnoses group, and Elixhauser Comorbidity Index Score ( Table 2 ). Interestingly, the sensitivity analysis using a subset of intervention group patients who had 50% or more of their inpatient nephrology encounters by telenephrology were significantly less likely to experience a longer length of stay compared with patients who had standard encounters (aRRR, 0.78; 95% confidence interval, 0.66 to 0.93; P value 0.005). However, the statistical significance does not persist (aRRR, 0.89; 95% confidence interval, 0.75 to 1.05; P value 0.17) when considering only the sites that offer hybrid care (Mankato, La Crosse) ( Table 3 ). Table 2. Outcomes for hospitalized patients who received hybrid care compared with those who received standard nephrology care Outcome Outcomes of Hybrid Care Compared with Standard Care at Telenephrology-Providing Sites (La Crosse and Mankato) Outcomes of Hybrid Care at Telenephrology Sites Compared with Standard Care at All Sites (Mankato, La Crosse, Eau Claire) n (%) or Mean (SD) Adjusted Odds Ratio a (95% Confidence Interval) P Value n (%) or Mean (SD) Adjusted Odds Ratio a (95% Confidence Interval) P Value Intervention Group ( n =194) Control Group ( n =235) Intervention Group ( n =194) Control Group ( n =421) Hospital transfer 4 (2%) 6 (3%) 1.10 (0.28 to 4.15) 0.92 4 (2%) 6 (1%) 1.19 (0.37 to 3.82) 0.77 Length of stay, d (aRRR, 95% CI) 7.8 (6) 7.3 (6) 1.08 (0.95 to 1.23) 0.25 7.8 (6) 8.9 (12) 0.94 (0.84 to 1.06) 0.34 30-Day readmission 29 (15%) 35 (15%) 0.97 (0.56 to 1.69) 0.93 29 (15%) 63 (15%) 0.97 (0.61 to 1.52) 0.88 Death within 30 days 34 (18%) 46 (20%) 0.87 (0.52 to 1.46) 0.60 34 (18%) 75 (18%) 0.90 (0.58 to 1.40) 0.63 Dialysis 64 (33%) 52 (22%) 1.80 (1.00 to 3.22) 0.05 64 (33%) 135 (32%) 1.24 (0.79 to 1.94) 0.36 Inpatient hemodialysis 59 (30%) 50 (21%) 59 (30%) 116 (28%) Continuous venovenous hemofiltration 15 (8%) 15 (6%) 15 (8%) 6 (1%) Open in a new tab Intervention group: received telenephrology plus face-to-face care (hybrid care). Control group: received only face-to-face care (standard care). Telenephrology sites: sites that offer hybrid care (Mayo Clinic Health System [MCHS] Mankato and La Crosse). Alternate site: offers only standard care (MCHS Eau Claire). 95% CI, 95% confidence interval. a Adjusted for age, Elixhauser, sex, race, diagnosis; adjusted relative rate ratios (aRRRs) reported for length of stay. Table 3. Outcomes for patients who were hospitalized who received hybrid care compared with those who received standard nephrology consults: Subset of patients with ≥ 50% visits being telenephrology compared with patients who were face-to-face at hybrid care sites Outcome Outcomes of Hybrid Care Compared with Standard Care at Telenephrology-Providing Sites (La Crosse and Mankato) Outcomes of Hybrid Care at Telenephrology Sites Compared with Standard Care at All Sites (Mankato, La Crosse, Eau Claire) n (%) or Mean (SD) Adjusted Odds Ratio a (95% Confidence Interval) P Value n (%) or Mean (SD) Adjusted Odds Ratio a (95% Confidence Interval) P Value Intervention Group ( n =87 of 194) Control Group ( n =235) Intervention Group ( n =87 of 194) Control Group ( n =421) Hospital transfer 2 (2%) 6 (3%) 1.20 (0.22 to 6.60) 0.84 2 (2%) 6 (1%) 1.19 (0.37 to 3.82) 0.77 Length of stay, d 6.4 (4) 7.3 (6) 0.89 (0.75 to 1.05) 0.17 6.4 (4) 8.9 (12) 0.78 (0.66 to 0.93) 0.005 30-Day readmission 17 (20%) 35 (15%) 1.42 (0.74 to 2.75) 0.29 17 (20%) 63 (15%) 1.04 (0.56 to 1.94) 0.88 Death within 30 days 15 (17%) 46 (20%) 1.00 (0.50 to 2.01) 0.99 15 (17%) 75 (18%) 0.90 (0.58 to 1.40) 0.90 Dialysis 29 (33%) 52 (22%) 2.11 (0.96 to 4.62) 0.06 29 (33%) 135 (32%) 0.76 (0.40 to 1.43) 0.39 Inpatient hemodialysis 25 (29%) 50 (21%) 25 (29%) 116 (28%) Continuous venovenous hemofiltration 5 (6%) 15 (6%) 5 (6%) 53 (13%) Open in a new tab Intervention group: received telenephrology plus face-to-face care (hybrid care). Control group: received only face-to-face care (standard care). Telenephrology sites: sites that offer hybrid care (Mayo Clinic Health System [MCHS] Mankato and La Crosse). Alternate site: offers only standard care (MCHS Eau Claire). a Adjusted for age, Elixhauser, sex, race, diagnosis; adjusted relative rate ratios (aRRR) reported for length of stay. Survey Results Receiving Hospital Provider Survey. The survey was sent to a total of 284 providers in the departments of hospital medicine, internal medicine and subspecialities, surgery, emergency medicine, and family medicine to capture the opinions of as many providers as possible because we could not target only those who had interacted with the telenephrology service ( Figure 2 ). In total, 43 health care team members responded to the survey, with 21 (49%, 11 physicians, three APPs, seven registered nurses) indicating they requested or cared for telenephrology consults as their patients. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Non-nephrology hospital provider views on clinical influence of inpatient telenephrology consults . Most (67%) of the providers requesting nephrology consults thought inpatient telenephrology was either somewhat influential or very influential in reducing patient mortality, whereas 14% thought it was not at all influential. Most (67%) thought telenephrology was as safe as standard nephrology care. Even more (76%) thought the telenephrology consults provided enough information to hospital providers to enable them to make medical decisions with and for their patients. Further, 59% said the telenephrology experience gave them more confidence to manage similar patients. Most (76%) non-nephrology hospital providers consider telenephrology to be somewhat influential to very influential in filling service gaps in the provision of care in the hospital, and 60% recommended expansion of it to other hospital practices. Over half of the non-nephrologist providers consider inpatient telenephrology somewhat influential to very influential in improving efficiency of medical staff (62%) and changes to medical management of patients (67%). In total, 48% thought it was somewhat or very influential in avoiding the need for urgent dialysis, and 38% thought it was somewhat influential to very influential in reducing transfers to the intensive care unit. Of the consult-requesting providers, 76% were very satisfied or satisfied and 10% were dissatisfied with the telenephrology consult response rate. With regard to the mode of communication with the telenephrologist, 62% of the spoke site providers preferred the telephone, whereas 48% preferred video, and 43% preferred reading the telenephrology electronic health record notes; some of the 21 survey respondents selected preferring more than one modality of communication. Several (60%) of the non-nephrology providers recommended expansion of telehealth consult services to other resource-limited areas of the institution ( Supplemental Table 2 ). Telenephrologist Provider Survey. The telenephrology service is staffed by ten physicians and three APPs; 11 providers (eight physicians and three APPs) responded to the telenephrology provider survey ( Figure 3 ). The top three recommendations from telenephrology providers were additional testing (91%), dialysis (91%), and change in medications (64%). The top three telenephrology difficulties the providers experienced were related to sound quality (82%), connectivity (36%), and other—digital stethoscope functionality (22%). There were no reported issues with picture quality. A majority (64%) of the telenephrology providers reported spending less time with patients compared with standard care, whereas 36% reported spending the same amount of time. Most of the telenephrology providers preferred using video (82%) rather than phone (19%) for communication during the telenephrology encounters with patients ( Supplemental Tables 3 and 4 ). Figure 3. Open in a new tab Telenephrology provider views on telenephrology service . Discussion Inpatient telenephrology provides a novel modality to augment nephrology care in resource-limited areas. This pragmatic retrospective study presents objective data on patient outcomes and provider perspectives on an inpatient hospital hybrid care model. We found no significant differences in measured outcomes between patients who were hospitalized and who received nephrology care via a hybrid care model versus those who received standard nephrology care. When used to support face-to-face nephrology care, telenephrology demonstrated clinical and experiential noninferiority. Sensitivity analyses suggested patients who had ≥50% of their inpatient nephrology encounters using telenephrology had significantly lower lengths of stay compared with patients who received standard care, suggesting the possibility of site-level practice differences between the sites that offer hybrid plus standard care (MCHS Mankato and La Crosse) versus MCHS Eau Claire, which offers only standard care. However, the analyses still suggest outcomes for patients who receive hybrid care are noninferior to those who receive standard care. Surveyed telenephrologists consider telenephrology to be safe, and ten out of 11 of them thought it is “always” or “most of the time” safe to provide virtual nephrology care. Over half of the non-nephrology hospital providers who requested telenephrology services also thought the service was either “influential” or “very influential” in reducing patient morbidity, and a small minority (three out of 21) felt it was “not at all influential.” Many providers also appreciate the use of telenephrology as a measure to supplement in-person nephrology patient care. Non-nephrology providers preferred using the telephone to communicate with telenephrologists. Whether this was due to timing, interruption of clinical flow, or other reasons warrants further investigation. Regarding provision of direct care, the majority of the telenephrologists preferred using video to provide virtual care. Opportunities exist to improve the virtual video care platform in the areas of connectivity and digital stethoscope functionality. Respecting the daily workflow of the nephrology teams is a crucial matter as many of the providers, especially nonphysicians, expressed concern that the telenephrology process would increase their workload responsibilities at a time when they are already busy with their usual clinical duties. Hence, a deep understanding of the care environment, while defining process flows that allow uninterrupted quality-based care, is necessary for successful telenephrology implementation. These positive patient outcomes and provider perspectives are similar to the 2-year inpatient telenephrology experience of the Emory/Sanderling Nephrology Services Telenephrology Program, where 125 patients were cared for without an in-house nephrologist in rural Georgia, United States. Although no objective data were presented, the study did not report a higher mortality or longer hospital length of stay, and a favorable reception among patients and their providers for this new model of kidney care ( 11 ). There are recognized limitations to this study. Although it was launched well before, the study occurred during the COVID-19 pandemic, when patients and providers had generally more receptive attitudes toward telehealth as people strove to limit face-to-face interactions to prevent the spread of COVID-19. Although telehealth is likely to persist in our health care system, it is unclear whether patient and provider attitudes will remain positive for this care modality. The patient population in this study is also overwhelmingly White, and although telehealth adoption in the United States has been widespread during the period of the study ( 12 ), its adoption has also varied by race ( 13 , 14 ). Our study focused more on provider perspectives, but this may also vary on the basis of how providers perceive telehealth reception by their patients. Because telenephrology was only offered for 7 consecutive days per month, some non-nephrology providers who prefer only face-to-face care may be less likely to consult nephrology during the days when only telenephrology is available. This may create a selection bias, however, it would not apply to patients requiring urgent nephrology care because delaying care is not possible or ethical. Our health system also has a centralized electronic health record that enables fast and efficient access to crucial patient data that greatly affects provider clinical decisions. The critical ability to communicate and act on recommendations was facilitated by a uniform electronic health record. Other medical centers, especially in more rural and resource-limited areas, may not have such a unified electronic health record. The voice of the patient is an essential component of a telenephrology program that is absent in this analysis. However, it is difficult to effectively survey patients who may be too sick to participate in the process. However, Amundson et al. did survey nine patients who received a total of 43 inpatient telenephrology encounters during a pilot study to introduce inpatient telenephrology at the MCHS Mankato from January to June 2020. Among the nine patients, eight responded to the survey and all reported the highest level of satisfaction with inpatient telenephrology ( Supplemental Figure 4 ). In conclusion, objective outcomes for in-hospital nephrology consults were similar between hybrid and standard care. Non-nephrology hospital providers and telenephrologists had favorable opinions of telenephrology, and most thought it is a safe modality to provide nephrology care. This work suggests that providing inpatient telenephrology care is not only possible but is effective and safe. Disclosures A.G. Kattah reports consultancy agreements with ICU Medical. B. Thorsteinsdottir reports employment with Mayo Clinic; reports owning stock in AMD, Apple, Aurora Blackberry, Cannabis, CRISPS Therapy, General Electric, General Motors, Genetic Tech, Global X Lithium, IBM, KD Kyndryl hldg, Li Auto Inc, Microsoft, Nokia, Novo Nordisk, ORF Genetics, a biotechnological company in Iceland not producing anything of relevance to the field of nephrology to date, and XPEV XPENG Inc; and reports having other interests or relationships with the Coalition for Supportive Care of Kidney Patients. L.A. Androga, L.M. Philpot, P. Ramar, R.C. Albright Jr., and R.H. Amundson report being employed by the Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN. M. D''uscio reports serving on the medical advisory board as volunteer member for The National Kidney Foundation (Twin Cities). Z. Zoghby reports employment with Mayo Clinic and research funding from Kadmon Corporation. All remaining authors have nothing to disclose. Funding This work was supported by the Mayo Clinic, Nephrology & Hypertension Research Committee, $5000 funding for statistical support. Supplementary Material Supplemental Material CJN.13441021SupplementaryData1.pdf (472.9KB, pdf) Acknowledgments We are grateful for the support from the Division of Nephrology & Hypertension at Mayo Clinic, the Department of Medicine, and the Division of Community Internal Medicine at Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN. Additionally, the authors wish to acknowledge the efforts provided by the teammates of the robust and unique environment of the Mayo Clinic Health System. Footnotes Published online ahead of print. Publication date available at www.cjasn.org . Author Contributions R. Albright Jr., R. Amundson, L. Androga, A. Kattah, L. Philpot, B. Thorsteinsdottir, and Z. Zoghby conceptualized the study; R. Albright Jr. and L. Androga were responsible for the data curation; L. Androga was responsible for the formal analysis; R. Albright Jr. and L. Androga were responsible for the funding acquisition; R. Albright Jr., R. Amundson, L. Androga, and M. D’Uscio were responsible for the investigation; R. Albright Jr., R. Amundson, M. D’Uscio, A. Kattah, L. Philpot, B. Thorsteinsdottir, and Z. Zoghby were responsible for the methodology; R. Albright Jr. and L. Androga were responsible for the project administration; R. Albright Jr., L. Androga, and B. Thorsteinsdottir were responsible for the resources; L. Androga, A. Kattah, and L. Philpot were responsible for the software; R. Albright Jr., A. Kattah, B. Thorsteinsdottir, and Z. Zoghby provided supervision; L. Androga, A. Kattah, and L. Philpot were responsible for the validation; L. Androga was responsible for the visualization and wrote the original draft; and R. Albright Jr., R. Amundson, L. Androga, M. D’Uscio, A. Kattah, L. Philpot, and Z. Zoghby reviewed and edited the manuscript. Data Sharing Statement All data used in this study are available in this article. Supplemental Material This article contains the following supplemental material online at http://cjasn.asnjournals.org/lookup/suppl/doi:10.2215/CJN.13441021/-/DCSupplemental . Supplemental Table 1 . Diagnoses groups. Supplemental Table 2 . Additional practice areas to which non-nephrology providers ( n =12) recommended expansion of telehealth. Supplemental Table 3 . Summary of non-nephrology hospital provider comments on telenephrology service. Supplemental Table 4 . Number of telenephrology encounters and face-to-face visits for patients participating in hybrid care model. Supplemental Figure 1 . Map of the Mayo Clinic Health System (MCHS). Supplemental Figure 2 . Inpatient telenephrology provider survey. Supplemental Figure 3 . Inpatient telenephrology non-nephrology providers survey (hospital internal medicine, emergency department, ICU, or other hospital-based staff). Supplemental Figure 4 . Inpatient telenephrology patient survey: pilot study by Amundson et al. References 1. HealthyPeople.gov, Healthy People 2020 : Foundation Health Measure. Available at: https://www.healthypeople.gov/2020/about/foundation-health-measures/Disparities . Accessed November 5, 2021 2. United States Census Bureau : Census 2010 Summary File (SF-1) 100- Percent Data. Available at: https://www.census.gov/library/stories/2017/08/rural-america.html . Accessed November 5, 2021 3. Douthit N, Kiv S, Dwolatzky T, Biswas S: Exposing some important barriers to health care access in the rural USA. Public Health 129: 611–620, 2015 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Harding JL, Li Y, Burrows NR, Bullard KM, Pavkov ME: US trends in hospitalizations for dialysis-requiring acute kidney injury in people with versus without diabetes. Am J Kidney Dis 75: 897–907, 2020 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Fisher M, Neugarten J, Bellin E, Yunes M, Stahl L, Johns TS, Abramowitz MK, Levy R, Kumar N, Mokrzycki MH, Coco M, Dominguez M, Prudhvi K, Golestaneh L: AKI in hospitalized patients with and without COVID-19: A comparison study. J Am Soc Nephrol 31: 2145–2157, 2020 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Pelayo J, Lo KB, Bhargav R, Gul F, Peterson E, DeJoy Iii R, Salacup GF, Albano J, Gopalakrishnan A, Azmaiparashvili Z, Patarroyo-Aponte G, Rangaswami J: Clinical characteristics and outcomes of community- and hospital-acquired acute kidney injury with COVID-19 in a US inner city hospital system. Cardiorenal Med 10: 223–231, 2020 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Ramnath VR, Ho L, Maggio LA, Khazeni N: Centralized monitoring and virtual consultant models of tele-ICU care: A systematic review. Telemed J E Health 20: 936–961, 2014 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Ensell, G: Rural, inpatient dialysis is the telemedicine trifecta: Improving access, improving outcomes and saving millions. 2019. Available at: https://telemedicine.arizona.edu/blog/rural-inpatient-dialysis-telemedicine-trifecta-improving-access-improving-outcomes-and-saving#:~:text=Rural%2C%20Inpatient%20Dialysis%20is%20the%20Telemedicine%