INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3297,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11579576','Is there an editorial glass ceiling? Editorial leadership in nephrology and transplantation journals: A gender-based cross-sectional analysis.','Scarlato RM, Wyburn K, Wyld ML.','2024','gatekeepers pronouns dissipates anzsn gatekeepers appointing pronouns genderize unrepresented pinho eics glacial remunerated caretaking undervalued ripple authoring crafting publishable fundable curtail starchl meritocracy gestalt actioning wyld wyburn scarlato wyld scarlato wyld wyburn nep scarlato wyburn wyld nep medicaldeans factsheet wick nejmsa1916935 malieckal potentiality printsnippet snippet pg_107 printtemplate builder nhmrcs krstacic yaligar e0261209 benjamens banning taj e614 txd pinho vassallo e061054 jayabalasingham geertzen dominici e1913682 palser lazerwitz fotopoulou s41593 azad wasan hubsch cevik editorship mcmullen kosiorek harbell lobl grinnell yost wysong ijwd clinicalkey clinicalkey ezproxy playcontent s174391911400212x returnurl referrer genderize genderize pinho vassallo womersley e2123026 reupert redressing sebo helmer schottdorf neef e21718 sumner s1049096519000945 starchl zollner schwetz knezevic amrein thelancet springernature nep','Abstract Aim Editors‐in‐Chief (EiC) play a key role as gatekeepers in academic medicine, often shaping research agendas. Women have historically been underrepresented in editorial leadership roles in academic medicine. The purpose of this study was to examine gender representation among EiC of contemporary transplantation and nephrology journals. Methods This cross‐sectional study evaluated gender disparities among EiC of transplantation and nephrology medical journals. The study population was drawn from journals in two subject categories (1) ‘Transplantation’ and (2) ‘Urology and Nephrology’ in the 2023 Journal Citation Reports. Binary gender classification (woman/man) was determined by the names/pronouns used to describe the EiC on the journal or institutional webpage. The primary outcome was the proportion of women EiC. Secondary outcome was the proportion of women EiC based on journal topic, location and metrics. Descriptive statistics were used. Gender differences were compared using students t ‐test or Fisher''s exact test. Results A total of 79 EiC were identified of which 16 (20%) were women and 63 (80%) were men ( p < .001). Transplantation and nephrology journals had 21% and 20% women EiC, respectively. The proportion of women to men EiC was not impacted by journal category ( p = .93), journal location ( p = .61), journal impact factor ( p = .71) or quartile ( p = .59). Conclusion There was a disparity in gender representation in EiC in nephrology and transplantation journals, with men holding 80% of all positions. These findings, among growing evidence of gender disparity, highlight a need for targeted efforts to promote gender equity in academic medicine. Keywords: cross‐sectional studies, female, gender equity, nephrology, sex factors Summary at a Glance This cross‐sectional analysis finds significant underrepresentation of women as Editors‐in‐Chief (EiC) in Nephrology and Transplantation journals irrespective of journal category, region or metrics. This further exemplifies the systemic gender disparities in academic medicine. There is need for continued research agendas focused on addressing equity of gender inclusion in academic medicine. 1. INTRODUCTION The pipeline of women entering the fields of nephrology and transplantation is robust. In the United States of America (USA), United Kingdom and Australia women have equally comprised approximately 50% of medical students for over two decades. 1 , 2 , 3 In the last 2 years within nephrology, 37% of first year USA fellows and 56% of advanced trainees (fellow equivalents) in Australia were women. 4 Women now make up 29% of nephrologists in both the USA and Australia. 5 , 6 However the proportion of women in roles with seniority and influence in nephrology and medicine overall then dissipates. 7 In the last 23 years there have been 21 presidents of the American Society of Nephrology (ASN), of whom 4 (19%) have been women (with none prior to 2009). 8 This story is replicated throughout nephrology globally; the Australian and New Zealand Society of Nephrology (ANZSN) has only had 3 (23%) women presidents (since 2000) and the European Renal Association (ERA) has in 2024 appointed its first female president. 9 , 10 Significant gender disparities exist in academic medicine. Women are less likely to be associate/full professors or departmental chairs and this gender gap has not diminished over time. 7 Females are less likely to receive grant funding thus limiting the size and scope of research. 11 In terms of academic publications, females are less likely to be first (27%) or last author (19%) in high‐impact journal publications, figures which have not improved over the past two decades. 12 Where females are first or last author, they receive fewer citations. 13 Additionally, females are less likely to be invited to review papers, 14 and are less likely to author an invited commentary even adjusting for fields of expertise, seniority and publication metrics. 15 Editors‐in‐Chief (EiC) and editorial boards overall, play a key role as gatekeepers of knowledge by appointing editorial board members, defining criteria for publication and hence shaping research agendas and careers. Serving as an EiC or editorial board member, can influence academic opportunities and career progression. 16 Across many specialities, it has been demonstrated that women are significantly underrepresented in positions of EiC and editorial boards more broadly. 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 Given the position of power of EiC, it is essential they represent the diversity of the specialities they represent. There has been no examination of gender representation among EiC in nephrology or transplantation journals. The objective of this study was to determine the gender representation among EiC in nephrology and/or transplantation journals. We explore the proportion of women EiC by region, journal impact factor and journal quartile rank. Our hypothesis was that women would be underrepresented in these critical positions. 2. MATERIALS AND METHODS This cross‐sectional study evaluated sex disparities among EiC of nephrology and transplantation academic medical journals. The study was conducted following the STROBE (STrengthening the Reporting of OBservational studies in Epidemiology) reporting guidelines. 23 2.1. Identification of eligible journals and data extraction We identified the study population from the journals listed by the Journal Citation Reports (JCR) (2023) in two subject categories of (1) ‘Urology and Nephrology’ and (2) ‘Transplantation’. We excluded journals that did not have; a website, a designated EiC, were nursing journals and/or were not Nephrology or solid organ transplantation focused. Table S1 lists each journal and the gender of the EiC. Data extraction was performed by a single author on 5 January 2024. Four variables were extracted: journal title, JCR impact factor and quartile, country and region of publication, the name and gender of the EiC/s. When the role of EiC was shared, all were included. We adopted a binary gender classification of woman/man to analyse the gender of journal editors, due to the limitations of available data for identification of other gender identities. The gender of the EiC/s was determined based on the names and pronouns used for the EiC on the journal webpage and/or the EiC''s personal institutional page. If this was not available genderize.io was used. 24 2.2. Outcomes The primary outcome was the proportion of women appointed as EiC. The secondary outcome was the proportion of women appointed as EiC based on journal characteristics including impact factor and quartile, geographic region and topic category (nephrology and/or transplantation). 2.3. Statistical analysis Descriptive statistics were used. We determined the total number and relative percentages of EiC who were women or men. Gender differences were compared using students t ‐test or Fisher''s exact test due to the small sample size. A p ‐value <.05 was considered statistically significant. STATA 17.0 (StataCorp, College Station, TX, USA) was used for analyses. 2.4. Ethics statement This study used publicly available online data only and so ethics approval and participant consent was not required. 3. RESULTS There were 73 journals that met the inclusion criteria for our study as seen in Figure 1 . Of these 21 (29%) were transplantation journals, 51 (70%) were nephrology journals and 1 (1%) was classified as both. Among the included journals, 67 (92%) had a single EiC, while 6 (8%) had two EiC. Of the 79 total EiC, 16 (20%) were identified as women and 63 (80%) were identified as men ( p < .001). Of the 16 women EiC, 2 (13%) were co‐EiC, 100% of whom had a man co‐EiC. This compared with 10 (16%) men EiC being co‐editors of whom predominantly shared with another man (75%) (Table 1 ). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Flow diagram of journal selection process and identification of Editors in Chief (EiC) for study population. JCR, journal citation reports; N , number of Editors‐in‐Chief; n , number of journals. TABLE 1. Gender distribution of Editors in Chief by journal demographics and metrics in 2023. Woman Man Total p ‐value Editor in Chief total n (%) 16 (20) 63 (80) 79 <.001 Single editor 14 (20) 53 (80) 67 Co‐editors 2 (16) 10 (84) 12 Journal category n (%) .932 Nephrology 11 (20) 43 (80) 54 Transplantation 5 (21) 19 (79) 24 Both 0 (0) 1 (100) 1 Journal location n (%) .61 North America 6 (25) 18 (75) 24 (30) Asia‐Pacific 0 (0) 7 (100) 7 (9) Western Europe 8 (20) 32 (80) 40 (51) Other 2 (25) 6 (75) 8 (10) Journal impact factor (median [IQR]) a 2.85 (1.05–6.45) 2.6 (1.3–3.8) 2.6 (1.3–3.9) .71 Journal impact factor quartile n (%) b .592 Q1 6 (35) 11 (65) 17 Q2 2 (12) 15 (88) 17 Q3 2 (17) 10 (83) 12 Q4 3 (20) 12 (80) 15 Open in a new tab a 1 (1.3%) journal with no reported journal impact factor in JCR 2023. b 18 (25%) of journals without a reported journal impact quartile in JCR 2023. As shown in Figure 2 , transplantation journals had 5 (21%) women and 19 (79%) men as EiC. Similarly, nephrology journals had 11 (20%) women and 43 (80%) men as EiC ( p = .93) (Table 1 ). FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Gender proportion of Editors‐in‐chief (EiC) by journal type in 2023. %, percentage of total Editors‐in‐chief. By region, journals published in North America had 6 (25%) women and 18 (75%) men as EiC, compared to 8 (20%) women and 32 (80%) men as EiC in Western Europe, 7 (100%) men as EiC in Asia‐Pacific and 2 (25%) women and 6 (75%) men as EiC in Latin America and the Middle East (Table 1 ). The proportion of women EiC were not statistically different by region ( p = .61). Of the 73 journals, 72 (98%) had a journal impact factor by JCR. Of those with a journal impact factor, the median impact factor for journals with a woman EiC was 2.85 (IQR 1.05–6.45), compared to a median of 2.6 (IQR 1.3–3.8) for men EiC. Impact factor was not statistically different between women and men EiC ( p = .71). By journal impact factor quartile, women were EiC of 6 (35%) in Q1, 2 (21%) in Q2, 2 (17%) in Q3 and 3 (20%) in Q4 journals ( p = .59) (Table 1 ). 4. DISCUSSION This comprehensive cross‐sectional study is the first to evaluate the gender composition of the EiC of 73 JCR recognised academic journals within nephrology and transplantation. We found a highly significant disparity in gender representation, with men holding 80% of all EiC positions. This gender disparity was present for both transplantation and nephrology journals, where respectively, 79% and 80% of EiC were men. Our study found that this gender disparity was consistent across geographic regions with North America, Europe and Asia Pacific all having a preponderance of male EiC. Gender distribution of EiC was unchanged when analysed by journal impact factor or by journal quartile with men similarly being overrepresented compared with women EiC. The findings of this study add to the growing body of literature that women are consistently unrepresented as EiC in medical and surgical fields including anaesthesiology, 20 dermatology, 21 gastroenterology, 18 infectious diseases, 19 and surgery. 22 Recognising the key role that EiC play in setting the broader research agenda, Pinho‐Gomes et al, recently published a study looking at the gender of EiC of the top 10 journals in 41 different medical specialities. 25 The authors found that females comprised 21% of all EiC (94 of 444), 25 a similar proportion to that identified in our study. With respect to our fields of interest, they found that in the top 10 Transplantation journals, just 1 (10%) EiC was a woman, while in the top 10 Nephrology and Urology (combined) journals 3 (30%) of the EiCs were women. 25 Our work has extended these findings by including all transplant and nephrology journals and shown that the gender disparity is entrenched regardless of journal type, geography or metrics. Why is it that gender disparities in senior leadership positions such as EiC are so entrenched despite continued growing numbers of women in medicine? There have been an equal number of women and men entering the field of medicine for decades, a trend that is also present in transplantation and nephrology specifically, yet any change in gender disparity of EiC has been glacial. The reasons are likely multifactorial and reflect the gendered milieu within which we all live and work. The systemic underrepresentation of women likely reflects implicit (and occasional explicit) gender bias, gender stereotypes that limit women seeking leadership roles, a woman''s continuing role as the primary caregiver in many if not most family structures, lack of women in academia as role models and possibly different career expectations and trajectories of women compared to men. 26 Societal stereotypes perpetuate the notion that women are more suited for nurturing roles rather than assertive leadership positions, leading to fewer opportunities and increased scrutiny when women seek high‐profile roles. This is highlighted in this study as all women in a position of co‐EiC have a male counterpart. Given that leadership roles are not often financially remunerated the interplay between gender roles and time balance is purported to play a role. Women often disproportionately undertake unpaid caretaking roles both at home and within academic environments, such as mentoring and teaching, which are essential but undervalued and not formally recognised. This then can have a ripple effect on available time to be given to roles of leadership and the activities required to achieve them. 27 In addition to being important from an equity perspective, fewer women represented in the most senior leadership position of academic journals in transplantation and nephrology has critical downstream effects. Diversity in EiC and in the editorial board more broadly, may lead to a more diverse pool of peer reviewers, invited commentaries, decrease publication bias against females and underpin the advancement of female academic careers. 17 It has previously been shown that journals with a female EiC published more articles with women as first and last authors and used more women as reviewers than journals with a man as EiC. 14 , 19 , 28 , 29 Authoring and reviewing academic articles are the core ingredients of crafting a scientific career and play a pivotal role in building a case for promotion. Additionally, there is evidence that the research interests of women and men differ systematically. 30 As EiC play a key role in setting research agendas and determining what is and is not publishable (and hence fundable), the preponderance of men as EiC may (indirectly and directly) curtail the research careers of women, bias research trends and impact the overall diversity of the research pool thus having clinical implications. Recently Starchl et al. compared the gender of EiC in the top journals in 12 disciplines between 2011 and 2021 and found the percentage of EiC that were women grew at a rate of <1% per year. The authors point out that at this rate, it will take until 2050 for there to be gender parity in editorial leadership. 31 Given the clear impacts of gender disparity in leadership positions targeted actions are required to ensure that gender parity is reached before the second half of this century. A number of strategies have been suggested and implemented to increase the representation of women. 31 Such strategies have included; establishing clear gender diversity policies with measurable targets alongside the formation of gender and diversity working groups alongside regular monitoring and reporting on gender initiatives as demonstrated by The Lancet group 32 which ensures transparency and accountability. Active recruitment and mentorship programs, such as those by the Australian Medical Association (AMA), 33 help support and prepare women for leadership roles. Flexible work arrangements, promoted by Springer Nature, 34 aiming to accommodate diverse needs and attract a broader pool of candidates. Targeted actions are required to counter balance the inherent bias entrenched in the traditional ‘meritocracy’ system applied for positions of leadership that has led to gender disparity as exemplified in this study. Our study has many strengths and is the first global gender assessment inclusive of all JCR listed journals in nephrology and transplantation. However, there are also limitations which include; the assumption that the EiC on the journal''s official website was accurate and up to date and the cross‐sectional design provides a snapshot at a particular point in time and does not account for potential changes and trends in editorial leadership over time. An important limitation to highlight is the use of a binary gender classification based on publicly available data to assign gender, which could have introduced potential misclassification bias. We recognise existence and importance of non‐binary and gender‐diverse identities however our use of binary gender classifications reflects the current challenges in accurately capturing the full spectrum of gender within the constraints of existing data sources. We acknowledge this as a limitation and advocate for more inclusive data collection practices to better represent all gender identities in future research. Similarly, while we acknowledge the collective contribution of an editorial board to the overall gestalt of a journal, however we have not further assessed the gender composition of editorial boards in this study in a bid to focus on gender representation in positions of leadership. In conclusion, this is the first comprehensive cross‐sectional study of the gender of EiC of all JCR ranked nephrology and transplantation journals. We found that females are consistently underrepresented as EiC regardless of topic area, geography, impact factor or quartile ranking. There is an urgent need for further investigation into causes of this disparity and for actioning of targeted efforts to promote gender equity in these critical positions. These findings provide a foundation for future research and discussion aimed at reducing gender disparities in academic medicine, particularly within the fields of nephrology and transplantation. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS Melanie L. Wyld was responsible for conception, design and oversight of study alongside Kate Wyburn. Rose‐Marie Scarlato performed data collection working alongside Melanie L. Wyld for analysis and interpretation. Rose‐Marie Scarlato and Melanie L. Wyld prepared the manuscript, with contributions and approval from Kate Wyburn prior to final submission. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Supporting information Table S1. Included journal by name and gender of Editor‐in‐Chief in 2023. NEP-29-895-s001.docx (21.8KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENT Open access publishing facilitated by The University of Sydney, as part of the Wiley ‐ The University of Sydney agreement via the Council of Australian University Librarians. Scarlato R‐M, Wyburn K, Wyld ML. Is there an editorial glass ceiling? Editorial leadership in nephrology and transplantation journals: A gender‐based cross‐sectional analysis. Nephrology. 2024;29(12):895‐900. doi: 10.1111/nep.14383 DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT The data underlying this article are available in the article and in its online Supporting Information . REFERENCES 1. Diversity in Medicine: Facts and Figures . AAMC. 2019. Accessed May 23, 2023. https://www.aamc.org/data-reports/workforce/report/diversity-medicine-facts-and-figures-2019 2. Medical Deans Australia and New Zealand . Medical Deans''’ Student Statistics Dashboard. Accessed May 23, 2023. https://medicaldeans.org.au/data/ 3. The state of medical education and practice in the UK 2021. 4. ABIM.org . Percentage of First‐Year Fellows by Gender and Type of Medical School Attended. Accessed May 23, 2023. https://www.abim.org/about/statistics‐data/resident‐fellow‐workforce‐data/first‐year‐fellows‐by‐gender‐type‐of‐medical‐school‐attended.aspx 5. Active Physicians by Sex and Specialty . AAMC. 2021. Accessed May 23, 2023. https://www.aamc.org/data-reports/workforce/data/active-physicians-sex-specialty-2021 6. Australian Government, Department of Health. Nephrology 2016 Factsheet. 7. Richter KP, Clark L, Wick JA, et al. Women physicians and promotion in academic medicine. N Engl J Med. 2020;383(22):2148‐2157. doi: 10.1056/NEJMsa1916935 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Malieckal DA, Ng JH, Shah HH, Hong S, Jhaveri KD. Trends in the proportions of women program chairs, moderators, and speakers at American Society of Nephrology Kidney Week 2011–2019. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol. 2020;15(2):265‐266. doi: 10.2215/CJN.08140719 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Australian and New Zealand Society of Nephrology: Past presidents . Potentiality Online Communities. Accessed May 24, 2023. https://nephrology.edu.au/?action=printsnippet&snippet=pg_107&printtemplate=on&builder=on 10. ERA‐EDTA Facts and dates . ERA. Accessed June 16, 2024. https://www.era-online.org/about-us/who-we-are/history/facts-and-dates/ 11. Gender disparities in NHMRC''s Investigator Grant Scheme . NHMRC. Accessed May 24, 2023. https://www.nhmrc.gov.au/about-us/news-centre/gender-disparities-nhmrcs-investigator-grant-scheme 12. Krstacic JE, Carr BM, Yaligar AR, et al. Academic medicine''s glass ceiling: author''s gender in top three medical research journals impacts probability of future publication success. PLoS One. 2022;17(4):e0261209. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0261209 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Benjamens S, Banning LBD, van den Berg TAJ, Pol RA. Gender disparities in authorships and citations in transplantation research. Transplant Direct. 2020;6(11):e614. doi: 10.1097/TXD.0000000000001072 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Pinho‐Gomes AC, Vassallo A, Woodward M, Peters S. Cross‐sectional study of the relationship between women''s representation among editors and peer reviewers in journals of the British Medical Journal Publishing Group. BMJ Open. 2022;12(5):e061054. doi: 10.1136/bmjopen-2022-061054 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Thomas EG, Jayabalasingham B, Collins T, Geertzen J, Bui C, Dominici F. Gender disparities in invited commentary authorship in 2459 medical journals. JAMA Netw Open. 2019;2(10):e1913682. doi: 10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2019.13682 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Silver JK. Gender equity on journal editorial boards. Lancet. 2019;393(10185):2037‐2038. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(19)31042-6 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Palser ER,