INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3335,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011003','Preprint Servers in Kidney Disease Research: A Rapid Review.','Vlasschaert C, Giles C, Hiremath S, Lanktree MB.','2020','nephjc rigors prepeer rapidity banned philanthropic zuckerberg synopses b2j banning banning nonpeer nonpeer nephritic glomerulus hypernatremia hepatorenal glomerulus nephjc renalfellow preprinting veritas postpublication nephjc jeopardize preprinted preprinted renovascular preprinting preprinting lector beware vetting missteps prepeer unsubstantiated prepeer accustomed unreviewed rapidity contrarily preprinted sparking preprinted nonparticipating nonconflicted scooping stamped indisputable stamp scooping preprinting preprinting scooping noncompetitive democratize presubmission uploading signaled preprinted percolate downside preprinted lanktree krescent 03800320supplementarydata asnjournals dcsupplemental ginsparg deja nasty bhalla chalfie drubin greider hendricks kiley pulverer rosenzweig spiro stebbins strasser swaminathan vijayraghavan wolberger zuckerberg abdill blekhman rxivist e3000269 hindle saderi prereview elifesciences 57d6b284 prereview pourquie prelights kelsall e752 kleinert rawlinson l2301 peiperl e1002563 fontanarosa flanagin deangelis delamothe maslove colbert topf oates rheault hiremath farouk savvy polka kiley e1005473 qunaj atoria gennarelli e180264 chiarelli pinfield richens f1000 k3628 bauchner polka lipsitch e1002549 modjarrad moorthy millett gsell kieny e1001935 kupferschmidt firehose wynants calster heinze schuit mmj damen jaa debray dhiman harhay henckaerts kreuzberger lohman luijken andaur sergeant skoetz ljm kie sperrin spijker steyerberg takada kuijk royen wallisch hooft kgm smeden m1328 majumder mandl e627 e630 yammine signalsblog annesley bastian polka foes marder scoops e30076 casadevall e00516 e00518 sarabipour debat emmott schwessinger hensel e3000151 penfold polka e1008565 pulverer lemberger e103998 serghiou hughey e52646 abdill blekhman e45133 berenbaum hyslop oakden detsky counterbalance 03800320supplementarydata','Abstract Preprint servers, such as arXiv and bioRxiv, have disrupted the scientific communication landscape by providing rapid access to research before peer review. medRxiv was launched as a free online repository for preprints in the medical, clinical, and related health sciences in 2019. In this review, we present the uptake of preprint server use in nephrology and discuss specific considerations regarding preprint server use in medicine. Distribution of kidney-related research on preprint servers is rising at an exponential rate. Survey of nephrology journals identified that 15 of 17 (88%) are publishing original research accepted submissions that have been uploaded to preprint servers. After reviewing 52 clinically impactful trials in nephrology discussed in the online Nephrology Journal Club (NephJC), an average lag of 300 days was found between study completion and publication, indicating an opportunity for faster research dissemination. Rapid review of papers discussing benefits and risks of preprint server use from the researcher, publisher, or end user perspective identified 53 papers that met criteria. Potential benefits of biomedical preprint servers included rapid dissemination, improved transparency of the peer review process, greater visibility and recognition, and collaboration. However, these benefits come at the risk of rapid spread of results not yet subjected to the rigors of peer review. Preprint servers shift the burden of critical appraisal to the reader. Media may be especially at risk due to their focus on “late-breaking” information. Preprint servers have played an even larger role when late-breaking research results are of special interest, such as during the global coronavirus disease 2019 pandemic. Coronavirus disease 2019 has brought both the benefits and risks of preprint servers to the forefront. Given the prominent online presence of the nephrology community, it is poised to lead the medicine community in appropriate use of preprint servers. Keywords: Preprint, social media, peer review, kidney disease, education Introduction Preprint servers are a disruptive force in scientific communication, bound to alter the model by which medical knowledge is disseminated. Preprints are draft manuscripts presenting original research that have not yet undergone peer review. Preprint servers present an option for free public posting of prepeer-reviewed manuscripts, with the goals of establishing a public record of novel ideas, improving the manuscript by public peer review, and improving the rapidity of research dissemination. The first widely used electronic preprint server, called arXiv (pronounced “archive”), was developed by the physics and mathematics community in 1991 ( 1 ). In contrast, major life science journals actively banned the submission of manuscripts that had been published elsewhere for over 3 decades ( 2 ). In the last 10 years, restrictions have been lifted, and preprint server access has grown within the life and medical sciences community ( 3 ). bioRxiv is an open access, not-for-profit online life sciences preprint server that hosts over 80,000 preprints as of May 2020. bioRxiv was founded in 2013 by the Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory with philanthropic funding from the Chan Zuckerberg Initiative ( 4 ). Access to the bioRxiv server is growing exponentially: there were over 2.2 million preprint downloads for October and November 2018, which is more than the cumulative total of downloads during its first 2.5 years of operation. Alongside this growth in preprint server access, tools have been developed to better use them, including dedicated search engines ( 5 ), public peer review platforms ( 6 ), and services that provide curated preprint synopses ( 7 ). More than 170 journals have partnered with bioRxiv to create a streamlined submission process called “B2J” (for “BioRxiv-to-Journal”), where manuscripts can be directly transferred to the peer-reviewed journals at the time of preprint upload. Adoption of preprint servers has lagged in medicine compared with other research fields. Most major medical journals only recently reversed their historic policies banning preprints ( 8 – 11 ), whereas others maintain policies banning them ( 12 ). Concerns about possible dangers of early public access to nonpeer-reviewed medical research primarily drive this policy divide ( 13 – 15 ). medRxiv is a medicine-specific preprint repository launched in June 2019 that was conceived with safeguards designed to reduce this risk ( 10 ). Its stated goals are to “improve the openness and accessibility of scientific findings, enhance collaboration among researchers, document the provenance of ideas, and inform ongoing and planned research through more timely reporting of completed research” ( 16 ). Preprints provide a means for timely discussion of medical research, complementary to other nonpeer-reviewed modalities such as conference presentations and educational blog posts. With one of the most interactive and engaged online medical communities ( 17 ), the field of nephrology is placed to affect the use of preprint servers in medicine. This rapid review will evaluate the current state of preprint server utilization in nephrology and review potential benefits and risks associated with preprint server use from the researcher, reader, publisher, and granting agency perspectives. Materials and Methods To evaluate the uptake of preprint server use in the nephrology field, we searched the titles and abstracts of bioRxiv and medRxiv preprint uploads on January 20, 2020, using the following keywords: “kidney” or “kidneys” or “renal” or “nephrology” or “nephron” or “nephropathy” or “nephrotic” or “nephritic” or “nephrolithiasis” or “ESRD” or “chronic kidney disease” or “CKD” or “dialysis” or “electrolyte” or “hypertension” or “urinalysis” or “glomerulus” or “hypokalemia” or “hyperkalemia” or “hypernatremia” or “hyponatremia” or “hypocalcemia” or “hypercalcemia” or “hepatorenal” or “cardiorenal” or “glomerulonephritis” or “GFR.” This returned 1117 results for bioRxiv and 277 results for medRxiv. Article abstracts were screened for relevance to nephrology by two reviewers and grouped into the clinical field of nephrology or the basic science of kidney function and disease. In total, 628 bioRxiv preprints (504 basic science and 124 clinical research) and 39 medRxiv preprints were deemed relevant and included in the analysis in Figure 1 . The most common reasons for study exclusion were ( 1 ) the search term “kidney” flagged research that had received funding through the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases but did not pertain to kidney disease, ( 2 ) the search term “glomerulus” identified studies on Drosophila olfactory system, and ( 3 ) the electrolyte disorders search terms appeared in studies unrelated to kidney disease. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Exponential growth in nephrology papers submitted to bioRxiv and medRxiv preprint servers since 2013. The time from study completion to publication was calculated for impactful clinical trials in nephrology as determined by the Twitter Nephrology Journal Club (NephJC) and the Renal Fellow Network ( renalfellow.org ) “Top Stories of the Year” from 2010 to 2019. Fifty-two studies were included in the analysis, and data regarding study completion and publication dates were obtained from the seminal publication and ClinicalTrials.gov . We contacted the managing editors for the 20 highest-effect journals in nephrology, according to the Scimago Journal & Country Rank list, in order to inquire about their policies on preprinting. Finally, we performed a rapid review of papers evaluating the role of preprint servers in medicine. We searched the term “preprint” and “preprints” in all titles and abstracts on PubMed on April 29, 2020. The 292 results were imported into Covidence systematic review software ( www.covidence.org ; Veritas Health Innovation, Melbourne, Australia). Two reviewers screened all texts for relevance. Inclusion criteria were: pertains to preprint servers in the life sciences or medicine, including but not limited to bioRxiv and medRxiv; 53 articles met these criteria, 43 of which are included in the text. A summary flow diagram is included as Supplemental Figure 1 , and a list of ten additional texts meeting inclusion criteria but not cited is in Supplemental Material . Preprint Servers in Nephrology: Current Uptake The field of nephrology has a large online presence, with prominent postpublication discussions in Twitter-based journal clubs, podcasts, and blog posts ( 18 ). Preprint submissions of nephrology research are growing at an exponential rate ( Figure 1 ). Over 600 kidney-related manuscripts have been uploaded to preprint servers, including 124 clinical papers in bioRxiv, 504 basic science papers in bioRxiv, and 39 clinical papers in medRxiv, as of January 20, 2020. The median time between study completion (according to ClinicalTrials.gov .) and final publication of impactful clinical trials in nephrology, as selected by the NephJC and Renal Fellow Network annual “Top Stories of the Year” series, was 300 days. Studies ranged in time from 45 to 1278 days ( Figure 2 ). The time of journal submission and electronic proof publication is included in Figure 2 where available. The time from study completion to publication would include time required for statistical analysis, manuscript production, and internal review, which would not be reduced even if the work was submitted to a preprint server. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Opportunity for faster research results dissemination. Elapsed time between study close to publication date for impactful nephrology clinical trials. The median time from study completion to publication was 300 days. We contacted the 20 top-ranked journals in nephrology about their policies regarding preprint servers ( Table 1 ). Of the journals focused on invited reviews, Nature Reviews Nephrology and Advances in Chronic Kidney Disease do not accept preprints, whereas Current Opinion in Nephrology and Hypertension does. Of the remaining 17 journals publishing primary research, 15 (88%) accept papers submitted to preprint servers. In email correspondence, the American Journal of Kidney Disease ( AJKD ) offered the following explanation: “Except by explicit, prior arrangement, manuscripts are considered for publication if the article or its key features ( 1 ) are not under consideration elsewhere, ( 2 ) have not already been disseminated in print or online, and ( 3 ) will not be disseminated in print or online prior to publication in AJKD ….By this policy, a manuscript that had been uploaded as a preprint would not be eligible for consideration by AJKD (unless some prior arrangement had been made).” The Journal of Renal Nutrition replied: “Except by explicit, prior arrangement, the Journal of Renal Nutrition will not consider for publication manuscripts that have been previously posted to [a] preprint server, as this may jeopardize the journal’s double-blind peer review practice.” Table 1. Preprint policies reported by the top 20 journals in nephrology Journals that Accept Preprinted Articles Journals that Do Not Accept Preprinted Articles American Journal of Nephrology Advances in Chronic Kidney Disease a BMC Nephrology American Journal of Kidney Disease Canadian Journal of Kidney Health and Disease Journal of Renal Nutrition CJASN Nature Reviews Nephrology a Clinical Kidney Journal Current Opinion in Nephrology and Hypertension a International Journal of Nephrology and Renovascular Disease JASN Journal of Nephrology Kidney International Kidney International Reports Kidney International Supplements Nephron Nephrology, Dialysis and Transplantation Pediatric Nephrology Seminars in Nephrology Open in a new tab a Only publishes invited reviews. Rapid Review Following a structured rapid review of literature describing the benefits and risks of preprint server use, the following themes were identified ( Figure 3 ). Figure 3. Open in a new tab Identified benefits and risks regarding preprint servers. CIHR, Canadian Institutes of Health Research; MRC, United Kingdom Medical Research Council; NIH, National Institutes of Health. Rapid Access and Dissemination Although improving the quality of research, the peer review and publication processes delay the dissemination of research findings. Median time to publication is roughly 4 months in the biologic sciences ( 19 ) and nearly 1 year for clinical studies ( 20 ). We found a similar delay in important clinical trials in nephrology. A purported benefit of preprints is that findings are shared earlier ( 21 ). Public interest in early access to research developments is particularly acute in medicine, distinguishing medical research from other fields where preprinting is a reflexive part of the publication process ( 14 ). Preprints have the potential to improve the care of patients, provided the contextual sequelae are identified and addressed ( 22 ). Some consider preprinting a compromise of rigor for speed of dissemination and propose that the preprint movement will allow flawed studies (or “bad science”) to become widely available and cited ( 15 ). Caveat lector , or “reader beware,” is an important principle because preprints include no vetting of methodology nor interpretation of results. In spite of the negative effect that it could have on a researcher’s reputation, there are examples of low-quality or incomplete research posted to stake a claim in a popular topic ( 21 , 23 ). However, ethical or scientific missteps in the “rush to publication” have also led to retractions of peer-reviewed papers from high-effect traditional journals ( 24 ). Public Access to Prepeer-Reviewed Research Medical preprints could pose greater risk for harm if relevant but unsubstantiated claims are adopted by physicians, patients, caregivers, or the media ( 14 , 21 , 25 ). Whereas research scientists are trained to identify potential pitfalls in prepeer-reviewed preprints, there is debate about whether this holds true for those not accustomed to reading primary literature and unfamiliar with critical appraisal ( 25 , 26 ). When it comes to health decisions, an individual’s health could be negatively affected by misinterpretation of an unreviewed ( 24 ) or flawed report ( 23 ). medRxiv implemented a checkpoint in an attempt to minimize the risk of harm: prior to upload, contributors are asked whether the preprint could be harmful to patients, which “may include, but is not limited to, studies describing dual-use research and work that challenges or could compromise accepted public health measures and advice regarding infectious disease transmission, immunization, and therapy” ( https://www.medrxiv.org/content/about-medrxiv ). If a paper is withdrawn from a preprint server, the corresponding author is notified, and a one-page description of the reason for withdrawal is posted on the paper’s main page on the preprint server. For transparency and record-keeping purposes, the original submission remains accessible via the “Manuscript History” tab, where it is labeled as withdrawn. Outbreak Science In the context of outbreak science, the rapidity of dissemination via preprint servers has specific benefits ( 27 ). Learning from the 2003 severe acute respiratory syndrome epidemic, where 93% of articles characterizing its epidemiology were published after its conclusion ( 11 ), stakeholders and journal editors agreed in a 2015 World Health Organization consultation that “public disclosure of information of relevance to public health emergencies should not be delayed by publication timelines and that early disclosure should not and will not prejudice later journal publication” ( 28 ). Preprints have provided rapid access to outbreak data during the current coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic ( 29 ), with over 6000 related papers uploaded to preprint servers within 4 months of the first confirmed case. Data from these preprints have been incorporated into systematic reviews to derive prediction models for diagnosing COVID-19 ( 30 ) and generate accurate early epidemiologic parameters estimates, such as the viral reproductive number (R 0 ) ( 31 ). Contrarily, the COVID-19 pandemic has also brought forward examples of preprinted papers with misleading titles or major errors, sparking debate about the safeguards that should be in place to balance speed of dissemination with public safety ( 32 ). Both scientists and journalists have obligations to correctly contextualize preprinted work in the media. Responsible reporting should include emphasis that the work is preliminary, not yet peer reviewed, and include comments solicited from nonparticipating and nonconflicted scientists to reflect the broader scientific community’s opinion ( 26 ). Preprints themselves should be explicitly labeled as not peer reviewed ( 11 ); bear an appropriate, descriptive title; and contain a lay summary that includes a description of study limitations ( 33 ). Mitigation of “Scooping” Preprints provide detailed accounts of work in progress and enable greater control over how research is disseminated. Because all preprints have permanent, time-stamped digital object identifiers, researchers can claim an indisputable stake and time stamp for discoveries ( 19 ). This digital object identifier mitigates the risk of “scooping”—a seemingly intuitive concern held by those considering preprinting ( 23 ). In disciplines where preprinting is the standard, scooping is virtually absent because the provenance of novel findings or concepts is part of the public domain ( 19 ). As well, in the traditional peer review model, established researchers serving as peer reviewers receive early access to yet unpublished results as part of the review process. Preprint servers remove the early access competitive advantage by allowing all the opportunity to view results before formal publication. Early, Noncompetitive, Transparent Peer Review Work submitted to a preprint server before journal submission may benefit from constructive criticism before the rigorous peer review process. The stated purpose of preprint servers is not to replace or undermine the importance of traditional peer review but to facilitate the ongoing evolution of a paper with widespread evaluation by the broader research community. Conference presentations have long served as the venue for delivery of unpublished results ( 34 ). Conference attendance provided early access to study results before peer-reviewed publication while providing authors with an opportunity for feedback from potential peer reviewers and journal editors. With social media, slides from conference presentations are often broadly distributed. Because physical conference attendance is likely to decline due to travel restrictions from COVID-19, there could be a greater role for early online peer review. Preprint servers may democratize access to early presubmission peer review because, formerly, only those in institutions with internal reviews or mentors available for advice may have had access ( 35 ). Preprint journal clubs are becoming increasingly commonplace, serving as an exercise in critical analysis and exposure to the peer review process for journal club participants while also providing valuable feedback to the authors to improve the paper before submission to the competitive publication process ( 36 ). Public feedback on preprints can be delivered via multiple channels. Although only 5% of all bioRxiv preprints have comments on bioRxiv itself, over 70% of authors report receiving feedback on their preprints via social media ( e.g. , Twitter, LinkedIn, and ResearchGate) or private communication. The lack of centralized public discussion space may reflect difficulties with the current comment section interface ( 37 ). Formal peer reviewers may embrace the opportunity to include public comments on a submission under review ( 38 ), and public feedback could be integrated into the peer review process ( 39 ). Further, some journals have begun uploading peer review reports to preprint servers, thereby further increasing the transparency of the peer review process ( 40 , 41 ). Greater Visibility and Recognition Preprints may generate interest in a topic before it is published. Two independent analyses of bioRxiv revealed that articles with preprints receive higher Altmetric scores and more citations than articles without preprints ( 42 , 43 ). Approximately two thirds of bioRxiv preprints are eventually published, after a median time interval of 5.5 months ( 44 ). This timeline is in keeping with the delay between study completion and publication that we observed in nephrology clinical trials. Interest in a preprint paper, signaled by high download count, could influence editorial outcomes: journals with higher-effect factors tend to publish papers with high preprint download count ( 44 ). Submission invitations for preprints of interest is commonplace in the life sciences ( 39 ). Still in its infancy, similar analyses of medRxiv are expected in the coming years. For both medRxiv and bioRxiv, preprints are automatically linked to the corresponding published papers, typically within a few weeks of publication. Reproducibility, Collaboration, and Innovation External validation greatly increases confidence in the validity of results. Researchers posting preprints addressing a similar question may realize that the effect of their results may be greater published in parallel or as a collaborative single paper ( 23 , 34 ). Because preprints are posted irrespective of the perceived importance of the work, they may lead to new discoveries ( 19 , 45 ). Preprints of confirmatory studies or studies with negative findings can help address the reproducibility crisis ( 46 ) and publication bias in science ( 47 ). Citation of Early Work in Grant Funding Applications Major health research funding bodies, including the National Institutes of Health, the Canadian Institutes of Health Research, and the United Kingdom Medical Research Council, have issued statements encouraging preprinted work in grant applications ( 23 ). Preprints may provide a more current and complete view of a researcher’s ideas and progression of work than peer-reviewed work alone ( 3 ). This may be particularly helpful for early-career researchers, whose research may not have had the time to percolate through the peer review process ( 37 ). Preprints can provide the details of the unpublished pilot data that grants reference ( 39 ). One potential downside is that allowing preprints burdens grant reviewers to critically appraise more work ( 3 , 14 ). Most nephrology journals currently accept primary research articles that have been submitted to publicly available preprint servers, and the number of preprinted papers in nephrology is increasing exponentially. A delay between study completion and publication exists, and results are often informally available at conference proceedings long before formal publication. Speed of research dissemination can be of great importance, especially in the context of outbreak science and global pandemics. The traditional model of peer review is evolving to promote transparency and equity of the scientific publication process. In medicine, safety concerns exist regarding public dissemination of scientific results publicly prior to traditional peer review. However, with appropriate safeguards in place, it seems that the benefits of preprint servers outweigh the risks, and their use is likely to continue to grow. Disclosures M.B. Lanktree has received speaker fees and served on advisory boards for Otsuka Pharmaceuticals outside the submitted work. He is supported by the Kidney Research Scientist Core Education and National Training (KRESCENT) program new investigator award. All remaining authors have nothing to disclose. Funding None. Supplementary Material Supplemental Data CJN.03800320SupplementaryData.pdf (217.5KB, pdf) Acknowledgments This manuscript has been submitted to the preprint server medRxiv ( https://doi.org/10.1101/2020.03.16.20036590 ). Footnotes Published online ahead of print. Publication date available at www.cjasn.org . Supplemental Material This article contains the following supplemental material online at http://cjasn.asnjournals.org/lookup/suppl/doi:10.2215/CJN.03800320/-/DCSupplemental . Supplemental Figure 1 . Rapid review of benefits and risks of preprint servers screening strategy. Supplemental Material . Additional references. References 1. Ginsparg P: Preprint déjà vu. EMBO J 35: 2620–2625, 2016. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Cobb M: Preprints: Recall Nature ’s nasty past. Nature 570: 36, 2019. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Berg JM, Bhalla N, Bourne PE, Chalfie M, Drubin DG, Fraser JS, Greider CW, Hendricks M, Jones C, Kiley R, King S, Kirschner MW, Krumholz HM, Lehmann R, Leptin M, Pulverer B, Rosenzweig B, Spiro JE, Stebbins M, Strasser C, Swaminathan S, Turner P, Vale RD, VijayRaghavan K, Wolberger C: Preprints for the life sciences. Science 352: 899–901, 2016. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Callaway E: bioRxiv preprint server gets cash boost from Chan Zuckerberg Initiative. Nature 545: 18, 2017. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Abdill RJ, Blekhman R: Rxivist.org: Sorting biology preprints using social media and readership metrics. PLoS Biol 17: e3000269, 2019. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Hindle S, Saderi D: PREreview—A new resource for the collaborative review of preprints. eLife, 2017. Available at: https://elifesciences.org/labs/57d6b284/prereview-a-new-resource-for-the-collaborative-review-of-preprints . Accessed May 6, 2020 7. Brown K, Pourquié O: Introducing preLights: Preprint hi