INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3337,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6188426','Role of interventional nephrology in dialysis access management.','Tamirisa S, Patel R, Martin KJ.','2011','avfs avfs avgs avfs polytetrafluoroethylene ptfe avfs avfs avfs avfs avgs recirculation outf pseudoaneurysm steal recirculation lok avfs5 redrawn lok al5 distally proximally radiocephalic radiocephalic brachiocephalic dialyzed radiocephalic cimino brescia heeding nonwhite pseudoaneurysms sternal catheters11 angioplasties beathard declot declot unsurpassed fistulograms angioplasties thrombectomies fistulograms malfunction reaped thrombectomies fistulograms angioplasties fistulogram transposed brachio basilic thrill thrill fistulogram stenotic transposed angioplastied fistulogram stenotic transposed transposed fistulogram cephalic stenotic nonwhite radiocephalic subjecting bch fasn srinath tamirisa rakesh martinkj slu sayeed 139x lok 139x tonnessen allon robbin kid551 lok allon moist ftm kindred broughan radiocephalic krishnamurthy 139x allon mccarley wingard shyr pettus hakim ikizler beathard litchfield sreenarasimhaiah margassery bander presternal','Abstract Interventional nephrology is an area of medicine under the subspecialty of nephrology that provides improved vascular access care for dialysis patients. Since vascular access issues are a major cause of hospitalization in dialysis patients, interventional nephrologists help reduce hospitalization rates and costs, providing focused and economical medical care. A case has been presented at the end that highlights some management principles of vascular accesses. We also present a comprehensive review of recent data regarding trends in dialysis access management. Introduction Vascular access has been a major problem in the management of end stage renal disease (ESRD) patients on chronic hemodialysis (HD). Native arteriovenous fistulas (AVFs) are the preferred vascular access for ESRD patients on HD. AVFs are superior to synthetic arteriovenous grafts (AVGs) because of better patency and fewer complications 1 . AVFs have a lower frequency of stenosis, thrombosis, infection, and higher primary patency rates than polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) grafts. Financial costs associated with AVFs are also lower 2 . There have been organized efforts to increase the prevalence of AVFs amongst ESRD patients on HD in the United States 1 . The “Fistula First Initiative” was implemented in 2003 with goals of primary AVF placement in at least 50% of incident HD patients, AVF use in at least 40% of prevalent HD patients, and chronic catheter in less than 10 % prevalent HD patients 3 . Despite these efforts, the rates of AVFs are still much lower than desired in our HD patient population. Many factors including late referral to the nephrologists, inadequate patient education, and lack of formal vascular access surveillance programs, have all been implicated as causes for poor AVF rates 1 . Another factor is high failure to mature rates of AVF, which range from 9–70% 4 . The various complications associated with AVFs, AVGs, and catheters are outlined in Table 1 . Table 1. Complications of Dialysis Access AVF/AVG Catheters Thrombosis Infection/Bacteremia Low flow Thrombosis Recirculation Fibrin Sheath Development Venous Outf low Stenosis Leak or Fracture of Catheter Aneurysm/Pseudoaneurysm Venous stenosis/Venous occlusion Prolonged bleeding post dialysis Low flow Steal Syndrome Recirculation Open in a new tab Current Status In 2006, Lok et al. published a preoperative, clinical prediction rule to determine which fistulas are likely to fail to mature and was validated rigorously. It was found to be simple and easily reproducible and applied to predictive risk categories. A scoring system was devised which placed patients into four different categories: low, moderate, high, and very high risk with predicted risk for failure to mature to be 24, 34, 50, and 69% respectively. These categories are dependent on age, CAD, PVD, and race (See Table 2 ) 5 . Table 2. Clinical Scoring system to predict failure to maturation rates of AVFs5 Variable Points Age > 65 yr +2 PVD +3 CAD +2.5 White −3 Baseline Score +3 Open in a new tab A score above 7 predicts a failure to maturation rate of over 70% and an alternate plan should be in place like an AVG (Redrawn from Lok et al5) Though AVF is the ideal access for a dialysis patient, one must remember that a good functioning AVG is better than a non-functioning fistula. In that respect, one must make sure that the fistula that is being planned in a dialysis patient has the highest chance of maturing. In planning a fistula, it becomes common sense that one must first start distally and move proximally so that proximal veins are spared in case the distal fistula does not mature. However, several reports suggest that the radiocephalic (RC) fistula should not always be the first option. Particular patient groups have been reported to have lower success rates with a RC-AVF, including diabetics, older patients, patients with peripheral vascular disease, women, and individuals with previous failed access procedures 6 . Hence, if based on one’s assessment, if a radiocephalic fistula has a high chance of failing, then one must proceed to a more proximal fistula like a brachiocephalic which has a higher chance of maturing and puts the patient at a lower risk of being dialyzed through a catheter. According to the “Fistula First Initiative,” surgeon selection should be based on best outcomes, willingness, and ability to provide access services. Choi et al. performed an analysis on arteriovenous access placement and outcomes in 75 patients when surgery was performed by one of two dedicated high-volume vascular access surgeons (community and academic medical center surgeon). Preoperative vascular mapping was performed in all the patients. Their analysis demonstrated that surgeon selection can have a significant impact on the rate of fistula placement and its overall survival despite similar findings on preoperative vascular mapping 7 . Lee et al. performed a retrospective study to compare the outcomes of 59 fistulas and 51 grafts that were placed in the upper arm after primary failure of an initial forearm fistula. Primary access failure was higher for subsequent fistulas than for subsequent grafts (44 versus 20%; P = 0.006). Fistulas required more interventions than grafts before their successful use (0.42 versus 0.16 per patient; P = 0.04). The time to catheter-free dialysis was longer for fistulas than for grafts (131 versus 34 d; P < 0.0001) and was associated with more episodes of bacteremia before permanent access use (1.3 versus 0.4 per patient; P = 0.003). Cumulative survival (from placement to permanent failure) was higher for fistulas than for grafts when primary failures were excluded (hazard ratio 0.51; 95% confidence interval 0.27 to 0.94; P = 0.03), but similar when primary failures were included (hazard ratio 0.99; 95% confidence interval 0.61 to 1.62; P = 0.97). Fistulas required fewer interventions to maintain long-term patency for dialysis after maturation (0.73 versus 2.38 per year; P < 0.001). In conclusion, as compared with grafts, subsequent upper arm fistulas are associated with a higher primary failure rate, more interventions to achieve maturation, longer catheter dependence, and more frequent catheter-related bacteremia. However, once the access is usable for dialysis, fistulas have superior cumulative patency than do grafts and require fewer interventions to maintain patency 8 . When the radiocephalic fistula first was described in 1966 by Cimino and Brescia, the patients’ average age was 43 years, almost all had chronic glomerulonephritis, and blood flows were 250 to 300 ml/min. The failure to mature rate was 11%. Today’s patients with ESRD are quite different. In 2003, a large and increasing proportion of people who started dialysis were 75 years or older with the greatest growth in patients who were 85 years of age. Three-quarters of them had five or more comorbidities, with 90% having cardiovascular disease. Overall, 50% of North American dialysis patients had diabetes, and approximately one half and one third had CAD and PVD, respectively. European patients tend to have less diabetes but still have substantial comorbidity. In addition, the average blood pump speed is much higher: 350 to 450 ml/min. Not surprising, achieving functional fistulas in today’s population is more challenging than ever 5 . Published data demonstrate that US nephrologists have been heeding the “Fistula First” recommendations. The proportion of US hemodialysis patients who undergo dialysis with fistulas increased from 26 to 35% in the five-year period between 1998 and 2003. However, during the same time period, catheter use increased from 19 to 27%. Therefore, it seems that each 1% increase in fistula prevalence is associated with a corresponding 1% increase in catheter prevalence. This is most likely due to increased numbers of primary fistula failures. This creates a dilemma in that adhering to one desirable guideline (increasing fistulas) has the unintended consequence of worsening adherence with another desirable guideline (reducing catheters). Achieving the optimal balance between these two competing goals requires an individualized approach 8 . In a subset of patients who are at high risk for failure of maturation of fistulas including women, nonwhite individuals, older patients, and obese individuals, it may be preferable to place a graft instead, so as to minimize the duration of catheter dependence. Certainly, concerted efforts to optimize fistula outcomes are indicated, including early referral to surgeons for fistula creation, preoperative vascular mapping, improvements of surgical technique, aggressive attempts to salvage immature fistulas, and education of staff in cannulation techniques 8 . Interventional Nephrology The cost of vascular access management exceeds $1 billion/year, not to mention the high hospitalization rates and high morbidity/mortality associated with vascular access problems 9 . Interventional nephrology is becoming an active subspecialty of nephrology 10 . Interventional nephrology plays a crucial role in addressing these issues in a timely manner, helping the patient and controlling costs. Their primary role consists of monitoring and surveillance of dialysis accesses that help in prospective treatment of venous stenosis, thus enabling increased access patency rates. The scope of interventional nephrology encompasses several procedures which have been listed in Table 3 . Table 3. Scope of Interventional Nephrology - Procedures Placement of Tunneled Dialysis Catheter Revision/Replacement/Repair of Tunneled Dialysis Catheter Angiography of AVF/AVG Angioplasty of AVF/AVG Thrombectomy of AVF/AVG Ligation/Occlusion of accessory veins Evaluation of poorly functioning AVF/AVG Evaluation of Limb swelling with AVF/AVG/Catheters Vein Mapping by Ultrasound and/or Venography\Placements of stents and stent grafts Endovascular therapy of aneurysms/Pseudoaneurysms of AVF/AVG Placement of Abdominal/Pre-Sternal peritoneal dialysis catheters11 Revision/Removal of Peritoneal dialysis catheter Open in a new tab While there has been a significant increase in angioplasties, from 0.09 per patient year during a period of no surveillance to 0.54 per patient year during aggressive surveillance phase, it has major benefits. Graft thrombosis rates were decreased from 0.71 to 0.16 events per patient year. Catheter use changed from 0.29 to 0.07 per patient year. Hospital days related to vascular access morbidity were reduced from 1.8 to 0.4 per patient year. Missed dialysis treatments were altered from 0.98 to 0.26 per patient year. The total cost of treatment for thrombosis related events was reduced by 49% 9 . Beathard et al. also published a case series involving a total of 14,067 cases who were categorized under six different procedures: 13,503 cases (96.18%) were successful. The number of cases performed in each individual category with success rates for each were as follows: Tunneled Dialysis Catheter (TDC) placement—1765 cases, 98.24% successful; TDC exchange—2262 cases, 98.36% successful, AVF-PTA— 1561 cases, 96.58% successful; graft-PTA—3560 cases, 98.06% successful; AVF declot—228 cases, 78.10% successful; graft declot—4671 cases, 93.08% successful. When the overall results obtained in the cases, performed by a diverse group of interventional nephrologists, are compared with that which have been reported in the medical literature derived primarily from interventional radiologists, it is clear that interventional nephrologists can perform these procedures both effectively and safely. An overall success rate of 94.86% for 14,067 procedures with a major complication (defined as those requiring an increase in level of care, or result in hospitalization, permanent adverse sequelae, or death) rate of only 0.28%, a procedure time of 39.5 minutes, and a fluoroscopy time of 5.9 minutes is unsurpassed 10 . An interventional nephrologist is in the best position to deal with dialysis access issues. He has the motivation to care for dialysis patients. Knowledge of dialysis and its principles allow continuity of care and timely arrangement of dialysis, especially post-procedure. Saint Louis University was one of the first nephrology programs in the country to establish an interventional nephrology program. We, at Saint Louis University, have a very active interventional nephrology program and perform all of the various procedures (see Table 3 ), including fistulograms, angioplasties, thrombectomies using various techniques, peritoneal dialysis catheter placement 11 PD catheter removal, and tunneled dialysis catheter placement. Our nephrologists are very active regarding access surveillance with frequent physical examinations and access flow measurements. The dialysis patients are immediately referred for fistulograms if there are any warning signs of access malfunction. These include low Kt/V, prolonged post dialysis bleeding, abnormal physical exam, and decreased access flow. Some of the benefits we have reaped over the years from our interventional nephrology program have included outpatient management of dialysis access problems, same day or within 24-hour thrombectomies, and maximizing the use of native fistulae through surveillance and timely fistulograms and angioplasties. This in turn has allowed us to minimize the use of dialysis catheters. Even in incident dialysis patients with no access, we have been able to place tunneled dialysis catheters immediately. This minimizes the risk of unnecessary multiple central venous accesses. We also perform vascular mapping on our patients and provide an integrated approach to vascular access care through our valuable input to our surgeons, improving care and outcomes. Case Presentation. A 40-year old African-American female was referred to us for a fistulogram of her right transposed brachio-basilic fistula, which had been placed six months prior but did not appear ready for use. On clinical exam, a palpable thrill was felt for only 4 cm. along the venous outflow tract after which the thrill was diminished. There was no dilated vein available for cannulation. This fistula had therefore failed to mature. We performed a fistulogram, which revealed a 90% stenotic lesion in the mid transposed vein. This lesion was angioplastied initially using a cutting balloon and then with a larger non-compliant 8 × 40 angioplasty balloon. After angioplasty, there was no residual stenosis. A post-angioplasty fistulogram revealed excellent flow across the stenotic lesion and uniform caliber of the transposed vein (See Figure 1 ). This transposed vein fistula can now mature and develop into a useful access. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Panel A - Fistulogram revealing 90% stenosis in the cephalic vein. Panel B - Angioplasty balloon placed across the stenotic lesion. Panel C – Angioplasty balloon inflated to eliminate stenosis. Panel D - Post-angioplasty angiogram revealing a satisfactory AV fistula of uniform caliber with excellent flow. Conclusion AVF continues to remain the ideal vascular access for ESRD patients. However, a good functioning AVG is better than a non-functioning AVF. Hence, it is ideal to have a scoring system, which could predict failure to mature of an AVF. In patients with a high risk for failure to mature, including women, nonwhite individuals, older patients, and obese individuals, one must consider placing an AVG which has a higher likelihood of functioning. Also, radiocephalic fistulas have higher non-maturity rates in high risk subjects. Hence, a proximal AVF or AVG should be considered as the access of choice in these patients. With more elderly patients being placed on dialysis, one must consider the overall prognosis before subjecting them to access placement (surgical procedure). In such circumstances, dialysis can be done using tunneled dialysis catheters. Interventional nephrology is a new and emerging field which has been highly successful in improving patency rates, reducing costs and hospitalizations, and improving dialysis delivery. Biography Kevin J. Martin, MB, BCh, FASN, Srinath Tamirisa, MD, and Rakesh Patel, MD, are all in the Division of Nephrology, at Saint Louis University Division of Gastroenterology and Hepatology. Contact: martinkj@slu.edu Open in a new tab Footnotes Disclosures None reported. References 1. Haq NU, Sayeed S, Ali SA. Impact of training in interventional nephrology on hemodialysis vascular access types. Semin Dial. 2009;22(1):90–2. doi: 10.1111/j.1525-139X.2008.00508.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Lok CE, Oliver MJ. Overcoming barriers to arteriovenous fistula creation and use. Semin Dial. 2003;16(3):189–96. doi: 10.1046/j.1525-139x.2003.16038.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Tonnessen BH, Money SR. Embracing the fistula first national vascular access improvement initiative. J Vasc Surg. 2005;42(3):585–6. doi: 10.1016/j.jvs.2005.05.030. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Allon M, Robbin ML. Increasing arteriovenous fistulas in hemodialysis patients: problems and solutions. Kidney Int. 2002;62(4):1109–24. doi: 10.1111/j.1523-1755.2002.kid551.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Lok CE, Allon M, Moist L, Oliver MJ, Shah H, Zimmerman D. Risk equation determining unsuccessful cannulation events and failure to maturation in arteriovenous fistulas (REDUCE FTM I) J Am Soc Nephrol. 2006;17(11):3204–12. doi: 10.1681/ASN.2006030190. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Jennings WC, Kindred MG, Broughan TA. Creating radiocephalic arteriovenous fistulas: technical and functional success. J Am Coll Surg. 2009;208(3):419–25. doi: 10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2008.11.015. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Choi KL, Salman L, Krishnamurthy G, Mercado C, Merrill D, Thomas I, et al. Impact of surgeon selection on access placement and survival following preoperative mapping in the “Fistula First” era. Semin Dial. 2008;21(4):341–5. doi: 10.1111/j.1525-139X.2008.00446.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Lee T, Barker J, Allon M. Comparison of survival of upper arm arteriovenous fistulas and grafts after failed forearm fistula. J Am Soc Nephrol. 2007;18(6):1936–41. doi: 10.1681/ASN.2006101119. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. McCarley P, Wingard RL, Shyr Y, Pettus W, Hakim RM, Ikizler TA. Vascular access blood flow monitoring reduces access morbidity and costs. Kidney Int. 2001;60(3):1164–72. doi: 10.1046/j.1523-1755.2001.0600031164.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Beathard GA, Litchfield T. Effectiveness and safety of dialysis vascular access procedures performed by interventional nephrologists. Kidney Int. 2004;66(4):1622–32. doi: 10.1111/j.1523-1755.2004.00928.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Sreenarasimhaiah VP, Margassery SK, Martin KJ, Bander SJ. Percutaneous technique of presternal peritoneal dialysis catheter placement. Semin Dial. 2004;17(5):407–10. doi: 10.1111/j.0894-0959.2004.17356.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',4,'2025-10-15 06:16:08','2025-10-15 06:16:08');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3338,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9280854','Documentation of acute kidney injury at discharge from the neonatal intensive care unit and role of nephrology consultation.','Chmielewski J, Chaudhry PM, Harer MW, Menon S, South AM, Chappell A, Griffin R, Askenazi D, Jetton J, Starr MC; Neonatal Kidney Collaborative.','2022','awaken awaken awaken awaken awaken nct02443389 awaken notated acyclic dag dag biasing discharging awaken awaken awaken uop uop uop uop uop uop uop outborn decompositions cde nde cde nde cde nde decompositions cde nde cde nde cde nde appreciably awaken awaken awaken nihms1820712 supplemental_files_1 samantha proofreading awaken medidata rave pican nkc awaken pican ul1tr001417 awaken ul1tr001449 u54tr001356 awaken nuwellis bioporto seastar inventions hl148394 l40 hl148910 hl146818 nct02443389 s41372 reprints reprints namasivayam ambalavanan selewski subrata jeffery carolyn abitbol marissa defreitas shahnaz duara ronnie guillet angio ayesa mian rademacher maroun mhanna rupesh raina deepak jetton brophy tarah colaizy ayse akcan arikan kupferman alok bhutada shantanu bonachea ingraham mahan arwa dower mamta fuloria kimberly reidy kaskel danielle soranno gien katja aftab chishti mina sangeeta hingorani tara ohls staples smriti rohatgi sidharth sanjay wazir surender khokhar perazzo patricio revenis mammen synnes wintermark zappitelli woroniecki shanthy sridhar centenary holtz charlottesville golisano metrohealth maimonides brooklyn aurora medant medicity gurgaon cloudnine gurgaon haryana apollo cradle gurgaon haryana stony stony babykidney jetton boohaker wazir rohatgi soranno awaken guillet reidy tipple jetton khwaja c179 carmody rhone twombley kula chabra hingorani twombley bonachea rigdon jetton guillet askenazi dill shrier acyclic textor zander gilthorpe liskiewicz acyclic dagitty askenazi awaken askenazi ambalavanan zappitelli ambalavanan askenazi moxey mims askenazi abitbol boohaker raina dower awaken cheapest guillet samra harer tipple reidy s41372 mammen skippen nadel collet selewski hyatt harbinger vanderweele unification nihms1820712 supplemental_files_1 babykidney','Abstract OBJECTIVE: To investigate whether NICU discharge summaries documented neonatal AKI and estimate if nephrology consultation mediated this association. STUDY DESIGN: Secondary analysis of AWAKEN multicenter retrospective cohort. Exposures: AKI severity and diagnostic criteria. Outcome: AKI documentation on NICU discharge summaries using multivariable logistic regression to estimate associations and test for causal mediation. RESULTS: Among 605 neonates with AKI, 13% had documented AKI. Those with documented AKI were more likely to have severe AKI (70.5% vs. 51%, p < 0.001) and SCr-only AKI (76.9% vs. 50.1%, p = 0.04). Nephrology consultation mediated 78.0% (95% CL 46.5–109.4%) of the total effect of AKI severity and 82.8% (95% CL 70.3–95.3%) of the total effect of AKI diagnostic criteria on documentation. CONCLUSION: We report a low prevalence of AKI documentation at NICU discharge. AKI severity and SCr-only AKI increased odds of AKI documentation. Nephrology consultation mediated the associations of AKI severity and diagnostic criteria with documentation. INTRODUCTION Acute kidney injury (AKI) is common in the Neonatal Intensive Care Unit (NICU), occurring in approximately 30% of high-risk neonatal admissions [ 1 ]. Neonatal AKI is associated with an increased risk of in-hospital mortality, longer hospital length of stay, and increased risk of poor long-term outcomes including chronic kidney disease [ 1 , 2 ]. Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) clinical guidelines recommend outpatient follow-up after an episode of inpatient AKI to assess kidney function and monitor for complications [ 3 ]. Despite its importance, AKI remains under-recognized in the NICU, where it is documented in only 10–30% of neonates with AKI [ 4 – 7 ]. Without documentation of AKI on the NICU discharge summary, primary care physicians and other pediatric providers may not identify which neonates had AKI and would thus benefit from long-term kidney function follow-up. This impacts providers’ ability to monitor and intervene in these patients to prevent the development or slow the progression of chronic kidney disease. Furthermore, it is unknown whether AKI severity or AKI diagnosis criteria (serum creatinine (SCr) and/or urine output) plays a role in AKI documentation. Existing studies evaluating the frequency of neonatal AKI documentation have been primarily single center and have used only SCr for diagnosis, limiting their generalizability [ 4 – 6 ]. We evaluated the association between AKI occurrence and discharge summary documentation in the Assessment of Worldwide Acute Kidney Injury Epidemiology in Neonates (AWAKEN) cohort. We hypothesized that the prevalence of AKI documentation on discharge summaries would be low. We also hypothesized that severe AKI would be associated with a greater prevalence of AKI documentation and AKI occurrence based solely by urine output (urine output-only) would be associated with a lower prevalence of AKI documentation. Additionally, we investigated whether nephrology consultation in the NICU moderated or mediated these associations. SUBJECTS AND METHODS Setting and patient population The AWAKEN study is a multinational, multicenter retrospective cohort study of neonates admitted to the NICU at 24 institutions in four countries between January 1 and March 31, 2014. A complete description of the AWAKEN study and the epidemiology of neonatal AKI have been published elsewhere [ 1 ]. Briefly, the AWAKEN cohort included infants who received intravenous fluids in the NICU for at least 48 h. Infants were excluded if they were admitted after 14 days of age, had a lethal chromosomal abnormality, died within 48 h of NICU admission, or had a severe congenital kidney or urinary tract abnormality. Infants with sufficient clinical data for AKI diagnosis, i.e., at least two SCr measurements and/or urinary output data were included in this analysis. The Institutional Review Board (IRB) at the University of Alabama at Birmingham approved this collaborative multicenter study, and each center received approval from their respective IRBs for participation. The study design allowed for a waiver of informed consent or parental permission. This study is registered with ClinicalTrials.org , number NCT02443389 . Definitions Our exposure of interest was AKI during neonatal NICU hospitalization, defined by the modified-neonatal KDIGO criteria. Data to determine AKI status were obtained as part of clinical care, including all SCr values during NICU hospitalization and urine output for the first week of age. AKI was defined by a rise in SCr of ≥0.3 mg/dL within 48 h, 1.5-fold increase from the previous lowest value, or urine output <1 mL/kg/hour during a 24-hour period on postnatal days 2–7 ( Supplementary Item 1 ) [ 1 , 8 ]. The maximum stage of AKI (stages 1–3) was determined based on modified-neonatal KDIGO definitions for either SCr or urine output criteria. We defined severe AKI as stage 2 and stage 3 AKI. For neonates with multiple episodes of AKI, the highest stage of AKI reached was used for analysis. The primary outcome of interest was AKI documentation at NICU discharge (AKI doc ). AKI doc was defined as NICU discharge summary documentation of AKI or related terms such as acute renal failure by ICD-9 codes beginning with 584.X. Sites participating in AWAKEN notated the presence of these ICD-9 codes in discharge summaries as part of the data entry sheets completed for each participant [ 9 ]. Statistical analysis We reported descriptive statistics such as frequencies with percentages, means with standard deviations (SD), and medians with interquartile ranges. We compared group differences in AKI doc with either X 2 or Fisher’s exact tests as appropriate and student’s t -test. McNemar’s Q was used to test agreement between AKI occurrence and AKI doc . We used bivariate and multivariable logistic regression to estimate the associations between the exposures and the outcome, reporting odds ratios (OR) with 95% confidence intervals (CI). We developed causal models a priori using directed acyclic graphs (DAG) informed by the literature and our clinical expertise to better understand the relationships among our factors of interest relative to our exposures and outcome and to identify confounding bias [ 10 ]. Our DAG-informed adjustment set identified birthweight, out-born status, and 5-minute APGAR score as minimally sufficient to close non-causal, biasing pathways [ 4 , 11 ]. We gave preference to birthweight alone and not gestational age due to concerns for collinearity; adding gestational age to the models did not substantially change our results [ 4 ]. We used our causal models to better evaluate the role of nephrology consultation during NICU hospitalization relative to the associations between exposures and outcome [ 8 ]. Based on the literature and our clinical knowledge, we conceptualized nephrology consultation as either a moderator or mediator of the associations of AKI severity and AKI diagnostic criteria with AKI doc . It is plausible that nephrology consultation could mediate these associations, whereby a NICU provider detects possible AKI of sufficient severity to consult a specialist to help manage the patient which, in turn, increases the likelihood that the discharging NICU provider would document an AKI episode on the discharge summary. Additionally, nephrology consultation may prompt AKI documentation for those patients with more severe AKI, where consultation would be moderating the association between AKI severity and AKI doc . For additional details of the causal mediation analysis methods, including the definition of terms used, please see Supplementary Methods ( Supplementary Item 2 ). For all analyses, a two-sided p value of <0.05 was considered statistically significant, including the interaction term p value between nephrology consultation and each exposure for evidence of effect modification. All statistical analyses were performed at the University of Alabama at Birmingham using SAS version 9.4 (Statistical Analysis Software Institute Inc., Cary, NC). RESULTS Patient characteristics Of the 2,162 neonates in the AWAKEN cohort, 268 met at least one of the main AWAKEN exclusion criteria ( Supplementary Item 3 ). Of the 1,894 eligible neonates, 605 had AKI (32%) and were included in this analysis. Compared to neonates without AKI, those with AKI had statistically significantly greater proportions of male sex (57%), White race (69%), and non-Hispanic ethnicity (73%). Among the 605 subjects with AKI included in this analysis, the mean GA was 33.9 weeks (SD 5.5) and the mean birthweight was 2335 g (SD 1132). Of those with AKI, 281 (46.5%) had stage 1 AKI, 143 (23.6%) had stage 2 AKI, and 181 (29.9%) had stage 3 AKI ( Table 1 ). AKI prevalence varied by study site ( Fig. 1 ). Table 1. Comparison of patient characteristics by acute kidney injury documentation at discharge. Entire cohort ( n = 605) Undocumented AKI ( n = 527) AKI doc ( n = 78) P Value a Patient characteristics Male sex (%) 347 (57.4) 303 (57.5) 44 (56.4) 0.9 Ethnicity (%) Hispanic 60 (9.9) 51 (9.7) 9 (11.5) 0.52 Non-Hispanic 441 (72.9) 382 (72.5) 59 (75.6) Unknown 104 (17.2) 94 (17.8) 10 (12.8) Race (%) White 364 (60.2) 316 (57.0) 48 (61.5) 0.96 Black 107 (17.7) 94 (17.8) 13 (16.7) Other 134 (22.1) 117 (22.2) 17 (21.8) Gestational age, weeks (%) 22 to <29 131 (21.7) 99 (18.8) 32 (41.0) <0.001 29 to <36 168 (27.8) 153 (29.0) 15 (19.2) ≥36 306 (50.6) 275 (52.2) 31 (40.0) Mean birthweight (SD) 2335 (1132) 2394 (1113) 1941 (1183) <0.001 Very low birthweight, <1500 g (%) 179 (29.6) 145 (27.5) 34 (43.6) 0.004 Small for gestational age (%) 117 (19.3) 107 (20.4) 10 (12.8) 0.12 Mode of delivery (%) Vaginal delivery 281 (46.5) 253 (48.0) 28 (36.0) 0.14 C-section scheduled 