iod (32.5 vs. 12.6, p < 0.001). There was significant variation in the number of SCr measurements between study sites ( Supplementary Item 4 ; p = 0.003). Using intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC), only 9% of the total variation in creatinine counts was due to study site while the majority (91%) of the variation was explained by variation among patients. Those with AKI doc were also more likely to have severe AKI compared to those without AKI doc (70.5% vs. 51%, p < 0.001). Neonates with urine output-only AKI were less likely to have AKI doc than those with SCr-only AKI ( p < 0.0001). We did not observe a difference in AKI doc between SCr-only AKI and SCr combined with urine output-defined AKI ( p = 0.44). There was a higher percentage of nephrology consultation for neonates with AKI doc compared to those without AKI doc (57.7% vs 4.7%, p < 0.001). AKI doc varied by study site, ranging from 0 to 45% ( Fig. 1 ). After adjustment for birthweight, out-born status, and 5-minute APGAR score, neonates with urine output-only AKI were less likely to have AKI doc compared to SCr-only AKI (OR 0.28, 95% CI 0.09–0.91) ( Table 2 ). After stratification according to nephrology consultation, stage 3 AKI was associated with AKI doc compared to stage 1 AKI (OR 4.59, 95% CI 1.15–18.3), among neonates with a nephrology consultation. Among neonates in whom nephrology was not consulted, urine output-only AKI was associated with decreased AKI doc compared to SCr-only AKI (OR 0.25, 95% CI 0.07–0.92). However, we did not observe statistically significant evidence of effect modification between nephrology consultation and either exposure (AKI severity or diagnostic criteria). Table 2. Association of AKI severity and AKI diagnosis criteria with AKI documentation at discharge, stratified by nephrology consultation. AKI doc (%) Unadjusted OR (95% CI) Adjusted OR a (95% CI) P value Stage of AKI 1 23 (8.2) Referent Referent 2 17 (11.9) 1.51 (0.78–2.93) 0.87 (0.38–1.96) 0.73 3 38 (21.0) 2.98