3 65.9–90.7 86.8 75.9–97.6 Open in a new tab CDE controlled direct effect, CL confidence limits, ERR excess relative risk, NDE natural direct effect, NIE natural indirect effect, OR odds ratio. a Adjusted for birthweight, out-born status, and 5-minute APGAR score. DISCUSSION In this large multinational, multicenter retrospective study of neonates with AKI in the NICU, prevalence of AKI doc was low, particularly in neonates with AKI defined solely by urine output criteria. We observed that only 13% of neonates with AKI had AKI doc , which is comparable to previous studies [ 4 – 6 ]. We observed that only AKI defined by urine output criteria was associated with a lower likelihood of AKI doc , and AKI severity was associated appreciably with AKI doc . There was also variation in the rates of AKI doc between clinical sites. Although the AWAKEN study was not designed to determine the contributing factors to low AKI doc , we provide the following possibilities. First, there remains no universally accepted definition of neonatal AKI [ 12 – 14 ]. While the modified-neonatal KDIGO definition is now considered the standard for AKI diagnosis in the NICU, it was not routinely used across centers during the time of this study [ 9 ]. Second, using SCr alone for neonatal AKI diagnosis will fail to capture all neonates with AKI as 37% of neonates with AKI in this analysis were diagnosed based solely by decreased urine output [ 1 , 15 ]. Urine output-only AKI is more challenging for providers to diagnose as urine output can be difficult to assess accurately and consistently in neonates [ 16 ]. Additionally, nephrologist availability and neonatologists’ decisions to involve nephrology are highly variable among NICUs, limiting universal involvement of nephrologists during the NICU hospitalization [ 6 ]. The observed heterogeneity of AKI doc across AWAKEN sites suggests this possibility. This heterogeneity is similar to what has been described in other similar studies, and is likely multifactorial in nature [ 7 ]. Provider perception of the clinical relevance of a mild change in SCr or urine output may also affect provider documentation or nephrology consultation, as a recent study found that neonatologists were less likely to document mild AKI [ 17 ]. Indeed, in the majority of centers a nephrology consultation requires that a NICU provider recognize possible AKI and be sufficiently concerned to consult a specialist. This was a major reason we investigated nephrology consultation as both a moderator and mediator in our causal model. Without studies that delineate the short and long-term consequences of AKI in neonates, providers may be less likely to appreciate fully the importance of documenting AKI [ 18 – 20 ]. Our causal mediation analysis found that nephrology consultation played a considerable role in the relationship between AKI occurrence and AKI doc . We found that nephrology consultation mediated approximately 80% of the total effect of both AKI severity and AKI diagnostic criteria on AKI doc and that approximately 90% of the total effect of both AKI severity and AKI diagnostic criteria on AKI doc would have been eliminated if nephrology consultation was absent. There are several potential explanations for these findings. More severe AKI and SCr-only AKI are more likely to be detected by NICU providers, who are then more likely to consult nephrology. The fact that NICU discharge providers are possibly more aware of AKI coupled with the fact that discharge providers are more likely to see that a pediatric nephrologist was involved in the patient’s care increases the likelihood that AKI is documented at NICU discharge [ 17 ]. While we did not find convincing evidence of an interaction between nephrology consultation and AKI severity, we did observe evidence of an interaction between nephrology consultation and AKI diagnostic criteria. This suggests that the magnitude of the association between AKI diagnostic criteria and AKI doc is greater if a nephrologist is consulted. Our analysis has several strengths, including use of a data from a large, well-described multicenter study and use of causal inference methods that are novel in this field to evaluate further the role of nephrology consultation. Despite these novel findings, we acknowledge several limitations of this study. We are not able to infer causal relationships based on the original study’s design. It is possible that unmeasured confounding factors exist for any of the evaluated associations. In particular, we note that we were unable to ascertain when AKI occurred relative to when nephrology was consulted or when potentially confounding factors occurred. Additionally, AWAKEN was a retrospective cohort study and we could not more rigorously determine the reasons underlying why AKI discharge documentation information was missing. For these reasons, our causal mediation analysis was an exploratory analysis. Despite these limitations, our findings further extend the existing body of work on documentation of neonatal AKI. Future studies can in part mitigate these potential concerns by including sensitivity analyses to estimate the effect of unmeasured confounding [ 21 ]. In conclusion, we report that AKI was infrequently documented in NICU discharge summaries among those neonates with AKI, especially those with AKI defined solely by urine output. We also found that nephrology consultation was an important mediator of this relationship, which may reflect NICU provider’s recognition of clinically relevant AKI that would benefit from nephrology involvement. Our findings emphasize the need for further collaboration between nephrologists and neonatologists in many ways, including the establishment of clinical guidelines for neonatal AKI (including guidelines for recognition, documentation, and management, including follow-up care). Establishing guidelines would allow those neonatologists without nephrologists available for inpatient consultation to better provide for neonates with AKI. Our findings also raise multiple questions in which future research is needed. Future studies should examine the impact of appropriate AKI diagnosis and documentation in the discharge summary on outpatient care, a better understanding if AKI doc improves follow-up rates, CKD detection or long-term kidney health, as well as strategies to improve the diagnosis and documentation of AKI in this patient population. Supplementary Material Supplemental Files 1-6 NIHMS1820712-supplement-Supplemental_Files_1-6.pdf (296.8KB, pdf) ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS The authors would like to thank Samantha Wallace (Department of Pediatrics, Indiana University School of Medicine) for help with technical editing and proofreading of this manuscript. FUNDING Cincinnati Children’s Hospital Center for Acute Care Nephrology provided funding to create and maintain the Assessment of Worldwide Acute Kidney Injury Epidemiology in Neonates (AWAKEN) study Medidata Rave electronic database. The Pediatric and Infant Center for Acute Nephrology (PICAN) provided support for web meetings and for the Neonatal Kidney Collaborative (NKC) steering committee annual meeting at The University of Alabama at Birmingham (UAB), as well as support for 2 of the AWAKEN study investigators at UAB (D.A. and R.G.). PICAN is part of the Department of Pediatrics at UAB and is funded by Children’s of Alabama hospital, UAB Department of Pediatrics, UAB School of Medicine, and UAB Center for Clinical and Translational Sciences (National Institutes of Health grant UL1TR001417). Finally, the AWAKEN study at The University of New Mexico was supported by the Clinical and Translational Science Center at The University of New Mexico (National Institutes of Health grant UL1TR001449) and by The University of Iowa Institute for Clinical and Translational Science (grant U54TR001356). The AWAKEN study investigators at the Canberra Hospital at the Australian National University Medical School were supported by