INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3345,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5839962','Point-of-care ultrasound education to improve care of dialysis patients.','Mullangi S, Sozio SM, Segal P, Menez S, Martire C, Shafi T.','2018','demeanor sphygmomanometer thermometer oximeter otoscope fortifying invented laennec parasternal plax sternal intercostal angled flapping plax epss parasternal costal subcostal xyphoid centimeters collapsibility collapsibility centimeters collapsibility anteriorly clavicular laterally posteriorly scapular comets rockets euvolemic lust ribs midaxillary costovertebral transhepatic hydroureter pubis caudally collapsibility hemothorax aium crackles uncommonly pedal crackles lymphatics collapsibility intradialytic intradialytic intradialytic midodrine intradialytic lust doqi sixes doqi overloaded diurese diuresis uremia echolucent crescent pseudogout pseudogout monosodium tophi ultrasonographer asdin bayview r03 parasternal markel nejmp048251 tenuous tether nejmp1509265 kaptein kaptein toth manikowski martire e4166 yogo showalter wolfson wineinger uddin rubenson 669e661 ruddox norum stokke edvardsen otterstad s12880 rosborough jcu siqueira moises razi ghani s10840 jenq panebianco dyspneic kircher himelman holmen pryor nakao ransil caval capomolla febo caporotondi sfx107 zoccali tripepi gargani sicari lust siriopol hogas voroneanu bioimpedance gft260 lust lust nct02310061 show_desc desc meziere s001340050771 zanobetti scorpiniti gigli prosen klemen strnad grmec r114 cc10140 laursen sloth mailhot mandavia lll aioui acoe aium bindman aubin bailitz nejmoa1404446 perloff ramani mehra e154 steinwandel rippey towell rosman jorc ishibe peixoto intercompartmental 139x kersh kronfield popper uremia nejm197403212901203 mullangi jaar intradialytic assimon flythe doqi s137 s0272 robbin lockhart kdoqi s322 doqi doqi daulaire e1651 kimberly marill schlesinger kem058 rope merell e7269 bahner royall hoppmann poston iusc s13089 hoppmann iusc s13089 molitoris molitoris mattana warburton negoianu maursetter','Abstract Point of care ultrasound (POCUS) is rapidly emerging as a bedside diagnostic tool that can enhance physical diagnosis and facilitate clinical decision-making. Although ultrasound is widely used by nephrologists for vascular access and kidney imaging, diagnostic POCUS skills in other anatomic areas are not part of routine nephrology training. In this narrative review, we will provide an overview of selected POCUS techniques, highlight potential uses of POCUS in routine nephrology practice, and describe a new curriculum implemented at Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine to teach diagnostic POCUS skills to nephrology fellows. “ Medicine is a science of uncertainty and an art of probability .” Sir William Osler Physical examination is at the core of patient assessment and diagnostic decision making. This component of clinical evaluation that begins with history-taking while observing patient demeanor, discomfort and non-verbal communication. Physical examination uses the examiner’s sensory perception (tactile, auditory, optic and olfactory) enhanced by point-of-care diagnostic tools that improve the objectivity of assessment, providing real-time data that are integrated by the clinician at the bedside for understanding the pathophysiology, making diagnostic decisions and engaging the patients in understanding their disease process. Many point-of-care diagnostic tools are in routine use including the stethoscope, weighing scale, sphygmomanometer, thermometer, pulse oximeter, otoscope, and ophthalmoscope. Improving the bedside clinical evaluation will require that the physician maintains the sensory perception skills while innovations ensure that the data provided by the point-of-care diagnostic tools are accurate, precise and reproducible, improving real-time diagnostic and management capabilities while simultaneously fortifying the physician-patient connection. These advances in clinical assessment are needed to improve care of all patients, including those managed by nephrologists. The stethoscope, a point-of-care device invented by Laennec over 200 years ago, was a technological advancement that radically changed our understanding of pathophysiology of disease and the practice of medicine 1 . The success of the stethoscope as a point-of-care device comes from its versatility and ease of use which allows for diagnosis and real-time monitoring of disease processes 2 . Recent technological advances have allowed for development of many new point-of-care devices. Of these, point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) has great promise to enhance the physical exam by providing clinically relevant information in a fast and efficient manner. POCUS is in widespread use in nephrology for procedures, such as temporary dialysis catheter placement. However, diagnostic POCUS is vastly underutilized and is an opportunity to improve the care of patients with kidney disease 3 – 5 . In the following sections, we will provide an overview of selected POCUS techniques, highlight potential uses of POCUS in routine nephrology practice, and describe a new curriculum implemented at Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine to teach diagnostic POCUS skills to nephrology fellows. Selected POCUS techniques that are useful for nephrologists POCUS can be used for formal ultrasound study or for more focused diagnostic imaging to augment physical examination. The following are the key focused diagnostic ultrasound skills that are easily taught and can contribute to improving care of patients seen in routine nephrology practice. Focused Cardiac Ultrasound The heart can be imaged from a number of different angles. The parasternal long-axis (PLAX) view takes less than 5 minutes to perform and can quickly provide valuable diagnostic information. The ultrasound probe is placed along the left sternal border at approximately the 4 th intercostal space, perpendicular to the patient’s chest with the probe index marker angled toward the patient’s right shoulder 6 . This view allows visualization of the left atrium emptying into the left ventricle, the mitral valve flapping toward the interventricular septum, and the right ventricle as well as the surrounding pericardium and descending aorta ( Figure 1 ). Using PLAX view, pericardial and pleural effusions are rapidly identified as dark black pockets of fluid around the heart. Left ventricular ejection fraction (EF) can be quantified using the mitral valve E point-septal separation (EPSS) method, which correlates the distance between the anterior mitral valve and the interventricular septum, with an estimated EF 7 . The greater the distance, the lower the ejection fraction – a distance of 1 cm or more corresponds to an EF of less than 45% – providing a quick bedside assessment of EF. POCUS cardiac image quality is comparable to the transthoracic echocardiography 8 . In previous diagnostic accuracy studies, compared to formal echocardiograms read by cardiologists, ejection fraction assessment using POCUS by non-cardiologists was highly reliable 7 . 9 – 13 Figure 1. Selected ultrasound images obtained using POCUS. Open in a new tab A) Cardiac parasternal long-axis view (Abbreviations: RV, right ventricle; LV, left ventricle; AO, Aorta; LA, left atrium; Note: left atrium is enlarged) B) Inferior vena cava from sub-costal view (Abbreviations: IVC, inferior vena cava; Note: wide and dilated IVC) C) Lung view demonstrating B lines D) Kidney longitudinal view (Note: significant ascites is also present) E) Bladder transverse view F) Dialysis fistula (Note: vertical diameter 1.16 cms) Inferior Vena Cava (IVC) Ultrasound The IVC is imaged from a subcostal view, inferior to the xyphoid process, with the probe flat on the patient’s abdomen ( Figure 1 ). After locating the right atrium and keeping an eye on its wall, the probe is rotated to see the junction of the IVC and the right atrium and the long-axis of the IVC. At approximately 2 centimeters from the IVC-right atrium junction, the IVC diameter and collapsibility during inspiration is assessed. The IVC diameter and collapsibility can provide an estimate of the central venous pressure (CVP) – a wide (>2.5 centimeters) IVC with poor to no collapsibility (“fixed and dilated IVC”) generally indicates that the CVP is higher than 15–20 mm Hg whereas a small IVC (<1.5) with >50% collapse during inspiration (“flat IVC”) is indicative of CVP less than 5 mm Hg. 14 – 18 In a number of studies, POCUS evaluation of IVC was highly reliable in estimating CVP 7 . Focused Lung Ultrasound A focused lung ultrasound can quickly allow assessment of pulmonary edema, pleural effusion and pneumothorax. The lungs are imaged by scanning the chest bilaterally anteriorly (mid-clavicular), laterally (mid-axillary), and posteriorly (mid-scapular). Pleural effusions are easily identified as dark black mirror images of the liver or spleen at the interface between the diaphragm and lung. Pulmonary edema can be identified and quantified by B-lines, also known as lung “comets” or “rockets” ( Figure 1 ). B-lines are repeated reflections of ultrasound waves within lung parenchyma as a result of alveolar edema 19 . B-line counts can allow serial monitoring of patients with pulmonary edema. Higher B-line counts in dialysis patients are associated with higher risk of death 20 . B-lines can also detect pulmonary edema in dialysis patients who may otherwise be considered euvolemic by routine physical examination 21 , 22 . The ongoing Lung Water by Ultrasound Guided Treatment in Hemodialysis Patients (LUST) trial is testing the hypothesis that target weight setting in hemodialysis patients by using POCUS guided lung water measurement (B-line score) will prevent cardiovascular outcomes (including death, myocardial infarction, left ventricular dysfunction, hospitalizations) 23 . In the emergency department, B-lines on POCUS can rapidly differentiate between different causes of dyspnea, shortening the time between evaluation and treatment 24 – 27 . Focused Kidney Ultrasound POCUS can allow rapid evaluation of the kidneys in the outpatient or the inpatient setting. The kidneys are visualized through the lower lateral ribs at the midaxillary line in the supine position or at the costovertebral angle in the prone position ( Figure 1 ). Transhepatic view of the right kidney allows determination of renal echogenicity by comparing it to normal liver. The longitudinal view of the kidney allows measurement of kidney length and can quickly identify marked hydronephrosis. Transverse views of the kidney can further delineate hydronephrosis and hydroureter. Focused Bladder Ultrasound Bladder ultrasound is one of the easiest skills to master and can rapidly identify patients with bladder outlet obstruction and urinary retention. The bladder is imaged with the ultrasound probe placed above the symphysis pubis, directed caudally toward the prostate or cervix. By measuring the maximum transverse and longitudinal bladder diameters, bladder volume is calculated using the following equation: Volume (mL) = length (cm) * width (cm) * height (cm) * (0.52) 28 . Many POCUS devices can automatically calculate the bladder volume by using these measurements. Bladder POCUS can detect urinary retention, avoiding unnecessary ultrasounds for post-void residual in the outpatient setting and reduce the need for bladder catheterization and its subsequent complications in settings of acute kidney injury and critical illness. Focused Dialysis Fistula Ultrasound The fistula is imaged by placing the ultrasound probe on the fistula. The cross-section of the fistula appears as a hollow dark black circle ( Figure 1 ). The width of the fistula, the distance between the surface and fistula (depth) and the blood flow rate can be assessed using POCUS. Integration of POCUS in Bedside Diagnostic Decision-Making Diagnostic POCUS has been most widely used and systematically evaluated in emergency department and critical care settings. There are a number of established POCUS protocols in use in these settings. The RUSH (Rapid Ultrasound in Shock) protocol is designed to distinguish between classic shock states. Focused on the heart and its function, RUSH evaluates patients for cardiac contractility, pericardial effusion, tamponade, IVC maximum diameter, and IVC collapsibility to categorize shock as hypovolemic, cardiogenic, obstructive, or distributive 29 . The BLUE (Bedside Lung Ultrasound in Emergency) can rapidly distinguish causes of acute respiratory failure, and the FALLS (Fluid Administration Limited by Lung Sonography) protocol provides an ultrasound guided approach to acute circulatory failure 30 . The FAST (Focused Assessment with Sonography for Trauma) and the Extended FAST (E-FAST) protocol assess free fluid in pleural, peritoneal, and pericardial spaces following blunt trauma (FAST) as well as hemothorax and pneumothorax (E-FAST). The use of FAST protocols is supported by guidelines from the American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (AIUM) and the American College of Emergency Physicians (ACEP) 31 . POCUS has also been rigorously evaluated in the emergency department setting for patients presenting with suspected nephrolithiasis. Routine use of ultrasonography in these patients significantly reduced the need for CT scanning, lowering radiation exposure without any increase in risk for nephrolithiasis-related complications 32 . Diagnostic POCUS for Bedside Decision-Making in Nephrology Patients with kidney disease often have many other comorbidities that influence overall patient management. In the following sections, we will discuss specific clinical situations in dialysis patients where POCUS can be helpful by providing bedside diagnostic information that can enhance clinical decision making. We will also briefly highlight other potential uses of POCUS in routine non-dialysis nephrology practice. Shortness of breath in a dialysis patient: Is this volume overload? Shortness of breath is a common symptom in hospitalized dialysis patients. In the setting of missed dialysis and clinical signs of volume overload, such as peripheral edema, elevated jugular venous pressure, and end-inspiratory crackles or X-ray findings of pulmonary edema, the diagnosis can be quite clear. However, not uncommonly, pedal edema may be due to venous insufficiency, jugular venous pressure may be difficult to assess, and lung exam or X-ray may be equivocal. Absence of both crackles and chest X-ray findings of pulmonary edema are also well-described in patients with chronic elevation of left ventricular end-diastolic pressure and are thought to be due to the enlargement of pulmonary lymphatics which prevent alveolar edema despite elevated left ventricular filling pressure 33 – 35 . In dialysis patients with suspected volume overload, POCUS can provide valuable information. IVC POCUS can provide objective data on right sided filling pressures (IVC diameter and collapsibility). Lung POCUS can identify pulmonary edema (B lines) and identify additional pathology such as pleural effusion that may be contributing to dyspnea 5 . Cardiac POCUS may identify low ejection fraction or valvular abnormalities. Dialysis patient with hypotension Hypotension is common in dialysis patients and can present in a variety of settings. In dialysis patients presenting with unexplained hypotension, cardiac tamponade, myocardial dysfunction, and effective circulating volume depletion is often in the differential and POCUS can rapidly provide valuable diagnostic information. Similar to the RUSH protocol, POCUS can quickly identify pericardial effusion or tamponade and/or low ejection fraction. IVC examination can provide further diagnostic information on effective circulating volume. In the setting of intradialytic hypotension, serial POCUS evaluation of IVC may identify patients where hypotension is a result of decreased effective circulating volume (“flat” IVC) 5 , 36 , 37 . Such patients may still have extravascular volume expansion and may require longer dialysis time or more frequent dialysis sessions. On the other hand, patients with intradialytic hypotension, normal ejection fraction and dilated IVC may develop intradialytic hypotension due to autonomic insufficiency with peripheral vasodilation 38 . These patients more likely to respond to α-adrenergic agonists such as midodrine 39 . Target weight in dialysis patients Identifying target weight for dialysis patients is challenging. Overestimation of target weight contributes to persistent volume overload and its sequelae whereas underestimation of target weight can cause intradialytic hypotension and its associated complications. POCUS can help identify patients with occult volume overload. As the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid (CMS) proposes new quality performance measures for ultrafiltration rate thresholds for dialysis patients due to the associations of higher mortality 40 , POCUS may serve as an invaluable tool to assist in appropriate assignment and adjustment of target weight. The ongoing Lung Water by Ultrasound Guided Treatment in Hemodialysis Patients (LUST) trial is currently testing this technique for volume management in hemodialysis patients 23 . Persistence of lung B lines when the patient is at target weight may suggest persistent volume overload which may improve with targeting a lower weight. Is this dialysis fistula ready for use? In hemodialysis patients with a new fistula, there is often uncertainty if the fistula is ready for use. The K/DOQI guidelines recommend that a fistula can be assessed using the “rule of sixes” – it should be <6 mm deep, >6 mm wide, and have a blood flow rate >600 ml/min 41 – 43 . POCUS can quickly provide measurements of depth and width of the fistula, providing objective data to monitor fistula maturation, communicate with vascular surgeons, and make decisions regarding cannulation. Asymptomatic left ventricular dysfunction in dialysis patients Left ventricular dysfunction is common in dialysis patients and reduced systolic function is associated with a high risk of death. The National Kidney Foundation’s Kidney Disease Outcome Quality Initiative (K/DOQI) guidelines recommend routine screening for reduced systolic function at dialysis initiation, after 3 years of dialysis, or if there are new symptoms suggesting left ventricular dysfunction 44 . POCUS of the heart can provide quick assessment of ejection fraction and may allow early detection of new systolic heart failure. Cardiorenal syndrome: Is this patient volume overloaded or depleted? Managing patients with heart failure and acute kidney injury (AKI) is challenging as effective circulating volume can be difficult to assess. POCUS evaluation of the lungs (B lines) and IVC can provide objective data that can influence decisions to diurese or to stop diuresis 5 . Patients presenting with uremia A common scenario in nephrology practice is the presentation of a patient to an emergency department, without any known past history of kidney disease and laboratory data demonstrating elevated urea and creatinine. A POCUS evaluation of the kidney can provide useful diagnostic information in this scenario. Hydronephrosis can be quickly excluded and renal size and echogenicity may provide information on the chronicity of kidney disease. Bladder ultrasound can rapidly provide information on bladder outlet obstruction and potentially avoid unnecessary urinary catheterization and associated complications. Hypertensive crisis: Does this patient have papilledema and malignant hypertension? Assessment of optic fundus for papilledema is an important part of evaluating patients with severe hypertension. POCUS evaluation of the eye can allow assessment of intracranial pressure – with severe elevation of intracranial pressure, echolucent areas can be seen in the optic nerve sheath (the “crescent” sign) 45 , 46 . The severity of papilledema can also be quantified. Acute joint pain and swelling: Is this gout or pseudogout? Gout and pseudogout are common in patients with kidney disease. Accurate diagnosis and differentiating between these two entities is essential for prevention of acute attacks and to avoid complications caused by uric acid lowering therapy. POCUS evaluation of joints can identify monosodium urate crystals on cartilage and tophi and can help with diagnostic decision making 47 . POCUS Education Nephrology fellows nationwide have identified that increased training in ultrasonography is a desired element of their curricula 48 . For POCUS to be incorporated into clinical care on a nation-wide level, educational curriculum and training programs need to be designed, tested, and integrated into physician training. Currently, many institutions have developed individualized programs; however, to standardize care and treatment, curricula must eventually be accepted and promoted on a national scale. Nephrology has not yet embraced any one educational curriculum as addressing a majority of encountered clinical dilemmas. However, other specialties, including emergency medicine and critical care 25 – 27 , 29 – 32 , 49 , have started to adapt focused protocols and test their educational curricula. Internal medicine and medical schools 50 – 53 are also starting to incorporate ultrasound basics to provide rising medical professionals exposure to ultrasound early in their training. POCUS education for nephrology fellows is being offered by some nephrology training programs but there are no competency guidelines provided by the American Board of Internal Medicine. The Johns Hopkins Nephrology POCUS Curriculum The Johns Hopkins Nephrology POCUS curriculum is designed to teach focused diagnostic POCUS skills to nephrology fellows. The goal of the curriculum is to provide nephrology fellows with basic ultrasound knowledge and develop skills to use POCUS for volume assessment and selective renal pathologies. The curriculum is designed to teach focused ultrasound skills to all fellows with options for advanced training in each area. The skills taught in the course are described in Figure 2 and Appendix 1 . The course is taught by an experienced ultrasonographer (CM). The curriculum includes a combination of didactic sessions and hands-on image acquisition ( Figure 3 ). The didactic sessions describe the image acquisition process and image interpretation for each selected area. The didactic session is followed by an image interpretation test which must be successfully completed (score ≥90%) before continuing with image acquisition. Knowledge and interpretation of what is being imaged improves the learner’s engagement in the image acquisition process. Image acquisition training is comprised of guided sessions followed by independent scanning. All independent images are interpreted by the learner and verified by the teacher. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Johns Hopkins Nephrology fellowship POCUS curriculum Figure 3. Open in a new tab POCUS Curriculum Administration Assessment of the curriculum, experience, and skills is performed across multiple levels ( Figure 4 ). At the end of each module, each learner’s competency is evaluated using a POCUS Objective Structured Clinical Evaluation (POCUS-OSCE; Appendix 2 ). At the end of the course, each learner assesses the ease, utility, and skills developed in POCUS using a survey ( Appendix 3 ). Learners are also provided a “Certificate of Completion”. The curriculum is modular – it is implemented in a 2-week elective block but can also be implemented one module at a time over a longer time period. Figure 4. Open in a new tab Assessment of the curriculum, experience, and skills across multiple domains Positioning of POCUS Training in the Nephrology Landscape Interest in nephrology as a career has seen a decline in interest 54 with trainees having different career interests compared to years past 55 . Studies have shown that the top reasons for residents not choosing nephrology are the belief that patients with end-stage renal disease were too complicated, the lack of a mentor, and that there are insufficient procedures in nephrology 56 . Recent innovations in nephrology curricula have focused on altering the inpatient experience 57 , 58 , and providing additional training in physiology 59 . Adding novel procedural and diagnostic aspects to a curriculum, including POCUS, may increase both the flexibility in skills for fellow trainees, and also combat the perceptions of what nephrology has to offer in a career. These experiences could be elective or required elements, as programs need to continue to demonstrate competency in the core aspects of nephrology 60 . Formal ultrasound training also has the capability of adding items for reimbursement for nephrologists. The American Society for Diagnostic and Interventional Nephrology ( www.asdin.org ) offers pathways for certification for kidney and bladder ultrasound – expansion of this program with pathways for certification in other areas can expand the potential for billing services for nephrologists. Conclusion Diagnostic POCUS has a substantial potential to advance the practice of nephrology and improve patient care, particularly in the management of volume overload and acute kidney injury – two of the most common problems encountered in routine nephrology practice. Although we may never truly shift medicine away from being a “science of uncertainty,” with more accurate diagnoses and focused treatments, we can better master the “art of probability” and provide state-of-the-art care to our patients. Acknowledgments We would like to thank Dr. David Hellmann, Chairman, Department of Medicine at Johns Hopkins Bayview Medical Center, for scientific feedback and institutional commitment to the project. Support This work was supported by the Johns Hopkins Center for Innovative Medicine. TS and CM were supported by Johns Hopkins Center for Innovative Medicine. TS is 