x) Appendix 2: Point-of-Care Ultrasound Objective Structured Clinical Evaluation (POCUS-OSCE) Appendix 3: Renal Fellows Point-of-Care Ultrasound Pretest/Posttest References 1. Markel H. The stethoscope and the art of listening. N Engl J Med. 2006;354(6):551–553. doi: 10.1056/NEJMp048251. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Edelman ER, Weber BN. Tenuous Tether. N Engl J Med. 2015;373(23):2199–2201. doi: 10.1056/NEJMp1509265. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Kaptein MJ, Kaptein EM. Focused Real-Time Ultrasonography for Nephrologists. Int J Nephrol. 2017;2017:3756857. doi: 10.1155/2017/3756857. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Ross DW, Barnett RL, Shah HH. Lung ultrasonography: a novel clinical tool to consider in nephrology. Kidney Int. 2016;89(3):720–721. doi: 10.1016/j.kint.2015.09.005. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Winters KD, Toth-Manikowski S, Martire C, Shafi T. Hand-carried ultrasound use in clinical nephrology: Case report. Medicine (Baltimore) 2016;95(30):e4166. doi: 10.1097/MD.0000000000004166. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Kimura BJ, Yogo N, O’Connell CW, Phan JN, Showalter BK, Wolfson T. Cardiopulmonary limited ultrasound examination for “quick-look” bedside application. Am J Cardiol. 2011;108(4):586–590. doi: 10.1016/j.amjcard.2011.03.091. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Kimura BJ. Point-of-care cardiac ultrasound techniques in the physical examination: better at the bedside. Heart. 2017;103(13):987–994. doi: 10.1136/heartjnl-2016-309915. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Khan HA, Wineinger NE, Uddin PQ, Mehta HS, Rubenson DS, Topol EJ. Can hospital rounds with pocket ultrasound by cardiologists reduce standard echocardiography? Am J Med. 2014;127(7):669e661–667. doi: 10.1016/j.amjmed.2014.03.015. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Ruddox V, Norum IB, Stokke TM, Edvardsen T, Otterstad JE. Focused cardiac ultrasound by unselected 