 wille cvvhdf nedergaard oxlund wian ytrebo nonsedation stucker ponte tataw wozniak pugin saudan acker flick bernardini lins elseviers niepen hoste malbrain lamontagne sadique jamale hase pradeep keskar jawale grp fsts lumlertgul peerapornratana trakarnvanich pongsittisak surasit fst thanapongsatorn chaikomon lumlertgul yimsangyad leewongworasingh kulvichit louw uehlinger eichelberger lassnigg donner grubhofer presterl druml hiesmayr renoprotective shashaty testani aqeel borovskiy ellenberg wiedemann haiberger pezzella garisto favia cogo ethacrynic sward valsson odencrants samuelsson ricksten srisawat tungsanga lumlertgul komaenthammasophon peerapornratana thamrongsat polymyxin hemoperfusion cathedia parienti thirion megarbane souweine ouchikhe ctg mackle parke haren fmp shotwell siemann acorn soh zarbock aken wempe endre frampton earlyarf jayaram emberson sankar muralidharan lalitha rameshkumar e2351710 lomivorotov kornilov boboshko shmyrev bondarenko soynov luciano favia garisto muraca fenoldopam gelatinase lipocaline r160 pesonen vlasov suojaranta hiippala schramko wilkman ringer ranucci castelvecchio biondi vincentiis vincentiis ballotta grp perner guttormsen tenhunen ringer jayashree nallasamy dayal rawat lyte spink rocco fenoldopam fielitz reade morgera seevenayagam sisillo ceriani bortone juliano salvi veglia fielitz devarajan matalanis martinet boulain philippart poussel maizel hydroxyethylstarch crystmas r94 kolodner fenoldopam magder varennes doucette fergusson colloids marenzi marana lauri assanelli grazi campodonico radiocontrast mattke schibler trnka splyt axelrod anglemyer aminophylline hiu coulthard holstein crosier utrafiltration hem kwiatkowski dls krawczeski regist rtd chandramouli allocco wassef desmarais remillard labrosse gravel e004713 modra vithanage abeygunawardana todorov arslani bengs goliasch amacher gebert grzonka lohri sabur juurlink hladunewich blecha zeman lydia pfefferle placek karagiannidis bein demirjian huml bakaeen poggio geube lcy dailey kroll desrosiers banigan upham dashtestani skiby pentel e43824 chander luhana sadarat leys parkash vranas slatore kerlin lodhi kibret mangalgi schiebinger macaluso lariviere soranno bora lohr bagga e2022060841 hladunewich odutayo cornelis pierratos heidari babor tort curno sager seidler maston bista e2444187 gires pewresearch mauvais berthold campesi carrero dakal franconi nicolson hammons lyn franson kuk whitley lindsey weckman naggayi soranno batte f304 lagosky rochon emulation stey ghneim gurney rattan abahuje progres e0002227 12916_2025_4202_moesm1_esm','Abstract Background How sex and gender are considered in randomized controlled trials (RCTs) in critical care nephrology is unclear. We aimed to perform a meta-epidemiologic study to describe the representation, sex and gender reporting, and sex- and gender-based analyses (SGBA) in high-impact RCTs in critical care nephrology. Methods We searched the Web of Science Core Collection for critical care nephrology papers from 2000 to 2024. We included trials published in high-impact journals in general medicine, pediatrics, critical care, and nephrology. The main outcome was the proportion of female/woman participants enrolled and the association with trial characteristics, findings, and women authorship. We estimated the participation-to-prevalence ratio (PPR) to evaluate the representativeness of females within identified RCTs and selected case-mix and disease populations. Sex and gender reporting and SGBA were investigated. Results A total of 117 RCTs, including 106,057 participants, were included. Sex (54.7%), gender (26.5%), both (2.6%), or none (16.2%) terminology were used for reporting. Male/female (82.1%), men/women (11.1%), both (4.3%), boys/girls (0.9%) and none (1.7%) were used as descriptors. Of the 115 RCTs with available sex/gender data, the median proportion of female/women participants was 35.4% (interquartile range (IQR) 31.2%–40.8%). Pediatric age group and process of care as an intervention were independently associated with the proportion of female/women participants. The median PPR was 0.89 (IQR 0.8–1.06), except in major surgery, for which PPR was 0.67 (IQR 0.29–0.73). Twelve (10.9%) and 49 (41.9%) studies used sex and/or gender as inclusion and exclusion criteria, respectively; 5 (4.3%) studies used sex/gender-stratified randomization; and 35 (29.9%) studies performed SGBA. RCTs with pregnancy, lactation, or women of childbearing age as exclusion criteria had a lower enrollment of female/women participants than RCTs that did not (33.6% vs. 36.8%, P = 0.04). Exclusion criteria of pregnancy, lactation, or childbearing age were considered strongly justified, potentially justified, and poorly justified in 36.1%, 14.9%, and 48.9%, respectively. There were no changes in the representation of females/women and SGBA across identified RCTs over the search range. Conclusions Females/women are less frequently represented in critical care nephrology RCTs. Significant gaps exist in sex- and gender-specific eligibility criteria, reporting, and analysis. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12916-025-04202-y. Keywords: Sex, Gender, Critical care nephrology, Randomized controlled trial Background Sex as a biological variable (SABV) and gender differences are increasingly recognized for their association with disease manifestation, provision of medical care, and outcomes [ 1 ]. Sex is defined by genetics, cells, and molecules and manifests differently by anatomy, physiology, and sex hormones. Gender refers to socially constructed norms, behaviors, expressions, and identities. These anatomical and physiological sex differences may lead to variations in the development and outcomes of various diseases, including acute kidney injury (AKI) [ 2 ]. In animal studies, females are less susceptible to AKI than males [ 3 ]. However, clinical studies have shown discordant evidence of the impact of sex differences on risks and outcomes of AKI [ 4 – 11 ]. Gender roles may also influence access to healthcare and receipt of therapies, including renal replacement therapy (RRT) and outcomes [ 12 , 13 ]. Despite increased awareness and efforts from funding and regulatory agencies to ensure appropriate sex-and gender-based analysis (SGBA) and reporting are followed, significant barriers remain [ 14 – 17 ]. Both SABV and factors related to gender may influence eligibility criteria and participation in clinical trials. Females/women are under-represented in trials of chronic diseases (e.g., cardiovascular diseases and kidney transplantation) [ 18 – 21 ]. In critical care nephrology studies, it is unclear how sex and gender are considered in the design, conduct, analysis, and reporting of randomized controlled trials (RCTs). Therefore, we aimed to conduct a meta-epidemiologic study to explore how sex and gender concepts are considered in RCTs in critical care nephrology. Methods Design This was a meta-epidemiologic study of RCTs in critical care nephrology published in high-impact general medicine, nephrology, critical care, and pediatric journals. Research ethics board approval was not required, as all data included in this evaluation are published and in the public domain. We prospectively registered the protocol on Open Science Framework ( https://doi.org/ 10.17605/OSF.IO/EX34D). Search strategy We searched the Web of Science Core Collection for critical care nephrology papers from 2000–2024. The search strategy was developed in consultation with a research librarian (JK) (Additional File 1: Methods) [ 22 ]. The search was filtered to include English-language-only articles published between January 1, 2000, and August 8, 2024. We included articles from the highest-impact general medicine journals (i.e., Lancet, the New England Journal of Medicine [NEJM], Journal of the American Medical Association [JAMA], British Medical Journal [BMJ], and the Annals of Internal Medicine [AIM]), intensive care medicine journals (Lancet Respiratory Medicine, Intensive Care Med [ICM], American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine [AJRCCM], Critical Care, and Critical Care Medicine [CCM]), nephrology journals (Kidney International [KI], the Journal of the American Society of Nephrology [JASN], American Journal of Kidney Diseases [AJKD], Clinical Journal of the American Society of Nephrology [CJASN], and Nephrology Dialysis Transplantation [NDT]), and pediatric journals (Pediatrics [Pediatr], JAMA Pediatrics [JAMA Pediatr], Lancet Child and Adolescent Health [Lancet Child Adolesc Health], Pediatric Nephrology [Pediatr Nephrol] and Pediatric Critical Care Medicine [Pediatr Crit Care Med]). We selected the top 5 highest impact journals across domains at the time of publication by use of www.scijournal.org and by consensus of the investigative team. Article selection We included RCTs in adult and pediatric populations. We excluded trials with < 50 participants (only for adult trials) and studies that were not RCTs (i.e., observational studies; meta-analyses; narrative reviews; case reports or series; consensus or clinical practice guidelines; experimental [non-human] studies; secondary or post-hoc analyses of randomized trials; protocols for randomized trials). The studies were uploaded in Covidence. The initial screening of titles and abstracts of retrieved articles was independently performed by two reviewers (NL and AW) to verify eligibility and suitability for critical care nephrology. The second screening of articles selected from the first screen involved title, abstract, and full-text review by the same reviewers to verify eligibility. Conflicts were resolved by consensus or adjudication by the third reviewer (SMB). Data extraction For each included article, we extracted the following details: the full title, author list, journal type, journal name, the digital object identifier (DOI), the year of publication, and the total number of citations, study design, number of centers, blinding, country and region of enrollment, randomized numbers of participants, funding, types of intervention, disease population, comparator, follow-up duration, primary outcome and results, nomenclature (sex or gender in the heading) and descriptors of sex or gender (male/female, men/women, boys/girls), numbers and proportions of male and female (if only male/female and men/women participants were reported, the remaining participants were assumed to be of the other sex or gender identity), sex/gender-based eligibility inclusion criteria/exclusion criteria (e.g., pregnancy, lactating, contraception), eligibility criteria that may have different reference ranges by sex/gender (e.g., hemoglobin, creatinine, cardiac biomarker), sex/gender-based stratification, and statistical analysis. Sex- and gender-based analysis (SGBA) was defined in this study as sex-aggregated analysis, subgroup analysis, or covariate adjustment by sex/gender [ 23 ]. Author names, including shared authorship, were obtained. Gender was investigated for all authors. The gender of the authors was determined from photographs (if available) and/or pronoun descriptors via a web search of academic sites (e.g., ResearchGate or LinkedIn), university or hospital websites, or social media websites. Genderize.io was further utilized to verify the gender of authors [ 24 ]. Any discrepancies were resolved by consensus. Exclusion criteria were adjudicated as poorly justified, potentially justified, or strongly justified in accordance with previous literature [ 25 ]. Exclusion criteria were defined as strongly justified if there was a risk to patient or fetal safety. Poorly justified exclusion criteria included any criterion that was not strongly justifiable according to existing studies and/or the United States Food and Drug Administration database [ 26 ]. Potentially justified exclusion criteria were neither strongly nor poorly justified. Statistical analysis Descriptive statistical methods were used to report n (%), mean ± SD, median (interquartile range, IQR). Proportions of female/women participants are described according to journal types, enrollment sites, trial characteristics, publication year and citation data, age group, disease population, intervention type, and gender authorship. Meta-analytic estimates of the proportion of participants were performed using the metaprop command in Stata Version 18.5 (College Station, Texas, USA) [ 27 ]. Pooled effect size estimates were calculated using random effects models with restricted maximum likelihood as the heterogeneity estimator. Confidence intervals for the binomial proportion were calculated using exact or Clopper–Pearson methods. We additionally performed a priori planned subgroup analyses according to publication date, enrollment site, age group, types of intervention, disease conditions, and trial leadership. Trial leadership was defined by evidence of women as either first or senior/corresponding authors. We used a random effects model to further explore heterogeneity and performed meta-regression to explore the association between these factors and females/women enrollment. Multivariable logistic regression was performed to identify factors which were associated with SGBA by using a priori specified covariates i.e. year of publication, region of enrollment, trial characteristics, funding, types of intervention, condition, number of participants, and trial leadership. To investigate the extent of women’s representation among participants in trials, we calculated the participation-to-prevalence ratio (PPR) , defined as the percentage of women among trial participants divided by the percentage of women in the underlying disease population [ 28 ]. A PPR between 0.8–1.2 indicates that the sex composition of the trial is representative of the disease population [ 29 ]. A PPR < 0.8 indicates underrepresentation, and a PPR > 1.2 indicates overrepresentation. The percentage of female/women within the population was obtained from prevalence estimates from the Global Burden of Disease [ 30 ], if available. Where trials were conducted across multiple countries, regionally or globally (if more than one region), prevalence estimates were assigned to available multinational databases or registries. Where trials were conducted in a single country, nationwide prevalence estimates or data from multicenter registries were used. If more than one reference was available, we used the reference with the publication date closest to when the trials were conducted. PPR was estimated overall and compared among age group, region of enrollment, type of intervention, disease condition, and year of publication by using the Kruskal–Wallis test. To assess whether PPR varied by study sample size, we calculated a sample size weighted mean (SSWM) by multiplying the trial PPR by the trial sample size and dividing by the sum of participants in all trials included in this study. The sum of this quantity is the SSWM. Bootstrap methods were used to obtain 95% confidence intervals (CIs) for the mean PPR and SSWM, using the percentile method with 100,000 iterations. Trends of PPR over time were displayed overall by the number of participants and mean PPR and assessed using the Kruskal–Wallis and Jonckheere-Terpstra proportion trend tests. We performed a complete case analysis. Statistical analysis was performed using Stata version 18 (StataCorp, College Station, TX). P < 0.05 was considered significant. Results Of the 970 eligible articles, 117 RCTs comprising 106,057 participants were included ( Fig. 1 ). A total of 115 studies had available sex/gender data. The majority were published in critical care journals ( n = 46 [40.0%]), followed by general medicine journals ( n = 43 [37.4%]), nephrology journals ( n = 24 [20.9%]), and pediatric journals ( n = 2 [1.7%]) (Additional File 1: Table S1). Almost all RCTs focused on adult populations ( n = 107 [93.0%]), with a median size of 212 (IQR 114, 710) participants. The overall proportion of women authors was 23.8% (IQR 16%–33.3%), and 110 (95.7%) studies had women authors constituting less than 50% of the total authors. The proportion of studies with women as first, senior, or corresponding authors was 33.0% (Table 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab PRISMA flow diagram Table 1. Characteristics of included studies and proportions of female/women participants Characteristics No. of studies (%) No. of total participants ( N = 106,057) No. of female/women participants ( N = 39,890) Female/women proportion of participants, median (IQR), % Total 115 106,057 39,890 35.4 (31.2–40.8) Journal type Critical care 46 (40.0) 10,419 3825 35.9 (32.2–40.6) General medicine 43 (37.4) 91,304 34,419 35.4 (29.4–39.6) General pediatrics 2 (1.7) 589 275 41.9 (35.6–48.3) Nephrology 24 (20.9) 3745 1371 33.1 (31.1–42.0) Year 2000–2005 14 (12.2) 3506 1126 31.5 (28.8–40.6) 2006–2010 22 (19.1) 7419 2706 36.0 (31–42) 2011–2015 28 (24.4) 21,319 7764 34.6 (32.1–39.0) 2016–2020 29 (25.2) 36,687 13,945 35.6 (32.2–42.4) 2021–2024 22 (19.1) 37,126 14,349 36.7 (31.0–42.7) Trial characteristics Region Multiregional 10 (8.7) 10,638 3424 35.3 (31.9–37.5) Asia 11 (9.6) 4090 1403 34.9 (29.3–50) Europe 58 (50.4) 31,683 10,748 34.2 (31.0–38.3) Latin and South America 2 (1.7) 10,640 4692 37.5 (30.8–44.2) North America 21 (18.3) 30,361 12,446 40.7 (32.5–43.9) Oceania 13 (11.3) 18,645 7177 38.7 (35.4–43.8) Number of centers 1 50 (43.5) 43,769 13,617 35.5 (31.7–42.7) > 1 65 (56.5) 71,288 26,273 35.4 (31.0–39.5) Study design Parallel 112 (97.4) 89,184 32,690 35.1 (31.1–40.6) Cluster 3 (2.6) 16,873 7200 42.4 (36.1–47.2) Comparator Active 61 (53.0) 77,923 30,187 35.4 (32.0–40.6) Placebo 30 (26.1) 12,984 4298 36.8 (31.7–43.8) Usual care 24 (20.9) 15,150 5405 34.1 (27.7–39.6) Blinding Blinded 49 (42.6) 47,746 17,946 36.5 (31.9–41.9) Open label 66 (57.4) 58,237 21,944 34.8 (31.0–39.4) Funding Academic/public 59 (51.3) 78,287 30,570 36.1 (31.2–42.4) 