cations with available 2-year citation data ** 91 publications with available 5-year citation data a Acute respiratory distress syndrome, hospitalized patients, diabetic ketoacidosis, AKI survivors, percutaneous coronary interventions, liver transplantation Sex representation in enrollment and RCT characteristics The median proportion of female/women participants was 35.4% (IQR 31.2%–40.8%) (male/man:female/woman = 1.85:1), ranging from 11.7% to 52.8% across studies. The proportion was highest in North America (median 40.7% [IQR 32.5%–43.9%]), in the pediatric population (median 43.8% [IQR 36.1%–50%]), and in cluster RCTs (median 42.4% [IQR 36.1%–47.2%]). The proportion was comparable across publication years, number of centers, comparator, blinding, types of intervention, follow-up duration, results of the primary outcome, female authorship, and citation numbers. Categorized by disease populations, female/women participants ranged from 33% (IQR 25.8%–35.8%) in cardiac surgery to 40.1% (IQR 32%–43.9%) in AKI (Table 1 ). Meta-analytic estimates were performed to estimate proportional enrollment after accounting for the number of trials and trial size. The results of the weighted estimates were similar to the crude estimates, except for a higher pooled effect size (ES) of females/women enrollment in trials from Latin and South America (0.44, 95% CI 0.43–0.45) and lower pooled ES from trials involving medical devices (0.29, 95% CI 0.15–0.42), and in major surgery populations (0.28, 95% CI 0.13–0.42). Heterogeneity by I 2 was high across all categories (Table 2 ). In meta-regression, pediatric age group ( p < 0.001) and process of care intervention ( p = 0.02) were positively associated, but cardiac surgery ( p = 0.001) and major surgery ( p = 0.006) were negatively associated with the proportions of enrolled female/women participants (Table 3 ). Table 2. Meta-analytic estimates of the proportion of female/women participants in critical care nephrology trials according 