s studies from Oceania had the highest PPR (1.07, IQR 0.89–1.20). Adult population had a median PPR of 0.89 (IQR 0.80–1.06), similar to the pediatric studies (median PPR 0.95, IQR 0.80–1.07). Studies including the major surgery population had the lowest PPR (0.67, IQR 0.29–0.73), compared with studies involving other populations (Fig. 2 A–D and Additional File 1: Table S3). There was no association between the year of publication ( p = 0.11) or the number of participants ( p = 0.53) and PPR (Additional File 1: Fig. S1). Meta-regression revealed significant associations between the Oceania region of enrollment ( p = 0.02) and higher PPR, and the major surgery population ( p < 0.001) and lower PPR (Table 4 ). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Participation-to-prevalence ratio (PPR) for female/women participants in critical care nephrology trials by A region, B age group, C types of intervention, and D disease conditions. Box and whisker plots represent the distribution of PPR within each category. The thick black horizontal bar represents the median PPR within the category, with the blue box representing the interquartile range between the first and third quartile of the dataset. The black lines represent whiskers that extend to one and a half times the interquartile interval; values beyond are extreme values represented as blue dots. The green box encompasses the PPR range which is considered acceptable, i.e., 0.8–1.2. PPR < 0.8 is considered underrepresentation, and PPR > 1.2 is considered overrepresentation of trial populations compared to reference disease populations Table 4. Meta-regression with participation-to-prevalence ratio (PPR) as the dependent variable PPR of female/women Coefficient 95% CI P value Journal type (critical care as reference) General medicine − 0.04 − 0.13 to 0.05 0.36 General pediatrics − 0.28 − 0.60 to 0.03 0.08 Nephrology − 0.07 − 0.18 to 0.04 0.24 Publication year − 0.0003 − 0.007 to 0.006 0.92 Region of enrollment (Asia as reference) Europe 0.