tation, or women of childbearing age were considered strongly justified (i.e., interventions with potential harm to the fetus) in 17 (36.1%), potentially justified (i.e., RRT dose and timing of initiation) in 7 (14.9%), and poorly justified (i.e., medications considered safe during pregnancy/lactation, RRT modality, types of fluid therapy) in 23 (48.9%) of the 47 studies (Additional File 1: Table S4) [ 90 – 135 ]. Trials with sex-specific exclusion criteria were more likely to have fewer female/women participants than trials that did not (33.6% vs. 36.8%, p = 0.04). Five studies (4.3%) used sex as a factor for stratification or minimization during randomization. Table 5. Sex and gender reporting and analysis of included studies No (%) ( N = 117) Eligibility Inclusion criteria a 12 (10.3) Exclusion criteria b 49 (41.9) Randomization: stratification/minimization 5 (4.3) Nomenclature Sex 64 (54.7) Gender 31 (26.5) Both 3 (2.6) Not reported 19 (16.2) Descriptor Male/female 96 (82.1) Men/women 13 (11.1) Both 5 (4.3) Not reported 2 (1.7) Other: boys/girls 1 (0.9) Analysis 35 (29.9%) Sex-stratified analysis 0 Subgroup analysis 14 (12.0) 27 (23.1) Open in a new tab a Sex included in AKI risk score ( n = 4), SCr or creatinine clearance cutoff by sex ( n = 8) b Pregnancy or breastfeeding ( n = 46), SCr or creatinine clearance cutoff by sex ( n = 7), women of child-bearing age ( n = 1) Sex- and gender-based reporting The nomenclature for the demographics included sex ( n = 64 [54.7%]), gender ( n = 31 [26.5%]), both sex and gender ( n = 3 [2.6%]), or neither sex nor gender being reported (19 [16.2%]) studies. Male/female were used as descriptors in 96 (82.1%) trials, followed by men/women in 13 (11.1%) trials, boys/girls in 1 (0.9%) trial, and not reported in 2 (1.7%) trials. In pediatric studies, the following subclassifications were used: boys/girls ( n = 1), male/female ( n = 5), both male/female and boys/girls ( n = 1), and not reported ( n = 2) (Table 5 ). Sex and gender terminology were used interchangeably in 3 (2.6%) trials, as were male/men and female/women in 5 (4.3%) studies. Of the 98 trials that featured a sex or gender heading, 41 (41.8%) used appropriate categories between the heading and descriptions in the tables or texts (e.g., “Sex: male/female” or “Gender: men/women”). No studies reported the inclusion of any gender-diverse participants, defined as individuals whose gender identity exists outside the traditional binary norm of male/female (e.g., non-binary) [ 136 ]. Sex- and gender-based analysis (SGBA) Overall, 35 (29.9%) studies reported SGBA. None provided a sex-aggregated analysis. Fourteen (12.0%) studies conducted sex subgroup analyses, and 1 study found a significantly different risk in the primary outcome between sex (Additional File 1: Table S5) [ 90 – 135 , 137 – 207 ]. Twenty-seven (23.1%) studies used sex in covariate adjustments (Table 5 ). In addition, 4 (3.4%) mentioned potential sex differences in the discussion section. Three (2.6%) had published or planned a separate post-hoc analysis with sex-aggregate analysis. The multivariable logistic regression showed that trials involving RRT (OR 14.6, 95% CI 2.04–104.48, p = 0.008), medication (OR 12.03, 95% CI 1.77–81.90, p = 0.01), and including more than 1000 participants (OR 12.2, 95% CI 2.19–68, p = 0.004) were more likely, whereas industry-funded trials were less likely to be associated with SGBA (OR 0.08, 95% CI 0.01–0.70, p = 0.02) (Additional File 1: Table S6). There were no trend changes in SGBA over time (P for trend = 0.42) (Additional File 1: Fig. S2). In addition, there is no analysis of interactions between age, hormonal status throughout the lifespan, and sex in all studies. Discussion We conducted a meta-epidemiologic study of RCTs in critical care nephrology published in high-impact journals in general medicine, nephrology, critical care, and pediatrics over a 24-year period. Females/women accounted for one-third of trial populations. The median participation-to-prevalence ratio was within 0.8–1.2 range. However, 22% had under- and 8% had over-representation of female/women participants. Sex-related factors were commonly listed as exclusion criteria, which were associated with a lower proportion of female/women participants. There was a lack of consistent reporting regarding sex and gender. Finally, sex- and gender-based analyses were infrequent. Adequate representation of females/women in clinical trials can help reflect the burden on the underlying population. We have demonstrated a lower ratio of enrollment of women in critical care nephrology trials. This finding is in line with previous literature of nephrology studies [ 21 ]. Despite regulatory policies on equitable inclusion [ 208 , 209 ], the proportion of females/women has not changed over the years. However, the proportions of females/women were generally representative of the reference populations when considering PPR, except for major surgery. Trials in other disciplines such as chronic kidney disease and cardiovascular diseases also demonstrated under-representation of females/women, especially in device or procedural trials [ 20 , 210 , 211 ]. Of note, the proportions of females/women admitted in critical care and who received life-sustaining treatment may have been influenced by numerous complex factors. Despite females/women having higher or similar disease severity at intensive care unit (ICU) admission [ 212 , 213 ], females/women were less likely than males/men to be admitted to ICU and receive invasive life-sustaining or organ support interventions, including surgery and RRT [ 214 – 216 ]. Lower muscle mass influencing serum creatinine concentrations might result in under or delayed diagnosis of AKI in females/women [ 217 ]. Sociodemographic factors could impact the decision to seek treatment, decision-making regarding acceptance of advanced life support, and willingness to participate in clinical trials by patients and their proxies. Research infrastructure in different areas could impact recruitment strategies, resulting in varied representation across regions. Techniques to enhance equity and retention in clinical trial enrollment include training research staff and collaborating with advocacy groups, community organizations, and healthcare providers throughout all processes (e.g., trial development, recruitment, intervention delivery, outcome assessment, analysis, reporting, and dissemination of results) [ 218 , 219 ]. The reporting should include the proportion of females/women being screened, consented, and retained in RCTs as well as reasons for ineligibility and attrition. Previous critical care and nephrology studies have shown that only 20–30% of lead authors were women [ 220 – 222 ]. Our study similarly showed a low proportion of women in senior authorship and an overall percentage of women authors. Women first and last/corresponding authorship have been linked to a higher proportion of female/women enrollment and sex-specific reporting [ 223 , 224 ]. Although these were not associated with proportions of female/women participants in our study, diversity in research teams could broaden perspectives and influence study design, reporting, and sex and gender-based analyses [ 225 ]. Eligibility criteria play a crucial role in study safety and the selection of at-risk populations. However, overly stringent criteria may limit the generalizability of the findings. Almost half of the studies in our analysis listed pregnancy or lactation as exclusion criteria, which may systematically bias against the recruitment of women of reproductive age. A systematic review of RCTs in critical care showed that 76% of studies excluded pregnant patients and 26.7% of lactating women [ 25 ]. The exclusions are likely justified based on maternal or fetal safety concerns or the impact of physiological changes during pregnancy on the pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of medications. However, these could lead to an underrepresentation of pregnant women who may have altered efficacy or safety from the intervention. For example, observational studies suggested benefits of a more intensive dialysis regimen in pregnant maintenance hemodialysis patients [ 226 ]. Due to exclusion criteria, dose and timing of RRT initiation and modality selection in the setting of acute or critical illness in patients with AKI during pregnancy or peripartum have not been adequately studied. Similarly, studies involving interventions with medications considered safe during pregnancy or lactation should not exclude these patients. In 2016, Sex- and Gender Equity in Research (SAGER) guidelines were published as a framework for reporting sex and gender information in research, study design, data analysis, and interpretation [ 227 ]. While the reporting of the sex or gender of participants was almost universal (97.4%) in our study, only sex as a biological variable and a binary factor was considered. Sex and gender were poorly distinguished, and the impact of sex hormone fluctuations across the lifespan (e.g., puberty, menopause, and andropause) remains under-investigated. There was no mention of any intersex, non-binary, or gender-diverse identity in the baseline characteristics of any of the studies. Of note, approximately 0.4–3% of the population identified themselves as gender-diverse [ 228 – 230 ]. Our study showed that SGBA was performed in less than one third of all studies. Sex differences have been demonstrated in the pharmacology, efficacy, and safety of drugs [ 231 – 233 ]. For example, furosemide has been shown to induce a higher sodium excretion rate in males compared to females [ 234 ]. Aminoglycosides, vancomycin, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, and digoxin have slower clearance rates in females than in males [ 235 ]. Performing sex-disaggregated analysis is useful to demonstrate the heterogeneity in the development and treatment effects of kidney-specific therapies, especially during acute illness [ 236 ]. Sex and gender-based analyses may help to reveal biological differences as well as social disparities that may be underpowered in individual studies but could be later incorporated into meta-analyses across RCTs [ 237 ]. The strengths of this study include the collection of comprehensive data from RCTs in critical care nephrology including adult and pediatric populations of participant and author sex/gender. We explored the eligibility criteria in detail and adjudicated their justification according to standards. All data abstractions were conducted independently and in duplicate. In addition to the proportions of female/women participants, PPRs were investigated using multiple reference data sources and described with various aspects. We also examined potential factors which may be associated with sex bias in trial enrollment and SGBA. Our study has several limitations. We only included articles from high-impact journals because these RCTs are more likely to pass stringent publication requirements including transparent reporting and have practice-changing results. We did not assess individual RCT quality due to significant heterogeneity. Our search strategy was limited to the Web of Science and the English language; therefore, relevant publications from additional databases or published in other languages may not have been identified. The proportions of females/women in different disease populations may not be representative of the sex-specific global disease burden and may vary over time. We acknowledge the challenges in ascribing the authors’ sex/gender. We only assessed sex as a binary variable; however, sex at birth and gender identity could be recorded separately in future trials. Finally, we chose to focus on critical care nephrology. However, a similar methodology could be effectively applied overall or to specific domains within critical care as well. Other types of studies such as target trial emulation, which uses observational data to identify causal inferences to emulate a trial, may better represent females/women and the gender-diverse population in real-world settings [ 238 ]. In addition, the impact of sex-related hormonal changes throughout the lifespan and AKI needs to be further explored. Conclusions There have been lower females/women than male/men representation in critical care nephrology trials over the past 24 years. Eligibility criteria introduce sex biases against broader inclusion of females/women, and this may be appropriate from a scientific or safety perspective in some but not all studies. Efforts are needed to ensure that sex representation better reflects the true health burdens of the population. Sex-specific analysis is crucial to ensure that the interpretation of data for both male and female populations is reliable. Journals should clearly distinguish the terms sex and gender and have policies regarding sex and gender reporting [ 239 ]. Finally, inclusion of sex- and gender-diverse participants may help elucidate different mechanisms, responses to treatment, and outcomes [ 240 ]. Supplementary Information 12916_2025_4202_MOESM1_ESM.docx (195.4KB, docx) Additional file 1. Supplementary Material. Acknowledgements SMB is supported by a Canada Research Chair in Critical Care Outcomes and Systems Evaluation. Abbreviations AIM Annals of Internal Medicine AJKD American Journal of Kidney Diseases AJRCCM American Journal of Respiratory and Critical Care Medicine AKI Acute kidney injury BMJ British Medical Journal CCM Critical Care Medicine CJASN Clinical Journal of the American Society of Nephrology CKD Chronic kidney disease DOI Digital object identifier ES Effect size ICM Intensive Care Med ICU Intensive care unit IQR Interquartile range JAMA Journal of the American Medical Association JASN The Journal of the American Society of Nephrology KI Kidney International NDT Nephrology Dialysis Transplantation NEJM New England Journal of Medicine PPR Participation-to-p