 ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Brenner BM, Cooper ME, de Zeeuw D, Keane WF, Mitch WE, Parving HH, et al. Effects of losartan on renal and cardiovascular outcomes in patients with type 2 diabetes and nephropathy. N Engl J Med. 2001;345:861–9. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa011161. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Lewis EJ, Hunsicker LG, Clarke WR, Berl T, Pohl MA, Lewis JB, et al. Renoprotective effect of the angiotensin-receptor antagonist irbesartan in patients with nephropathy due to type 2 diabetes. N Engl J Med. 2001;345:851–60. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa011303. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Ruggenenti P, Perna A, Loriga G, Ganeva M, Ene-Iordache B, Turturro M, et al. Blood-pressure control for renoprotection in patients with non-diabetic chronic renal disease (REIN-2): Multicentre, randomised controlled trial. Lancet. 2005;365:939–46. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(05)71082-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Perkovic V, Jardine MJ, Neal B, Bompoint S, Heerspink HJL, Charytan DM, et al. Canagliflozin and renal outcomes in type 2 diabetes and nephropathy. N Engl J Med. 2019;380:2295–306. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1811744. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Wanner C, Inzucchi SE, Lachin JM, Fitchett D, von Eynatten M, Mattheus M, et al. Empagliflozin and progression of kidney disease in type 2 diabetes. N Engl J Med. 2016;375:323–34. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1515920. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',41,'2025-10-15 06:28:30','2025-10-15 06:28:30');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3394,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9718023','Nephrology Program Director Protected Time for Program Administration in the United States.','Yuan CM, Young BY, Watson MA, Sussman AN.','2022','decrement stipulations uploading dedicates iqrs edo stipulation devoting redacted devoting devoting donating directorships aspirational directorship stipulations paused championed underfilled timekeeping wellbound nerdc s0001 rajeev raghavan nee sussman sussman sussman sussman asnjournals dcsupplemental 148_nephrology_2021 mcgann joyner griesbach theobald kolman stutzman roett dregansky hamstra guidebook milestonesguidebook kempenich campi schenarts momentive higherlogicdownload amazonaws fecab58a 0e31 416b 8e56 46fc9eda5c37 uploadedimages 2022_aaim_letter_to_acgme_on_concerns_about_core_faculty_reqs dio dedicated_time_core_faculty_2022 yeqiumdc jaar lipner brossman s0001','Visual Abstract Open in a new tab Keywords: nephrology, program director, fellowship program, protected time, program administration, didactic teaching Abstract Background and objectives The Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) required that program directors receive 10–20 h/wk of protected time for program administration (including didactic teaching). In July 2022, this was reduced for all internal medicine subspecialties on the basis of program size, with 8 h/wk required for programs with fewer than seven fellows, the majority of nephrology programs. Design, setting, participants, & measurements We surveyed all 151 US adult nephrology program directors (ACGME Public List of Programs 2021–2022) to determine how much protected time they receive, how much they think is necessary, and the division of their professional time. The anonymous 20-question online survey was administered from March 31 to April 30, 2022. The analysis was descriptive. Results Response rate was 66% (99 of 151). Geographic distribution and approved fellow positions were similar to programs nationally; 59% had fewer than seven approved positions. Median protected time was 10 h/wk (interquartile range, 5–10), with 8 h/wk (interquartile range, 5–10) for those with fewer than seven positions. Program directors estimated needing 12 h/wk (interquartile range, 10–16) to effectively administer programs, including those with fewer than seven positions, a median 5 h/wk (interquartile range, 0–7) more than received. Of program directors reporting <10 h/wk for program administration, 62% provided >20 hours of direct patient care. Thirty-nine percent had no protected time for core faculty. Fellow recruitment (68%) was the most time-consuming task, and didactic teaching (80%) was the most professionally rewarding. Conclusions Approximately half of the nephrology programs surveyed were not in compliance with the ACGME-stipulated 10-h/wk minimum protected time for the 2021–2022 training year. Program directors estimated a median of 12 h/wk are needed to effectively manage programs. Introduction On July 1, 2022, the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME), citing a desire for standardization, adjusted protected time for program administration for all internal medicine subspecialty program directors ( 1 ). ACGME defines “program administration” as “nonclinical teaching and administration” ( 2 ). “Protected time” is defined as “salary support, supplemental compensation, educational value units, or relief of time from other professional duties” ( 2 ). Changes in ACGME-stipulated protected time for nephrology program leadership (program directors, associate program directors, and core clinical faculty) from 2019 to 2022 are summarized in Table 1 . Table 1. Minimum dedicated/protected time for nephrology program leadership specified by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Program Leadership Before July 1, 2019 ( 5 ) July 1, 2019 to June 30, 2022 ( 5 ) July 1, 2022 ( 3 ) Program director 20 h/wk 10–20 h/wk a 8 h/wk for <7 clinical fellows; 10 h/wk for 7–9 clinical fellows; 12 h/wk or more for ≥10 clinical fellows b Associate program director(s) (also count as core clinical faculty) None stipulated None stipulated Required. As core faculty, 4 h/wk plus additional time on the basis of program size: 0 h/wk for <7 fellows; 1.2 h/wk for 7–9 fellows; 1.6 h/wk or more for ≥10 fellows Key/core clinical faculty 10 h/wk None stipulated 4 h/wk c Minimum total hours for 6 fellow programs (program leadership: 1 program director; 4 core faculty, including the associate program director) 60 h/wk (program director: 20 h; 4 core faculty: 40 h) 10 h/wk (program director: 10 h) 24 h/wk (program director: 8 h; associate program director: 4 h; 3 additional core faculty: 12 h) Open in a new tab a Defined as 25%–50% of salary. A 25% full-time equivalent was defined as “one and one quarter days per week” in the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Nephrology Program Requirements 2021 ( 2 ). On the basis of a standard 40-hour week, this would be 10–20 h/wk or 0.25–0.5 full-time equivalent. b Defined by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education as 0.2 full-time equivalent (fewer than seven fellows), 0.25 full-time equivalent (seven to nine fellows), and 0.3–0.5 full-time equivalent (ten or more fellows). One full-time equivalent is assumed to be a standard 40-hour work week. c Defined by the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education as 0.1 full-time equivalent. One full-time equivalent is assumed to be a standard 40-hour work week. These changes may have profound effects within nephrology training programs, reducing the minimum required program director protected time from 10 to 8 h/wk for programs with fewer than seven approved fellow positions ( 2 , 3 ). Although adjustments are made for larger programs, approximately two thirds of nephrology programs have fewer than seven positions, and the majority will be negatively affected ( 4 ). Core clinical faculty (one for every 1.5 fellows) will be required to have 4 h/wk of protected time, having had no specific requirement for the past 3 training years. Associate program directors will be required for all programs, but those in programs of fewer than seven fellows will receive no additional protected time other than for core faculty. The 2022 requirements appear to represent an increase in overall protected time for training program leadership but only if the last 3 training years are considered ( Table 1 ). Before July 2019 (since 2007), nephrology program directors were to dedicate 20 h/wk to the program (regardless of size), and key clinical faculty (now designated core faculty) were to dedicate at least 10 hours ( 5 ). In July 2019, ACGME required that program directors “must be provided with support adequate for administration of the program,” stipulated as 25%–50% of salary (10–20 h/wk) ( 2 ). No specific protected time was required for core faculty other than that they “devote a significant portion of their entire effort to … education and/or administration” ( 2 ). The 2022 requirements increase protected time for program leadership in a six-fellow program from a minimum of 10 h/wk up to 24 h/wk, but this is a decrement from the 60 h/wk required before July 2019 ( Table 1 ). Program directors with seven or more fellows are given more protected time on the basis of fellow number, but none would receive 20 h/wk. ACGME has provided no rationale for these requirements, and the literature regarding protected time in any specialty or subspecialty is sparse. A 2013 survey of pediatric specialty/subspecialty program directors found that those with fewer than seven fellows received on average 0.2 full-time equivalent (FTE; 8 hours) of protected time but that they required 0.25 FTE (10 hours) to adequately perform necessary program administration ( 6 ). A 2021 survey of urology program directors found that the majority felt that 0.2 FTE (8 hours) protected time “helped with administrative work,” but 31% felt that more time was required for larger programs ( 7 ). The Society of Teachers of Family Medicine recently published guidelines on the basis of family practice program faculty surveys recommending that program directors receive a minimum 0.5 FTE (20 hours) protected nonclinical time for program administration and that core faculty members have 0.3 FTE (12 hours) ( 8 ). Over the past 10 years, the ACGME administrative requirements have increased due to the New Accreditation System, the Milestones Project, and expanded Common Fellowship Program Requirements ( 9 ). The time and cost of meeting these requirements have increased; the greatest cost is associated with faculty and program director time for evaluation and education ( 10 ). There is no published information as to how much actual protected time for program administration is made available to nephrology program leadership by their sponsoring institutions, whether ACGME stipulations are being met, and how program director time is distributed. Most importantly, there is no information as to how much protected time program directors believe is needed for effective program administration. Therefore, we surveyed all nephrology program directors in the United States to define the amount of protected time their institutions allow for program administration, how much time they think is necessary, and how they distribute their professional time overall. We hypothesized that some were not receiving the 25%–50% protected time (10–20 h/wk) of salary support stipulated in the 2019 program requirements and that their estimates of time required to effectively administer their programs are greater than stipulated in the 2022 program requirements on the basis of program size. Materials and Methods The 20-question anonymous online survey was developed by the authors and reviewed and edited by R.N. (core faculty) and R.R. (a former program director) for format and clarity. After uploading to the survey platform ( 11 ), it was tested by three program directors (A.N.S., M.A.W., and B.Y.Y.). Program directors ( n =151) were identified from the ACGME Public List of Nephrology Specialty Programs ( 12 ). The survey ( Supplemental Material ) was administered between March 31 and April 30, 2022. Respondent IP addresses were blocked. Survey links were delivered by email with delivery receipt. Reminders were sent at 1-week intervals. The first question, which was required, asked respondents to confirm program director status. If “no,” they were directed to the end of survey and asked to forward the link to the program director. All surveys were preceded by explanatory text, allowing potential respondents to exit if they did not wish to participate. Demographic information collected included years in the position (<5, 5–10, >10–20, or >20 years), whether they had been in the position before the 2019–2020 training year, and the geographic location of the program (Northeast, Southern, Midwest, or West census areas). Program site identification was not requested. Although 1 FTE is commonly defined as 40 h/wk, we recognized that institutions may variably define FTE, and program director work hours differ. Therefore, we asked that surveyed program directors report and estimate time in hours per week. Objectives were as follows: (1) to determine median hours per week of protected time for nephrology program directors on the basis of program size; (2) to determine the percentage of program directors not receiving 10 h/wk of protected time and if those with <5 years versus those with ≥5 years of experience are less likely to receive 10 h/wk; (3) to determine median hours per week of protected time program directors estimate are required to effectively administer their programs (on the basis of program size); (4) to determine how program directors distribute professional time in addition to program administration; (5) to identify the three most time-consuming program administration tasks, the three giving the most professional satisfaction, and the three giving the least professional satisfaction; (6) to determine whether there is a program administrative coordinator (an ACGME requirement) and how many hours per week that person dedicates to the program; (7) to determine whether there is an associate program director and their median hours per week of protected time; and (8) to determine median hours per week of protected time for core faculty. Statistical analyses were descriptive, with percentages, means, SDs, medians, and interquartile ranges (IQRs) used as appropriate. The Fisher exact test was used to compare the percentages of programs meeting the ACGME requirements on the basis of time as program director (<5 versus 5 or more years). Geographic distribution and clinical fellow complement of respondent programs versus programs nationally were compared using the chi-squared test. P <0.05 was considered statistically significant. The protocol (EDO-2022–0896) was approved by the Walter Reed National Military Medical Center Department of Research Protections as exempt from institutional review board review per 32 CFR 219.104(d)(4)(ii). There was no funding source. Results Ninety-nine of 151 nephrology program directors responded to the survey (66% response rate) either partially or completely. The number responding to each question is shown in Supplemental Material . Average question completion rate was 84%. Geographic distribution and approved fellow complement of respondent programs were similar to those of US nephrology programs overall ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Summary of program director survey results Characteristics of Program and Program Leadership Survey Result Reference Information References: 2021–22 and 2022–23 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Nephrology Program Data and Program Requirements ( 2 , 3 ); National Resident Matching Program 2021 Appointment Year Match Data ( 4 ) Years as program director, n (%) n =89 NA <5 40 (45) NA 5–10 28 (31) NA >10–20 17 (19) NA >20 4 (4) NA Program geographical area ( P =0.84; chi-squared test), n (%) n =89 151 US nephrology programs a Northeast 33 (37) 52 (34) South 30 (34) 52 (34) Midwest 14 (16) 28 (19) West 12 (13) 19 (13) Approved clinical fellow positions ( P =0.07; chi-squared test), n (%) Median=6 (IQR, 4–8; n =92) National median=6 (IQR, 4–8; n =151) ( 4 [NRMP]) 6 or fewer clinical fellows 54 (59) 99 (65); 3 positions or fewer offered in 2021 NRMP b 7–9 clinical fellows 22 (24) 22 (15); 4 positions offered in 2021 NRMP b 10 or more clinical fellows 16 (17) 30 (20); ≥5 positions offered in 2021 NRMP b Program director estimated average hours worked per week Median=56 (IQR, 50–60; n =88) NA Program director h/wk protected time for program administration Median=10 (IQR, 5–10; n =83) 2021–2022: 10 h/wk required 6 or fewer clinical fellows 8 (IQR, 5–10) 2022–2023: 8 h/wk required 7–9 clinical fellows 9 (IQR, 6–10) 2022–2023: 10 h/wk required 10 or more clinical fellows 10 (IQR, 10–15) 2022–2023: ≥12 h/wk required Estimated h/wk required for effective program administration Median: 12 (IQR, 10–16; n =88) NA 6 or fewer clinical fellows 12 (IQR, 10–15) NA 7–9 clinical fellows 10 (IQR, 10–15) NA 10 or more clinical fellows 20 (IQR, 16–20) NA Program coordinator? 100% yes ( n =81) 2021–2022: Required Hours per week dedicated to the nephrology program 20 (IQR, 10–20) 2022–2023: Minimum 20 h/wk for 4- to 6-fellow program Associate program director? 72% yes (58/81) 2022–2023: Required Hours per week protected time for program administration for associate program director 4 (IQR, 1–5) 2022–2023: Minimum 4 h/wk for 6 or fewer clinical fellows No. of core clinical faculty (not including the associate program director) 6 (IQR, 4–10, =79) Minimum: 1 core faculty per 1.5 approved clinical fellows, not including the program director Hours per week per faculty protected time for program administration for core faculty 2 (IQR, 0–5); 39%=0 h 2021–2022: No stipulation; 2022–2023: 4 h/wk Open in a new tab IQR, interquartile range; NRMP, National Resident Matching Program. a The Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Public List of Nephrology Specialty Programs ( 12 ). b Total positions estimated by multiplying the number offered in the 2021 National Resident Matching Program by two (2 years of clinical fellowship). This likely underestimates the number of programs having seven (three alternating with four positions offered yearly) or nine positions (four alternating with five positions offered yearly). Numbers are on the basis of the number of nephrology clinical fellowship positions offered for the 2021–2022 training year published in NRMP 2021 Appointment Year Match Data ( 4 ). Overall, program directors received a median of 10 h/wk (IQR, 5–10) of protected time for program administration. Programs of fewer than seven fellows received a median of 8 h/wk (IQR, 5–10). Forty-eight percent received <10 h/wk, not meeting the 2021 ACGME protected time requirement, with 7% reporting 0 hours of protected time. For those <5 years in their positions ( n =36), 61% received ≥10 h/wk of protected time (median, 10; IQR, 7–12). For those with ≥5 years ( n =46), 44% had ≥10 hours of protected time (median, 8; IQR, 4–10; P =0.13). Seventy percent ( n =62) had been a program director before July 2019, and of these, 52 (84%) reported that they had not received 20 h/wk of protected time as required. There were 149 nephrology programs in 2018–2019; this represents, at minimum, 37% of programs not in compliance at that time ( 13 ). Program directors estimated requiring a median of 12 h/wk (IQR, 10–16) of protected time to effectively administer their programs ( Table 2 ). Those with fewer than seven fellows estimated needing 12 h/wk (IQR, 10–15), whereas those with ten or more fellows needed 20 h/wk (IQR, 16–20). Overall, program directors ( n =82) estimated they needed a median of 5 h/wk (IQR, 0–7) more than received to effectively administer their programs. Seventy-three percent (60 of 82) estimated that they needed more time than provided, 23% (19 of 82) estimated that the time was sufficient, and 4% (three of 82) estimated they could do with less time. Those <5 years in their positions ( n =36) estimated needing a median of 4.5 h/wk (IQR, 2–7.25) more than received, whereas those ≥5 years ( n =46) in their positions reported needing 4 h/wk (IQR, 0–5). Figure 1 shows the division of professional time. Although 48% reported <10 h/wk of protected time, 65% reported devoting <10 h/wk to program administration, which includes didactic, nonclinical teaching. One commented that “salary support is there for the program director, however, clinical activity is not changed” (redacted comments) ( Supplemental Material ). This is suggested by the finding that the majority spend most of their time delivering direct and indirect clinical care. Sixty-nine percent spent at least 10 h/wk directly/indirectly supervising fellow-delivered patient care (clinical teaching). Fifty-two percent spent >20 h/wk personally providing direct and indirect patient care. Program directors devoting <10 h/wk to training program administration reported a median of 8 h/wk (IQR, 4–10) of protected time, and 62% reported providing >20 h/wk of patient care personally. On the other hand, those devoting 10 h/wk or more to program administration had a median of 10 hours (IQR, 10–15) of protected time, and only 32% provided >20 h/wk of patient care personally. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Division of professional tasks by average hours per week among program directors (PDs; n =82). Time spent on hospital-required administration and training tasks was <8 h/wk for 77% of PDs (63 of 82). Time dedicated to teaching and other program administration ( e . g ., medical students or medical residents) was <8 h/wk for 78% of PDs. These tasks are not shown. Notably, five PDs (6%) were simultaneously medical directors of dialysis. Table 3 summarizes the three most time-consuming, the three most professionally rewarding, and the three least professionally rewarding tasks associated with program administration. The most time-consuming tasks were fellow recruitment, didactic teaching, and routine required administrative work. Didactic teaching, despite being time consuming, was considered the most professionally rewarding task, followed by curriculum development and mentorship of fellow projects and research. The least professionally rewarding tasks were routine required administrative work, also considered one of the most time consuming, followed by fellow remediation and Program Evaluation Committee Improvement Plan implementation. Table 3. Program administration tasks Program Administration Task Most Time Consuming Most Professionally Rewarding Least Professionally Rewarding Curriculum development 26 (32) a 45 (56) a 2 (2) Didactic teaching 44 (54) a 65 (80) a 0 (0) Attendance at didactic conferences 16 (20) 40 (49) a 6 (7) Developing and scheduling fellow rotations 21 (26) a 7 (9) 22 (27) a Fellow and faculty evaluation/milestone assessment 10 (12) 2 (2) 12 (15) Mentorship of fellow projects and research 15 (19) 41 (50) a 3 (4) Fellow remediation 8 (10) 1 (1) 30 (37) a Fellow recruitment 55 (68) a 14 (17) 23 (28) a Annual fellow orientation and graduation administration 8 (10) 16 (20) 7 (9) Clinical competency committee administration 10 (12) 2 (2) 16 (20) Program evaluation committee and program improvement action plan administration and implementation 20 (25) a 4 (5) 27 (33) a Routine ACGME/GME administrative work, response to data calls, and documentation 41 (51) a 0 (0) 66 (81) a Open in a new tab Data are presented as n (percentage). ACGME, Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education; GME, Graduate Medical Education. a Those chosen by 25% or more of program directors as the three most time consuming, most professionally rewarding, and least professionally rewarding ( n =81). As shown in Table 2 , 100% (81 of 81) reported having a program coordinator, as required by the ACGME. Seventy-one percent (57 of 80) had an associate program director (required as of July 2022). Thirty percent of these had two or more associate program directors; the median number of approved fellow positions for these programs was eight (IQR, 6–12). Median protected time for associate program directors was 4 h/wk (IQR, 1–5). Some arranged protected time for their associate program director by donating some of their own. Core clinical faculty (required to have 4 h/wk of protected time beginning in July 2022) had a median of 1.5 h/wk (IQR, 0–5). Thirty-nine percent of responding programs had no protected time available to core clinical faculty. One program director commented, “We ask a lot of them, but they get no time to do it” ( Supplemental Material ). Another said, “For the last several years, recruitment has been challenging. This has required faculty to pick up the tasks usually assigned to fellows” ( Supplemental Material ). Discussion Our survey indicates that a substantial number of US nephrology program directors were not receiving ACGME-required institutional support for protected time for program administration in training year 2021–2022. This situation was present before the coronavirus disease 2019 pandemic. Before July 2019, when required protected time was reduced from 20 to 10–20 h/wk, at least one third of program directors did not receive 20 h/wk. At the time of the survey (April 2022), approximately half were not receiving 10 h/wk, and about half of programs with fewer than seven fellows would not be in compliance with the 8 h/wk requirement that went into effect in July 2022. Some reported they were given 10 h/wk officially, but scheduled direct patient care and other administrative duties ( e . g ., dialysis unit directorships and supervision of advanced practice providers) made time for program administration aspirational, unless done on one’s own time and despite working a median of 56 h/wk. The respondents estimated that minimum protected time should increase to a median of 12 h/wk in order to effectively meet program administration time needs, even in smaller programs of fewer than seven fellows, rather than decrease to 8 h/wk. Program directors with ten or more fellows estimated needing a median of 20 h/wk. No contradictory data are available in the literature indicating that a median of 12 h/wk of protected time (with 20 h/wk for larger programs) is an overestimate of program director needs. As ACGME defines them, the duties are considerable: “The program director must have responsibility, authority, and accountability for: administration and operations; teaching and scholarly activity; fellow recruitment and selection, evaluation, and promotion of fellows, and disciplinary action; supervision of fellows; and fellow education in the context of patient care” ( 2 ). Decreasing time allotted for program administration may make the position vulnerable to being viewed as an ancillary duty, and at some institutions may already be (comments in Supplemental Material ). Some institutions may not factor program directorship into academic promotion decisions. For some, time for program administration (which includes curriculum development and didactic teaching) is undertaken in addition to a demanding personal and supervisory clinical load, further extending the work week and affecting work-life balance. Sponsoring institutions and division chiefs may have conflicts in ensuring that clinical work hours are maximized and that ACGME stipulations are met. As the majority of nephrology program directors experience a reduction in protected time, another difficulty will be securing protected time for core faculty to help shoulder the burden of program administration and education. Almost 40% of core faculty members now receive no protected time. Some programs may struggle to secure the mandated 4 h/wk from their sponsoring institutions, and ACGME has recently paused implementation of this requirement ( 14 , 15 ). It is unclear how core faculty given no protected time are able to “devote a significant portion of their entire effort to fellow education and/or administration, and must, as a component of their activities, teach, evaluate, and provide formative feedback to fellows” ( 2 ). For nephrology, the erosion of protected time has occurred in a setting of declining board pass rates (74%–80% over the last 3 years) ( 16 ). This has been attributed to poor fellow preparation and associated with international medical school graduate status, smaller program size, and lower Internal Medicine-certifying examination scores among fellows entering training ( 17 ). ACGME and the nephrology training community should consider the possibility that another contributor to poor board performance may be a lack of sufficient time for curriculum development and didactic teaching by program directors and core faculty. This might be the focus of future research. Although program directors have expressed the highest satisfaction in developing curriculum, participating in and delivering didactic teaching, and mentoring fellows in scholarly work, they may have little time for these pursuits. Despite what ACGME has championed, as protected time for didactics and curriculum innovation declines and routine administration and data submission burdens increase, the majority of time with fellows may be spent in bedside clinical teaching. Our survey had an excellent response rate (66%) and was verified as delivered to all US nephrology program directors. Respondents were not significantly different in terms of geographic distribution and program size to national nephrology programs. To internally validate responses, we asked program directors to indicate the amount of protected time they received and later estimate how many hours per week they dedicated to program administration. The survey was conducted in April when program leadership begins work on graduation tasks and planning for the new training year but not during the intense work of the recruitment/matching period and during the turnover of the training year. Limitations include that respondents had to estimate the time spent in multiple tasks, and average to weekly effort. Many tasks associated with program administration take up blocks of time depending on training cycle stage ( e . g ., preparing for the academic year, recruiting and interviewing, and didactic curriculum development) or cannot be routinely anticipated ( e . g ., remediation and new curriculum or evaluation requirements from ACGME). We did not inquire about the number of filled versus approved fellow positions, which would have provided information regarding protected time needs in underfilled programs. There was no external validation of time estimates, which would require an external or personal timekeeping process over the course of a training year and might be a focus for future research. In conclusion, our survey demonstrated that one third to one half of US nephrology programs were not in compliance with the ACGME-stipulated 10-h/wk minimum protected time for program directors. Although no public data are available, this may also be the situation for other internal medicine subspecialties. It is unclear what the effect of the new standardized protected time requirements will be nationally or if there will be unintended consequences. Many training institutions will likely provide no more than the minimum protected time required. As we demonstrate, many do not provide the minimum. The majority of nephrology program directors estimate that at least 12 h/wk are needed to effectively educate fellows and manage their programs, and it is not unreasonable to suppose that this is also true for other subspecialties. In view of this survey, ACGME should prospectively track compliance with the requirements and effects on fellow education and board performance. Consideration should be given to collecting objective data on the time necessary to effectively complete the tasks associated with program administration followed by reassessment of the 2019 and 2022 decreases in protected time, as they do not align with program director–reported needs for education and administration. ACGME should undertake similar efforts for all medicine subspecialties. Disclosures M.A. Watson reports employment with the US Army. B.Y. Young reports other interests or relationships as the Medical Director of Satellite Wellbound University Home Dialysis Clinic. C.M. Yuan reports employment with Walter Reed National Military Medical Center and other interests or relationships as an administrator and member of the Nephrology Education Research and Development Consortium ( https://nerdc.org ). The remaining author has nothing to disclose. Funding None. Supplementary Material Supplemental Data CJN.09050822-s0001.pdf (1.1MB, pdf) Acknowledgments We thank Drs. Rajeev Raghavan and Robert Nee for their assistance in development of the survey. The views expressed are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the official policy of the Department of Defense or the US Government. Footnotes Published online ahead of print. Publication date available at www.cjasn.org . Author Contributions C.M. Yuan conceptualized the study; C.M. Yuan was responsible for data curation; A.N. Sussman, M.A. Watson, B.Y. Young, and C.M. Yuan were responsible for investigation; C.M. Yuan was responsible for formal analysis; A.N. Sussman, M.A. Watson, B.Y. Young, and C.M. Yuan were responsible for methodology; C.M. Yuan was responsible for project administration; C.M. Yuan was responsible for resources; A.N. Sussman, M.A. Watson, and B.Y. Young were responsible for validation; C.M. Yuan wrote the original draft; and A.N. Sussman, M.A. Watson, and B.Y. Young reviewed and edited the manuscript. Data Sharing Statement All data used in this study are available in this article. Supplemental Material This article contains the following supplemental material online at http://cjasn.asnjournals.org/lookup/suppl/doi:10.2215/CJN.09050822/-/DCSupplemental . Supplemental Material . Program director survey, the number of survey respondents answering each survey question, and survey comments. References 1. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education : Internal Medicine Program Requirements and FAQs, 2022. Available at: https://www.acgme.org/specialties/internal-medicine/program-requirements-and-faqs-and-applications/ . Accessed May 25, 2022 2. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education : ACGME Program Requirements for Graduate Medical Education in Nephrology. Focused Revision Approved June 13, 2021, Effective July 1, 2021, 2021. Available at: https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/pfassets/programrequirements/148_nephrology_2021.pdf . Accessed September 22, 2022 3. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education : ACGME Program Requirements for Graduate Medical Education in Nephrology (Approved February 7, 2022, Effective July 1, 2022), 2022. Available at: https://www.acgme.org/specialties/internal-medicine/program-requirements-and-faqs-and-applications/ . Accessed March 6, 2022 4. National Resident Matching Program : Results and Data: 2021 Main Residency Match, Washington, DC, National Resident Matching Program, 2021 [ Google Scholar ] 5. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education : ACGME Program Requirements for Graduate Medical Education in Nephrology (Editorial Revision: Effective July 1, 2019, Copyright, 2019 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME). 6. Fleming GM, Brook MM, Herman BE, Kennedy C, McGann KA, Mason KE, Weiss P, Myers AL: Recommended protected time for pediatric fellowship program directors: A needs assessment survey. Acad Pediatr 16: 415–418, 2016 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Cannon S, Sorensen MD, Joyner B: Program directors’ perspective s of ACGME requirements and effectiveness for training urology residents: A national survey in the era of the next accreditation system. Urology 149: 46–51, 2021 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Griesbach S, Theobald M, Kolman K, Stutzman K, Holder S, Roett MA, Friend L, Dregansky GV, Frazier W, Lewis GR: Joint guidelines for protected nonclinical time for faculty in family medicine residency programs. Fam Med 53: 443–452, 2021 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Edgar L, McLean S, Hogan SO, Hamstra S, Holmboe ES: The Milestones Guidebook (Version 2020), 2020. Available at: https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/MilestonesGuidebook.pdf . Accessed March 5, 2022 10. Kempenich JW, Willis RE, Campi HD, Schenarts PJ: The cost of compliance: The financial burden of fulfilling Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education and American Board of Surgery requirements. J Surg Educ 75: 47–53, 2018 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. SurveyMonkey : SurveyMonkey, Momentive, 2022. Available at: https://www.surveymonkey.com/ . Accessed 3 November 2022 12. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education : Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Public List of Nephrology Specialty Programs. Available at: https://apps.acgme.org/ads/Public/Reports/Report/1 . Accessed November 3,2022 13. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education : Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education Public List of Nephrology Specialty Programs, Academic Year 2018–2019, Copyright, 2019 Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education 14. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education : Letter from Alliance for Academic Internal Medicine (AAIM) President to Chief Accreditation Officer, September 8, 2022. Available at: https://higherlogicdownload.s3.amazonaws.com/IM/fecab58a-0e31-416b-8e56-46fc9eda5c37/UploadedImages/Documents/advocacy/09-08-2022_AAIM_Letter_to_ACGME_on_Concerns_about_Core_Faculty_Reqs.pdf . Accessed September 25, 2022 15. Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education : Specialty-specific program requirements: Core faculty dedicated time. Core Faculty Dedicated Time by Specialty Updated 06/2022, 2022. Available at: https://www.acgme.org/globalassets/pdfs/specialty-specific-requirement-topics/dio-dedicated_time_core_faculty_2022.pdf . Accessed September 29, 2022 16. American Board of Internal Medicine : Initial Certification Pass Rates: Internal Medicine and Subspecialty Certification Examinations: 2017–2021. Available at: https://www.abim.org/Media/yeqiumdc/certification-pass-rates.pdf . Accessed May 22, 2022 17. Berns JS, Weng W, Jaar BG, Lipner RS, Brossman BG, McDonald FS: Analysis of performance trends from 2010–2019 on the American Board of Internal Medicine nephrology certifying exam. J Am Soc Nephrol 32: 2714–2723, 2021 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplemental Data CJN.09050822-s0001.pdf (1.1MB, pdf)',2,'2025-10-15 06:28:42','2025-10-15 06:28:42');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3395,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12239107','A practical guide for nephrologist peer reviewers: evaluating artificial intelligence and machine learning research in nephrology.','Wang Y, Cheungpasitporn W, Ali H, Qing J, Thongprayoon C, Kaewput W, Soliman KM, Huang Z, Yang M, Zhang Z.','2025','spotlights biostatistical mislead raking ignorable overestimations underestimations tidy overtly inflates unfiltered adjudicating scraping deleting equalizing analytically undersampling incrementally equalize discarding misinterpret wrongly mlconfound mlconfound queue scikit caret boruta featureextraction tpot dbscan sne dqn adaboost scikit mlflow tpot h2o automl keras tuner hyperparameters scikit lightgbm keras tensorflow pytorch automl tpot h2o scikit mlflow hyperopt optuna kfold stratifiedkfold optuna hyperopt scikit hyperopt optuna keras tuner tpot tensorflow mlflow docker kubeflow prometheus grafana seldon kubeflow lightgbm svms posttransplant nephropathology curse nephropathological nephropathological adjusts discards splits benchmarked encryption anonymization appraises shap shap shapley stipulate anonymizing bleu rouge bleu understudy rouge understudy gisting undersampling overlooking bootstrapped resamples sweeps starved parsimonious shrinks autoencoders subtracts overfit resamples penalized baselines impartiality supplementary_code_1 irnf_a_2513002_sm9798 zhongheng huadong lhdmd24h150001 2023yfc3603104 2022yfc2504500 gzy 2024ky1099 longquan zjdxlqcxzcjbgs2024016 herewith scratching cm9 gogoi valan s11255 butte s41581 vaid khosravi nugen demystifying arth informetr joi ramkumar mystical panacea arthro wolfram abuzahra referees reseval rvab005 ensor kie m441 m441 efthimiou chalkou e078276 autotrial viola conderino divers e001666 bmjph ofac669 ofid ofac669 pencina nejmp2000589 carlin jun29 b2393 b2393 kohane spisak bruns h5527 h5527 cochranelibrary kgm dhiman e078378 shearer warehous gradus rosellini schapranow bayat rasheed nephrocage e48892 pavlou ambler seaman h3868 h3868 niel bastard waikar cric recognit patrec sidey jam sidey mallick xkme grolleau gaudry personalizing akiki ocae004 ensor kie i3140 i3140 dhiman e074819 mayerich padmanabhan nephropathology pickkers hoste briefings bioinf bib bbae364 coletti duranton e202400108 pmic debray e071018 gallifant afshar ameen andaur damen jaa takada n2281 n2281 barocas fragi bihorac s41581 nagendran lovejoy m689 m689 shickel loftus sabin nejmp2201180 supplementary_code_1 irnf_a_2513002_sm9798','Abstract Artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) are transforming nephrology by enhancing diagnosis, risk prediction, and treatment optimization for conditions such as acute kidney injury (AKI) and chronic kidney disease (CKD). AI-driven models utilize diverse datasets—including electronic health records, imaging, and biomarkers—to improve clinical decision-making. Applications such as convolutional neural networks for kidney biopsy interpretation, and predictive modeling for renal replacement therapies underscore AI’s potential. Nonetheless, challenges including data quality, limited external validation, algorithmic bias, and poor interpretability constrain the clinical reliability of AI/ML models. To address these issues, this article offers a structured framework for nephrologist peer reviewers, integrating the TRIPOD-AI (Transparent Reporting of a Multivariable Prediction Model for Individual Prognosis or Diagnosis–AI Extension) checklist. Key evaluation criteria include dataset integrity, feature selection, model validation, reporting transparency, ethics, and real-world applicability. This framework promotes rigorous peer review and enhances the reproducibility, clinical relevance, and fairness of AI research in nephrology. Moreover, AI/ML studies must confront biases—data, selection, and algorithmic—that adversely affect model performance. Mitigation strategies such as data diversification, multi-center validation, and fairness-aware algorithms are essential. Overfitting in AI is driven by small patient cohorts faced with thousands of candidate features; our framework spotlights this imbalance and offers concrete remedies. Future directions in AI-driven nephrology include multimodal data fusion for improved predictive modeling, deep learning for automated imaging analysis, wearable-based monitoring, and clinical decision support systems (CDSS) that integrate comprehensive patient data. A visual summary of key manuscript sections is included. KEYWORDS: Artificial intelligence, machine learning, kidney diseases, nephrology, personalized treatment, peer review 1. Introduction Kidney diseases have become a significant global health burden, affecting millions of people worldwide. Chronic kidney disease (CKD) and end-stage renal disease (ESRD) are particularly prevalent, and their incidence and prevalence continue to rise [ 1 , 2 ]. Nevertheless, kidney diseases are frequently underdiagnosed in their early stages due to the limited sensitivity and specificity of traditional diagnostic methods, resulting in delayed treatment and adverse outcomes [ 3 ]. Consequently, the early detection and effective management of kidney disease continue to pose significant challenges in healthcare. In recent years, ML has emerged as a promising tool to address these challenges. With its advanced algorithms and robust predictive capabilities, ML has shown potential to diagnose kidney diseases more accurately, predict disease progression, and develop personalized treatment strategies for patients [ 4 ]. ML is a branch of artificial intelligence (AI) that focuses on learning patterns from data and making predictions. AI encompasses a broader range of technologies, such as expert systems, natural language processing (NLP), and computer vision, while ML is one of its core technologies. These algorithms are capable of processing extensive datasets—including electronic health records and diagnostic imaging—to reveal patterns and correlations that conventional clinical methods may overlook [ 5 ]. Furthermore, the clinical integration of ML is impeded by challenges such as data privacy concerns, limited model interpretability, and the requirement for large, diverse datasets for effective algorithm training [ 3 ]. Figure 1 is provided to visually summarize the manuscript’s key sections. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Peer-review framework for AI in nephrology. Machine‑learning methods differ from conventional biostatistical techniques primarily in the dimensionality of the data they target. Most AI frameworks are designed to learn predictive patterns from high‑dimensional feature spaces—for example, thousands of laboratory, medication, waveform or voxel‑level inputs—in which traditional regression quickly over‑fits or becomes computationally intractable [ 6 ]. Conversely, when a nephrology study involves only a small set of well‑defined clinical covariates, standard multivariable regression, Cox models, or generalized estimating equations remain the recommended first‑line tools. We therefore urge authors and peer reviewers to ask, at the outset: ‘Is the problem truly high‑dimensional?’ The inherent complexity of ML models, combined with the need for interdisciplinary collaboration, complicates the peer review process by necessitating expertise in both nephrology and ML. This dual requirement poses significant challenges for clinicians who may be less familiar with advanced ML methodologies. This editorial offers a comprehensive and practical guide for nephrologists, researchers, and peer reviewers to critically assess ML research ( Figure 2 ). Our structured guidance bridges the gap between fundamental concepts and advanced evaluation techniques, thereby fostering the advancement of high-quality nephrology research. A complete and reproducible code example can be found in Supplementary code 1. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Graphical overview of 8 proposed steps to ML evaluation. 2. The necessity of peer review in ML research 2.1. Rapid growth of ML in nephrology The application of ML in nephrology has grown exponentially, with ML models increasingly being used for the diagnosis, prognosis, and treatment optimization of kidney diseases. This rapid expansion highlights the critical importance of peer review in ensuring the quality and integrity of scientific research. Peer review acts as a safeguard, guaranteeing that only high-quality research is published, which is essential for advancing knowledge and informing clinical practice [ 7 ]. 2.2. Challenges faced by clinical reviewers The rapid proliferation of ML in nephrology has introduced new challenges in the peer review process, particularly for reviewers with a clinical background who may lack expertise in ML. This raises concerns about whether such reviewers can adequately assess ML models and their applications in nephrology. Unlike traditional clinical research, ML studies often involve complex data structures, algorithmic details, and computational techniques. Reviewers unfamiliar with key ML concepts—such as model validation, feature engineering, and overfitting—may struggle to evaluate the reliability and reproducibility of these studies. As a result, the critical assessment of the technical aspects of ML research becomes more difficult. This knowledge gap can lead to the publication of low-quality research, which may mislead clinicians and hinder scientific progress [ 8 ]. 2.3. The importance of high-quality ML research Ensuring high-quality ML research in nephrology is essential, not only to ensure that the models perform well statistically but also to guarantee their clinical efficacy. To achieve this, ML research must undergo rigorous peer review, with reviewers critically assessing the methodologies employed, including data collection, preprocessing, model selection, and validation techniques. Reviewers must confirm that the research is free from biases or methodological flaws that could undermine its applicability in clinical settings. 2.4. Responsibilities of reviewers Reviewers bear significant responsibility in identifying low-quality ML research, which could lead to the adoption of ineffective or harmful models in clinical practice [ 9 ]. They must focus on ensuring methodological rigor, verifying that the selected ML models are appropriate for the problem being studied, and confirming that the study’s results are reproducible. This responsibility is especially critical when the research has potential implications for patient care. 3. Cohort construction and evaluation: cross-sectional studies vs. longitudinal follow-up studies The evaluation of cohort construction primarily involves two key aspects: cross-sectional studies and longitudinal follow-up studies. Cross-sectional studies involve observing and analyzing a target population at a specific point in time or over a short period. The evaluation criteria for cross-sectional studies focus on the scientific rigor of sample size calculation, the precision of inclusion and exclusion criteria, the representativeness of the cohort relative to the target population, the management of selection bias, and the standardization of the data collection process. In contrast, longitudinal follow-up studies emphasize the dynamic tracking of study participants over an extended period. Key evaluation points for longitudinal studies include the rationale behind the duration of the follow-up period, effective control and management of attrition rates, appropriate handling of time-dependent variables, and the clarity and consistency of outcome event definitions and adjudication criteria. A comprehensive and thorough evaluation of these two study designs provides a solid foundation for research, ensuring the scientific validity, accuracy, and generalizability of the study outcomes. 3.1. In-depth analysis of cross-sectional cohort construction: sample characteristics, inclusion and exclusion criteria, selection bias, and data reliability investigation The proper determination of sample size is fundamental to the success of a cross-sectional study. When calculating sample size, key factors such as the research objective, anticipated effect size, significance level, and statistical power must be thoroughly considered. For example, if the aim is to investigate the prevalence of a specific disease and its associated factors within a defined population, effect size and expected prevalence should be accurately estimated based on prior similar studies or pilot data, thus enabling the selection of an appropriate sample size calculation formula [ 10 ]. A well-calibrated sample size allows the model to learn diverse features and patterns in the data, significantly improving the model’s generalizability to unseen data and reducing the risk of overfitting [ 11 ]. Clear and precise inclusion and exclusion criteria are essential for ensuring a high degree of homogeneity among study participants and improving the comparability of research results. These criteria are critical to the reliability and validity of the study. Inclusion criteria should specify the characteristics required for participants, such as age range, disease diagnostic criteria, and specific exposure conditions. Conversely, exclusion criteria should detail factors that may confound the study results, such as comorbidities that could significantly impact outcomes or poor compliance with study protocols [ 12 ]. Accurate inclusion/exclusion criteria effectively control for confounding variables, thereby enhancing the internal validity of the research. Ensuring that the cohort is highly representative of the target population is one of the core elements of a cross-sectional study. When selecting participants, scientifically sound sampling methods, such as random or stratified sampling, should be employed to minimize the risk of selection bias. Furthermore, a thorough analysis and evaluation of potential selection bias are necessary, such as comparing the basic characteristics of included and non-included participants to determine the presence of bias [ 13 ]. Effective methods to evaluate and mitigate selection bias include raking, post-stratification, and multilevel regression with post-stratification. These methods have been shown to reduce the impact of selection biases in prevalence estimates, although residual biases may remain at smaller geographic levels or when selection is non-ignorable [ 14 ]. If selection bias exists, it can lead to overestimations or underestimations of the true strength of associations. Spectrum bias (also called ‘intermediate‑phenotype’ bias) is a ubiquitous yet often under‑appreciated form of selection bias in nephrology AI studies. Rather than enrolling a consecutive clinical population, many machine‑learning investigations construct a tidy case‑control dataset that contrasts a well‑defined disease cohort (e.g., biopsy‑proven focal segmental glomerulosclerosis) with overtly healthy controls. By design, all borderline or mixed‑phenotype patients—such as those with early‑stage CKD, comorbid renal impairment, or atypical histology—are excluded a priori . Consequently, the training set encompasses only the extremes of the disease continuum, artificially inflates apparent discrimination, and produces models that perform poorly in real‑world settings where intermediate phenotypes predominate. Mitigation strategies include: (i) prospectively recruiting all patients who satisfy broad presentation criteria—for instance, enrolling every nephrology referral during the study period—instead of limiting the cohort to ‘textbook’ cases and healthy volunteers; (ii) coding disease severity on an ordinal or multi‑class scale (e.g., mild, moderate, severe) or treating renal function as a continuous outcome, thereby training algorithms across the entire clinical spectrum [ 15 ]; (iii) re‑weighting spectrum‑biased historical data with propensity‑score or inverse‑probability weighting so that the marginal distribution of phenotypes matches that of the intended target population [ 16 ]; (iv) performing external validation, before declaring clinical readiness, in an unfiltered cohort without a priori case–control selection. Reviewers should explicitly ask, ‘Were intermediate phenotypes intentionally or inadvertently excluded?’, and should require authors to justify any such exclusions, quantify their impact, and, where feasible, report performance metrics stratified by phenotype severity. Data collection must adhere to standardized procedures and tools, and personnel involved in data collection should undergo uniform training to ensure consistency and accuracy. For instance, when collecting clinical symptoms, signs, and laboratory test data, clear guidelines should be established regarding the timing of data collection, measurement methods, and operational protocols. Additionally, a robust data quality control mechanism must be in place to audit and clean the collected data promptly, preventing issues such as data loss or errors [ 17 ]. 3.2. In-depth analysis of longitudinal cohort construction: duration rationality, attrition control, time-dependent variable management, and outcome definition The duration of follow-up in longitudinal studies should be scientifically determined based on the research objectives and the natural development of the disease. If the follow-up period is too short, the study may fail to observe the expected outcome events, leading to biased results. Conversely, an overly long follow-up period increases the risk of attrition and imposes greater costs in terms of human resources, materials, and time. Attrition is an inevitable challenge in longitudinal studies, and a high attrition rate can severely compromise the validity and reliability of the study results. Additionally, many variables exhibit dynamic changes over time, and these time-dependent variables may significantly influence the study outcomes. For example, treatment regimens and lifestyle factors may evolve during the follow-up period. Therefore, appropriate statistical methods, such as time-dependent Cox regression models, must be employed to accurately assess the dynamic effects of these variables on the outcomes [ 18 ]. The definition of outcome events should align closely with the research objectives while considering the clinical context to ensure that the outcomes have clear clinical significance. The criteria for determining outcome events must be thoroughly defined, including the basis for identifying their occurrence. This determination should incorporate multidimensional information, such as clinical symptoms, signs, laboratory test results, and imaging findings. Furthermore, personnel responsible for adjudicating outcome events should receive standardized training to ensure consistency and reliability in the assessment process. 4. Evaluation of dataset quality In ML, the quality of the dataset directly influences the performance, generalizability, and effectiveness of the model. Therefore, assessing dataset quality is a crucial and integral component throughout the research and application process. The following discusses dataset quality evaluation from three key perspectives: data feature analysis, the advantages of high-quality data, and the identification of potential issues. 4.1. Data feature analysis Datasets are often characterized by diverse sources, spanning multiple domains and types of channels. Data collection methods must strictly adhere to scientific protocols, utilizing a variety of sources, such as publicly available datasets, corporate or industry-specific databases, social media data retrieved via web scraping, observational data from experiments, user-generated data, and simulated data, to ensure the accuracy and completeness of the dataset. Errors, omissions, or inconsistencies during the data collection process can compromise dataset quality. Depending on the distribution characteristics of the data, specialized statistical analysis tools and methods should be employed, with a focus on key statistical measures such as mean, variance, skewness, and kurtosis. For normally distributed data, standardization techniques should be applied to achieve a uniform mean and standard deviation, thereby enhancing the data’s applicability in model training. For skewed data distributions, transformations, such as logarithmic or Box-Cox transformations, should be applied based on the direction and degree of skew to approximate a normal distribution, ensuring stability and reliability in subsequent analysis and model training. For missing values, visualization tools should be employed to clearly present the distribution of missing data across the dataset, offering a comprehensive view of data completeness. Based on the proportion of missing values across different feature dimensions and the data types, the most appropriate handling methods should be carefully selected, such as deleting samples with excessive missing values, filling missing values with the mean or median, or using multiple imputation techniques [ 19 ]. In more complex data scenarios, ML algorithms, such as random forests or k-nearest neighbors (KNN), can be utilized to predict missing values by learning from existing data features, thereby preserving as much original information and underlying relationships as possible. 4.2. Advantages of data quality In the research process, strict standardization procedures should be followed to effectively eliminate heterogeneity within the data, significantly enhancing its usability. The data labeling process can benefit from techniques such as multiple annotators and cross-validation to ensure label accuracy and consistency. Regular audits and evaluations of labeling results should be conducted by experienced industry professionals, who, using a precise set of evaluation metrics, perform comprehensive quantitative reviews to promptly identify and correct labeling errors, thereby improving labeling quality. Rather than artificially equalizing class frequencies during data collection, datasets should mirror the true prevalence of each outcome in the target population. When that prevalence is highly skewed, researchers can correct the resulting imbalance analytically—through class weighting, cost-sensitive loss functions, synthetic minority over-sampling, focal loss, and related methods—without compromising epidemiologic representativeness. After quantifying the class distribution, investigators may also employ sampling-based strategies, such as targeted oversampling or undersampling, to incrementally equalize sample counts across categories. In feature engineering, classical methods like Principal Component Analysis (PCA), Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA), and emerging deep learning-based automatic feature extraction techniques should be used to effectively extract and transform features from the raw data [ 20 ]. In feature analysis, statistical methods such as correlation analysis and Chi-square tests should be applied to accurately identify representative and discriminative features. For example, in image recognition datasets, careful selection of image features—such as color, texture, and shape—should be performed, discarding redundant or irrelevant features that contribute minimally to classification or prediction tasks, such as noise features that do not significantly affect image categorization. This rigorous process improves model training efficiency and significantly enhances the model’s performance, leading to substantial improvements in evaluation metrics like accuracy and recall across various test sets. 4.3. Identification of potential issues Data bias refers to systematic errors within a dataset that may lead to deviations in the model’s prediction results. These biases are typically categorized as selection bias, measurement bias, and confounding bias. Confounding bias arises when a third variable is related to both the predictors and the outcome, creating a spurious association that the model may misinterpret as causal. For instance, if both hospital‑acquired infection rates (outcome) and serum creatinine levels (predictor) are influenced by length of stay (confounder), the model might wrongly infer that creatinine determines infection risk. Detecting confounding is challenging in high‑dimensional machine‑learning settings because traditional distribution‑free (non‑parametric) tests were unavailable. Newly developed tools—such as the mlconfound partial‑ and full‑confounder tests—fill this gap by controlling the type‑I error without assuming linearity or normality, helping researchers decide whether a predictive signal reflects an unmeasured confounder rather than a genuine physiological link. These tests, as implemented in the package mlconfound, can assist in assessing and improving the generalizability and biological plausibility of predictive features, thus enhancing the development of clinically useful ML biomarkers [ 21 ]. Label noise can be detected and corrected through label auditing and cross-validation procedures. Feature redundancy refers to the presence of repetitive or irrelevant features in the dataset, which can negatively impact model training efficiency and performance. Using feature selection and dimensionality reduction techniques, redundant features can be identified and eliminated, thereby improving both training efficiency and model performance. 5. Evaluation of ML methodologies ML techniques have seen increasingly widespread application in nephrology, particularly in disease diagnosis, predictive modeling, and personalized treatment strategies, demonstrating significant potential. This section reviews commonly used methodologies, including supervised learning, unsupervised learning, and reinforcement learning, with a focus on their applications in nephrology. Additionally, the strengths, limitations, and appropriate use cases of each methodology will be discussed ( Table 1 ). It is crucial to emphasize that feature selection should be conducted ‘internally’ within the cross-validation process to mitigate the risk of overfitting. Table 1. Summary of step-by-step guide to ML evaluation. Step Description Key tools/guidelines Clinical question Specific questions asked in clinical practice that are used to guide diagnosis, treatment, or prognosis. And confirm that the research question involves high or low-dimensional data. STARD [ 22 ], PICO framework [ 23 ], TRIPOD [ 24 ] Database/cohort Database construction/observational queue construction. STROBE [ 25 ] Data preprocessing The process of processing and converting raw data into a standard format through a series of steps. CRISP-DM [ 26 ] Feature engineering The process of extracting and transforming from raw data (normalisation, missing value handling, coding category features). Python(Scikit-learn, Feature-engine), R(caret; Boruta, MLR, FeatureExtraction), TPOT Model selection Selection of suitable algorithms according to the type of problem. Supervised Learning: Linear Regression, Ridge Regression, Lasso Regression, Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, Random Forest, SVM, KNN, Neural Networks, Neural Networks Unsupervised Learning : K-Means Clustering, Hierarchical Clustering, DBSCAN(Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise), PCA(Principal Component Analysis), t-SNE(t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding) Reinforcement Learning : Q-Learning, Deep Q-Network, DQN, Policy Gradient Methods Other special types of algorithms : Random Forest, GBM(Gradient Boosting Machines), XGBoost, AdaBoost, CNN, Convolutional Neural Networks, RNN, Recurrent Neural Networks, RNN, Recurrent Neural Networks Scikit-learn, MLFlow, TPOT, H2O.ai, Google AutoML, Keras Tuner, XGBoost Model training Model training using training dataset, tuning model hyperparameters. Scikit-learn, XGBoost, LightGBM, Keras, TensorFlow, PyTorch, AutoML tools (TPOT and H2O.ai) Model validation and feature selection Validating the model with different sets of data, cross-validation techniques, and performance metrics like accuracy, precision, recall, etc. Meanwhile selecting the most predictive features (variables) from the raw data. Scikit-learn, MLFlow, Hyperopt, Optuna, KFold, StratifiedKFold, Optuna, Hyperopt Model Optimization Optimization of models based on evaluation results. Scikit-learn, Hyperopt, Optuna, Keras Tuner, TPOT Model deployment Deploy the trained model to the production environment for real-time prediction. TensorFlow Serving, MLFlow, Docker, KubeFlow Model monitoring and maintenance Monitor model performance in real time and regularly update the model to adapt to data changes. Prometheus + Grafana, Seldon Core, Evidently AI, Kubeflow Pipelines Open in a new tab 5.1. Supervised learning Supervised learning refers to the process of training a model on a labeled dataset, where the goal is to learn the mapping between inputs and outputs to make predictions on unseen data [ 27 ]. In nephrology, supervised learning methods are commonly applied to tasks such as disease classification, risk prediction, and pathological image analysis. For example, in the study of AKI, clinical indicators such as patient age, serum creatinine, and blood urea nitrogen are collected as feature variables, with the occurrence of AKI as the target label. Using these data, feature selection can be performed with LightGBM, and models such as Random Forest (RF) are employed to build real-time AKI prediction models [ 28 ]. Evaluating survival models is essential for accurately predicting patient outcomes, such as kidney disease progression or transplant success, to guide clinical decision-making and improve patient care ( Supplementary Information ). In nephrology, supervised learning heavily relies on precise label definitions. Labels should account for multiple factors, including the patient’s clinical presentation, laboratory test results, and imaging assessments. For instance, labels such as ‘CKD’ or ‘AKI’ must be accurately defined through expert diagnosis and verified multiple times. Model selection must consider the scale of the data, the complexity of the features, and the nature of the problem. Common models include decision trees, random forests, and support vector machines (SVMs). In nephrology, random forests are often preferred due to their ability to handle complex features and nonlinear relationships, as well as their robustness to missing values, making them a popular choice for predictive modeling. The heterogeneity of patient populations in nephrology impacts the effectiveness of random forests in predictive modeling by necessitating the development of learning software systems to investigate population specifics, such as understanding population heterogeneity, treatment specificities, and individual impacts on selected posttransplant outcomes [ 29 ]. Additionally, performance metrics such as accuracy, recall, and F1 score on both the training and validation sets should be compared to select the most optimal model. To assess the generalization ability of the model, cross-validation methods are commonly used. In nephrology, due to the heterogeneity of patient populations, K-fold cross-validation can help mitigate the risk of overfitting and ensure the model’s stability and accuracy across different datasets. In nephrology, the limited availability of data often presents a challenge due to difficulties in sample collection, the complexity of diseases, and the constraints of clinical research. When the dataset is insufficient, models are prone to overfitting, where the model excessively learns from noise and irrelevant details in the training data, leading to poor generalization on new, unseen data [ 30 ]. Common methods to control overfitting include regularization, ensemble learning, and increasing the dataset size. Regularization techniques, such as adding L1 or L2 regularization terms to the logistic regression model, can help constrain model parameters, preventing them from becoming excessively large and overly complex. Ensemble learning combines multiple weak learners—such as the random forest algorithm, which builds multiple decision trees and aggregates their predictions—making the model more robust and reducing overfitting. Increasing the dataset size enables the model to learn a broader range of features and patterns, reducing reliance on specific subsets of the data. However, acquiring large datasets in nephrology research is often challenging. 5.2. Unsupervised learning Unsupervised learning refers to the process of discovering underlying patterns or structures within data without the use of labeled data, by analyzing similarities or differences between data points [ 31 ]. In nephrology, unsupervised learning methods are applied to tasks such as patient stratification, identification of disease subtypes, and prediction of clinical progression. For instance, in clinical research on CKD, consensus clustering algorithms are used to group patients based on multidimensional clinical features, helping to identify distinct subtypes of the disease. These subtypes show significant differences in the development of CKD, cardiovascular disease, and mortality risk, thereby aiding in the development of personalized treatment plans [ 32 ]. Different clustering methods have unique characteristics, and the choice of method depends on the distribution and nature of the data. For example, K-means clustering is commonly used to uncover latent patterns within patient populations. The algorithm iteratively optimizes cluster centers to group patients with similar characteristics into the same category. Hierarchical clustering, in contrast, builds a tree-like structure, enabling clinicians to understand the relationships and similarities between different patient groups [ 33 ]. In nephropathology image data, which is often high-dimensional and contains substantial redundant information, dimensionality reduction is essential to reduce computational complexity, improve algorithm efficiency, and avoid the curse of dimensionality. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) is a widely used dimensionality reduction technique that projects high-dimensional data into a lower-dimensional space through linear transformation, while retaining the major features of the data [ 34 ]. In nephropathological image analysis, PCA can remove noise and irrelevant information from images, extracting the principal components that best represent the image’s features, thus preserving key information while reducing dimensionality. A key challenge in unsupervised learning is the interpretability of the results. In clustering analysis of nephropathological images, the results must be interpreted alongside clinical knowledge and the expertise of pathologists. For example, by analyzing the features of images within different clusters and comparing them to known renal disease pathological types, one can assess whether the clustering results align with clinical realities, thereby ensuring the reliability of the interpretations. The strength of unsupervised learning lies in its ability to uncover hidden patterns or emerging relationships within the data. For instance, certain patient groups that were not recognized as distinct pathological categories in traditional medicine may reveal new disease subtypes or clinical manifestations through technological analysis. 5.3. Reinforcement learning Reinforcement learning (RL) is a learning approach in which an agent interacts with its environment and continuously adjusts its strategy to maximize cumulative rewards [ 35 ]. In nephrology, RL can be applied to optimize treatment decisions, develop personalized treatment plans, and make real-time adjustments during the dynamic monitoring of patient health. One example of RL’s application in nephrology is in determining the optimal timing for initiating renal replacement therapy (RRT) in patients with AKI. By regularly assessing the patient’s clinical condition, RL can help identify the best time to initiate treatment, improving survival rates and reducing unnecessary interventions [ 36 ]. The reward function, a core component of reinforcement learning, defines the learning objectives and plays a decisive role in guiding the agent’s actions. In nephrology, the reward function can be based on patient treatment responses and clinical indicators, such as serum creatinine levels and urine output. The state space should include key patient information, such as age, sex, underlying diseases, renal function parameters, and complications, which can inform the optimal treatment strategy. Strategy learning methods in RL include value-based approaches (e.g., Q-learning) and policy-based approaches (e.g., policy gradients). Value-based methods estimate the value of each state to select the best action, while policy-based methods directly optimize a policy function. Convergence analysis ensures the stability and effectiveness of RL models. These algorithms need to be validated through extensive simulations and experiments to ensure they converge to an optimal strategy in diverse clinical scenarios, thus avoiding adverse effects from over-adjustment or inappropriate treatment. 6. External validation evaluation External validation is a crucial step in assessing the real-world applicability of ML models. It aims to evaluate the model’s ability to generalize and perform reliably across different datasets and environments [ 37 ]. External validation typically involves several components, including internal validation, multi-center validation, and international validation. 6.1. Internal validation The core of internal validation lies in the proper partitioning of the validation dataset. Common approaches include random splitting or stratified sampling, both of which ensure independence between the training and validation sets to prevent data leakage. In nephrology research, the validation set should include data from patients across various disease types and stages of advancement to enhance the representativeness of the validation results. Cross-validation is a critical method for evaluating model generalization, with techniques such as K-fold cross-validation. Single-split internal validation entails dividing the dataset into two parts and treating one half as a so-called external validation cohort. This practice has several shortcomings: it discards half of the data that could inform model development, yields highly variable performance estimates, and fails to uncover temporal or site-specific biases that genuine external validation would reveal. By partitioning the data into multiple subsets, the model is trained and tested on different training and validation sets, which helps minimize random errors. The integrity of cross-validation ensures that each data point is used for validation at least once, reducing the risk of overfitting [ 38 ]. Stability analysis typically involves repeated cross-validation, altering data splits, and introducing noise to assess the model’s robustness. A model with poor stability may struggle to adapt to variations in clinical environments. 6.2. Multi-center validation Multi-center validation involves testing the model’s applicability using data from different hospitals or research centers, incorporating diverse patient populations and extensive clinical data. This process ensures that the model performs well across a broad spectrum of clinical settings. For instance, it should include large general hospitals, specialized institutions, and primary healthcare settings, allowing for the assessment of the model’s performance across various tiers of medical environments. Multi-center validation also requires accounting for variations in population characteristics, such as physiological traits, disease susceptibility, and lifestyle factors, as these differences may affect the model’s predictive accuracy. In nephrology, patient populations from different regions may display significant variations in disease manifestations and treatment responses. As a result, the model’s performance during multi-center validation should be assessed in specific subgroups to evaluate its generalizability. Geographic and demographic differences, such as diet, lifestyle, and genetic background, can substantially impact the incidence and course of kidney diseases. Therefore, when performing multi-center validation, it is essential to consider the influence of these regional and demographic disparities, ensuring that the model is applicable across different environments. 6.3. International validation The challenge of international validation lies in the comparability of cross-national data. Variations in patient populations, disease standards, data collection methods, and other factors across countries may affect the consistency of the data. To ensure the effectiveness of international validation, it is essential to standardize data collection and processing procedures across regions and apply appropriate statistical methods to adjust for inter-country differences. Furthermore, racial disparities are particularly pronounced in nephrology, as different racial groups may exhibit significant variations in genetic susceptibility, disease pathogenesis, and clinical manifestations of kidney diseases. Therefore, international validation must account for the impact of racial differences by assessing the model’s predictive accuracy and biases across various ethnic groups, ensuring its cross-racial applicability. Another critical consideration in international validation is the variation in healthcare systems. Differences in medical resources, technological capabilities, and diagnostic standards across countries and regions can influence the model’s practical effectiveness. Thus, the validation process should adjust for these systemic differences to ensure that the model performs optimally across diverse healthcare settings. 7. Clinical translational value assessment and mechanisms exploration ML offers significant potential for clinical applications in nephrology. However, its true value extends beyond technology, requiring a comprehensive evaluation of its feasibility and practical effectiveness in translating to various clinical settings. This assessment is conducted from two main perspectives: comparison with existing methods and practical application value. The accuracy of ML models typically needs to be benchmarked against traditional diagnostic methods. In nephrology, established diagnostic techniques include imaging studies, laboratory tests, and clinical scoring systems, among others. ML has the potential to enhance diagnostic accuracy by integrating diverse data sources, such as imaging data, genomic information, and clinical indicators, especially in areas like early disease detection and disease trajectory forecasting. Compared to conventional methods, ML often leads to significant improvements in efficiency and a reduction in physicians’ workload. For example, in renal pathology screening, particularly the analysis of kidney tissue slide images, ML enables rapid processing of large volumes of imaging data through automated image recognition, thereby reducing both costs and time associated with manual screening. Moreover, it allows real-time analysis of patient monitoring data, providing immediate feedback to support clinical decision-making [ 39 ]. The key to assessing the clinical utility of ML lies in determining whether it can be effectively integrated into physicians’ daily workflows. However, in clinical practice, data often suffer from missing values and noise, which can undermine the accuracy of models. Additionally, many models lack interpretability, which hinders their broader clinical adoption. Many ML models have been validated exclusively on specific datasets, with limited cross-center validation, which undermines their reliability in real-world clinical settings. Furthermore, model training often relies on processed data that may fail to capture the complexities of real-world clinical data, creating challenges in transitioning these models into clinical tools. For example, despite the promise of ML in predicting AKI, it still depends on indirect markers, such as serum creatinine and urine output, which delay early diagnosis when compared to traditional diagnostic methods [ 40 ]. In clinical translation, ethical and privacy concerns are critical factors that must not be overlooked. ML models often rely on large volumes of patient data for training, which may include sensitive personal information. During the technological implementation process, it is essential to comply with privacy protection laws and regulations strictly, and to use techniques such as encryption and anonymization to safeguard patient information. Moreover, the role of ML in clinical decision-making must remain transparent, with efforts to ensure that both physicians and patients can understand and trust the model’s judgments. This transparency is crucial for fostering confidence in the use of these technologies within clinical settings. ML is not only applicable to clinical data analysis but is also widely utilized in multi-omics research. Multi-omics studies encompass various data types, including genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, and metabolomics. It enables the effective analysis of complex biological mechanisms. For instance, integrating multi-omics data with ML strategies by combining genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics, and metabolomics has the potential to elucidate the molecular mechanisms underlying CKD pathogenesis [ 41 ]. By leveraging ML to integrate diverse global omics data, we can deepen our understanding of the biological mechanisms contributing to the high heterogeneity of CKD and enhance the prospects of precision medicine in CKD management [ 42 ]. 8. Critical appraisal by peer reviewers In nephrology research, rigorous peer review is essential for ensuring high-quality studies that translate into practical clinical applications. To ensure both reliability and transparency, peer reviewers should employ specialized assessment tools and checklists. One valuable tool is the TRIPOD-AI framework, which critically appraises the implementation of ML models in medical research. Its primary goal is to evaluate the quality, transparency, and credibility of AI/ML models ( Table 2 ) [ 43 , 44 ]. Table 2. The core elements of the TRIPOD-AI assessment checklist. Element Assessment question Example in nephrology research 1. Definition of the problem and context of the model Is the clinical context and research question of the model clearly stated? Predicting risk of AKI in ICU patients using AI-based early warning systems. 2. Data sets and data sources Are the data sources used to train the model clearly described? Utilizing EHR data from multicenter cohorts to train an AI model for CKD progression prediction. 3. Feature selection and data preprocessing Does it explicitly list the features used to train the model and describe the feature selection process? Selecting key variables such as serum creatinine, urine output, demographics, and comorbidities for an AKI risk prediction model. 4. Model selection and training process Is the type of model chosen clearly described (e.g., neural network, support vector machine, random forest, etc.)? Comparing logistic regression, random forest, and deep learning models for predicting hyperkalemia in CKD patients. 5. Model assessment Has model performance such as accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, ROC curves, AUC, etc., been adequately assessed and reported? Reporting AUC, precision-recall curves, and calibration metrics for an AI model predicting dialysis initiation in CKD patients. 6. External validation of models Was external validation performed, and was the sample of validation representative? Validating an AKI prediction model using an independent dataset from a different hospital network. 7. Interpretability and transparency of models Is interpretability of the model provided to help clinicians understand the modeled decision-making process? Using SHAP values to highlight important predictors in a CKD progression model. 8. Ethical and clinical implications Are practical applications of the model in clinical settings, ethical issues, and potential biases discussed? Assessing the impact of AI-driven fluid management tools in dialysis patients while addressing bias related to underrepresented populations. Open in a new tab Abbreviations: EHR (electronic health record); AUC (Area Under the Curve); ICU (Intensive Care Unit); ROC (Receiver Operating Characteristic); SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations). For example, in studies predicting the risk of AKI in patients, the TRIPOD-AI assessment criteria stipulate that the datasets and feature selection process must be clearly defined. This includes key variables such as biochemical indicators (e.g., creatinine, urea), blood pressure, medical history, and medication usage. A detailed description is essential to clarify whether these features have been standardized, as well as to explain the methods and rationale behind the feature selection process. Furthermore, model evaluation should report performance metrics across multiple datasets, including accuracy and AUC, and ensure model stability and generalizability through cross-validation to assess its adaptability and reliability across different datasets. The TRIPOD-LLM (Transparent Reporting of a multivariable model for Individual Prognosis Or Diagnosis–Large Language Models) checklist extends TRIPOD-AI by addressing the unique considerations of large language models (LLMs) in medical research. This framework ensures standardized reporting, transparency, and reproducibility in studies leveraging LLMs for nephrology applications ( Table 3 ) [ 45 ]. Table 3. The core elements of the TRIPOD-LLM assessment checklist. Element Assessment question Example in nephrology research 1. Task Definition and Context Is the purpose of the LLM clearly defined within a clinical or research setting? Using an LLM to extract structured information from unstructured nephrology consultation notes. 2. Data Acquisition and Preprocessing Are the data sources, preprocessing steps, and inclusion criteria explicitly described? Cleaning and anonymizing patient notes from EHRs before fine-tuning an LLM for predicting CKD progression. 3. Model Architecture and Training Is the architecture and training procedure of the LLM described? Fine-tuning GPT-4 for automated triage of nephrology referrals. 4. Performance Evaluation Are relevant performance metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1-score, BLEU, ROUGE) reported? Evaluating the ability of an LLM to generate nephrology consult summaries with high specificity and sensitivity. 5. External Validation and Robustness Has the model been validated on independent datasets? Testing an LLM for automated report generation in a different hospital system. 6. Interpretability and Explainability Does the study describe mechanisms for interpreting model predictions? Using attention heatmaps to illustrate how an LLM prioritizes nephrology-related findings in radiology reports. 7. Ethical Considerations and Bias Mitigation Are potential biases and ethical considerations addressed? Analyzing disparities in LLM-generated recommendations for dialysis initiation across different demographic groups. 8. Human Oversight and Autonomy Does the study define the level of human oversight required? Describing whether an LLM-generated CKD risk report requires physician review before being used in clinical decisions. 9. Prompting Strategies and Model Adaptation Are prompting techniques, instruction tuning, or reinforcement learning strategies clearly described? Comparing zero-shot, few-shot, and fine-tuning approaches in LLM-assisted nephrology diagnosis. 10. Temporal and Geographic Generalizability Has the study considered shifts in data over time or across different regions? Testing an LLM trained on U.S. nephrology data on patient records from Thailand. 11. Regulatory and Deployment Considerations Does the study discuss regulatory compliance, deployment in clinical settings, and alignment with guidelines? Evaluating an AI nephrology assistant’s adherence to FDA/EMA AI risk classification frameworks. 12. Reproducibility and Open Science Are datasets, code, and model parameters made available for replication? Publishing training data and model weights of an LLM-based nephrology chatbot to encourage further research. Open in a new tab Abbreviations: BLEU (Bilingual Evaluation Understudy); F1-score (Harmonic Mean of Precision and Recall); GPT-4 (Generative Pre-trained Transformer 4); ROUGE (Recall-Oriented Understudy for Gisting Evaluation). By incorporating TRIPOD-LLM, researchers can ensure the quality, reliability, and ethical integrity of LLM applications in nephrology. This checklist enhances transparency and reproducibility, helping nephrologists critically evaluate AI-driven research and its clinical utility. 9. Biases and limitations in ML research In ML research, bias and limitations are critical issues that cannot be overlooked, as they can significantly affect the performance, reliability, and generalization ability of models. Data bias refers to the inherent systematic biases present in the training data, which can influence the learning process and result in inaccurate performance for certain groups or contexts [ 46 ]. For example, if a specific group (e.g., patients of a particular race or gender) is underrepresented in the training data, the model may fail to accurately recognize the features of these groups, leading to flawed predictions or diagnoses [ 47 ]. Strategies such as diversifying data sources, data augmentation, oversampling, and undersampling can help mitigate data bias. Label bias occurs when there are errors or inconsistencies in the labels (e.g., disease diagnosis outcomes) within the training data. These label errors can directly impact the learning effectiveness and performance of ML models. Label bias can be reduced through expert annotation, label validation, consistency checks, and semi-supervised learning techniques. Selection bias arises during the data collection process when certain groups or patient populations are inadequately represented. This lack of representation in the training data leads to model bias. Randomized data collection, multi-center and multi-regional data gathering, as well as stratified analysis and grouping, can help reduce selection bias. Algorithmic bias refers to biases introduced by the design of the ML model or its default parameter settings. For instance, certain complex deep learning models might be more sensitive to common patterns, while overlooking rare features that could be clinically significant. Dominant drivers of overfitting is small N, large P. In statistical and machine‑learning discourse, the dimensions of a data matrix are conventionally denoted by N and P. N is number of independent observations (rows of the data matrix); P is number of predictor variables or features (columns of the data matrix). The single most pervasive root-cause of poor reproducibility in nephrology ML studies is an unfavorable N≪P ratio—that is, few patients but thousands of candidate predictors [ 48 ]. In such settings: Variance inflation occurs because model parameters are fitted to noise; median optimism in AUC can exceed 0.10 when N < 200 and p > 1 000. Unstable feature selection means that seemingly ‘important’ variables shift drastically with bootstrapped resamples, undermining biological plausibility. Hyper‑parameter search explosion (‘hyper‑overfitting’) arises when grid or Bayesian tuning sweeps hundreds of configurations across an already data‑starved training set. Table 4 summarizes the four most common and fatal technical pitfalls for small N≪P scenarios and their corresponding mitigation measures. Table 4. Mitigation checklist for small N/large P overfitting. Mitigation step Rationale/mechanism Practical implementation Pre-specify a parsimonious feature set by domain knowledge or dimensionality reduction Shrinks the parameter space, lowers variance, and screens out noisy predictors that dominate when P≫N Retain only clinically relevant variables. Apply PCA/autoencoders and keep ≤50 principal components. Use nested cross-validation for all hyper-parameter tuning Separates model selection from performance estimation, blocking information leakage and optimism. Outer k-folds for testing; inner folds for grid/Bayesian search. Report optimism-adjusted metrics (e.g., bootstrap-corrected AUC/C-index) Quantifies and subtracts the in-sample performance inflation that characterizes overfit models. ≥200 bootstrap resamples; present apparent vs. optimism-corrected values. Disclose an unfavorable N:P ratio and benchmark against simpler models Encourages proportional model complexity and transparency; demonstrates whether high-dimensional ML truly adds value. If N < 10*candidate features, state this explicitly and compare with penalized or classic regression baselines. Open in a new tab 10. Future trends in ML development By modeling various data sources, such as patient history, laboratory results, and imaging data, ML can identify high-risk populations in advance, supporting early disease screening and intervention. For instance, by analyzing trends in renal function indicators (e.g., creatinine levels, urine protein), ML can predict the trajectory of AKI or CKD [ 49 ]. In the future, ML will increasingly rely on the fusion of multimodal data. By integrating data from diverse sources, a more comprehensive evaluation of a patient’s health status can be achieved, leading to more accurate disease risk assessments. Deep learning techniques, particularly convolutional neural networks (CNNs), have already made significant progress in imaging analysis [ 50 ]. In the future, deep learning will further drive the automation of renal imaging analysis, especially in applications related to the automatic detection of kidney injuries, tumors, and tubular lesions. By combining deep learning with imaging data, systems will be able to automatically detect kidney abnormalities and provide real-time, precise analysis [ 51 ]. Real-time monitoring and intelligent alert systems are emerging as key trends in the application of ML in nephrology. With advancements in wearable devices and sensor technologies, more kidney disease patients will be able to conduct daily monitoring at home. ML models can analyze these patients’ physiological data (e.g., blood pressure, blood glucose, urine volume) in real time and provide immediate feedback. Additionally, real-time monitoring of dialysis data (e.g., blood flow rates, electrolyte levels) by ML models can optimize the dialysis treatment process [ 52 ]. Clinical decision support systems (CDSS) will integrate all relevant patient data, including clinical information, laboratory tests, imaging data, and treatment history, and provide diagnostic and therapeutic recommendations through ML models. In the future, the application of ML in nephrology will increasingly focus on ethics and privacy protection. Developing transparent and interpretable systems that safeguard patient privacy and enhance patient trust will be critical for future advancements. Furthermore, ensuring fairness and impartiality in the decision-making of ML models, and preventing biases related to race, gender, or age, will be essential issues that need to be addressed [ 53 ]. 11. Conclusion The application of ML in nephrology research holds significant potential by leveraging vast amounts of clinical data, genomic information, imaging data, and more. These advancements can help researchers identify potential risk factors for diseases, predict disease trajectory, and develop personalized treatment strategies. However, strict adherence to scientific principles is essential at every stage, including data collection, model selection, validation methods, and ensuring the interpretability and transparency of models, to guarantee the reliability and clinical applicability of research findings. Researchers, journals, and peer reviewers must maintain rigorous control over data quality, employ appropriate algorithms, conduct thorough validation, and address ethical and privacy concerns to prevent issues such as bias and overfitting. We recommend that peer‑review panels broaden their composition beyond bioinformatics experts to include specialists in data analysis, data mining, big data engineering, and domain biology. This multidisciplinary review process ensures rigorous evaluation of manuscripts for computational validity, data management, and biological relevance. Only through these measures can high-quality, reproducible research be ensured. Supplementary Material Supplementary_code_1.docx IRNF_A_2513002_SM9798.docx (47.1KB, docx) Funding Statement Zhongheng Zhang received funding from the Huadong Medicine Joint Funds of the Zhejiang Provincial Natural Science Foundation of China (LHDMD24H150001), the National Natural Science Foundation of China (82272180, 82472243), the China National Key Research and Development Program (2023YFC3603104), the China National Key Research and Development Program (2022YFC2504500), a collaborative scientific project co-established by the Science and Technology Department of the National Administration of Traditional Chinese Medicine and the Zhejiang Provincial Administration of Traditional Chinese Medicine (GZY-ZJ-KJ-24082), the General Health Science and Technology Program of Zhejiang Province (2024KY1099), and Project of Zhejiang University Longquan Innovation Center (ZJDXLQCXZCJBGS2024016). Disclosure statement The views expressed in this manuscript reflect the author’s own insights and findings from their research and do not represent the